window.DB = {"version":"anking-step1-19topics-320q-2026-07-05","fuentes":[{"id":"anking","nombre":"AnKing Overhaul — Step 1 (v11)","idioma":"en","descripcion":"USMLE Step 1 questions derived from the AnKing Overhaul Anki deck (cloze cards converted to single-best-answer MCQs).","licencia":"Personal study use. Deck by the AnKing team, based on First Aid / Pathoma / Boards & Beyond / Sketchy."}],"temas":[{"id":1,"nombre":"Medical Ethics","sistema":"Public Health & Ethics","fuente":"anking"},{"id":2,"nombre":"Histamine & Antihistamines","sistema":"Respiratory Pharmacology","fuente":"anking"},{"id":3,"nombre":"Reproductive Pharmacology","sistema":"Reproductive System","fuente":"anking"},{"id":4,"nombre":"Antimicrobials","sistema":"Microbiology","fuente":"anking"},{"id":5,"nombre":"Endocrine Pathology","sistema":"Endocrine","fuente":"anking"},{"id":6,"nombre":"Hematology & Oncology Pathology","sistema":"Hematology & Oncology","fuente":"anking"},{"id":7,"nombre":"Autonomic Drugs","sistema":"Pharmacology","fuente":"anking"},{"id":8,"nombre":"Reproductive System Pathology","sistema":"Reproductive System","fuente":"anking"},{"id":9,"nombre":"Neurology & Special Senses Pathology","sistema":"Neurology & Special Senses","fuente":"anking"},{"id":10,"nombre":"Gastrointestinal Pathology","sistema":"Gastrointestinal","fuente":"anking"},{"id":11,"nombre":"Immune Responses","sistema":"Immunology","fuente":"anking"},{"id":12,"nombre":"Renal Physiology","sistema":"Renal","fuente":"anking"},{"id":13,"nombre":"Metabolism","sistema":"Biochemistry","fuente":"anking"},{"id":14,"nombre":"Neoplasia","sistema":"Pathology","fuente":"anking"},{"id":15,"nombre":"Cardiovascular Pathology","sistema":"Cardiovascular","fuente":"anking"},{"id":16,"nombre":"Musculoskeletal Pathology","sistema":"Musculoskeletal, Skin & Connective Tissue","fuente":"anking"},{"id":17,"nombre":"Respiratory Pathology","sistema":"Respiratory","fuente":"anking"},{"id":18,"nombre":"Psychiatry Pathology","sistema":"Psychiatry","fuente":"anking"},{"id":19,"nombre":"Epidemiology & Biostatistics","sistema":"Public Health & Ethics","fuente":"anking"}],"preguntas":[{"id":"anking-ethics-001","fuente":"anking","tema":1,"anki":"1521934672882","q":"A legal determination that the care provided fell below the accepted standard of practice and resulted in injury or death to the patient is termed:","o":{"A":"Malpractice","B":"Battery","C":"Informed refusal","D":"Abandonment"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Malpractice is professional negligence. It requires the '4 D's': a Duty of care, Dereliction (breach of the standard of care), Damage to the patient, and the breach being the Direct cause of that damage.","incorrectas":{"B":"Battery is intentional, non-consented physical contact with a patient — not a standard-of-care failure.","C":"Informed refusal is a competent patient declining recommended care after being told the risks; it is a patient right, not negligence.","D":"Abandonment is the unilateral termination of the physician–patient relationship without adequate notice; it is one specific act, not the general term for harmful substandard care."}}},{"id":"anking-ethics-002","fuente":"anking","tema":1,"anki":"1522552355675","q":"Which core ethical principle centers on treating patients fairly and equitably?","o":{"A":"Justice","B":"Autonomy","C":"Beneficence","D":"Nonmaleficence"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Justice concerns the fair and equitable distribution of benefits, risks and resources among patients.","incorrectas":{"B":"Autonomy is respecting the patient's right to self-determination.","C":"Beneficence is acting in the patient's best interest.","D":"Nonmaleficence is the duty to avoid harm ('do no harm')."}}},{"id":"anking-ethics-003","fuente":"anking","tema":1,"anki":"1522553684804","q":"In triage, patients are prioritized unequally according to acuity. Which core ethical principle remains preserved despite this unequal treatment?","o":{"A":"Justice","B":"Autonomy","C":"Fidelity","D":"Nonmaleficence"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Justice demands fairness, not identical treatment. Allocating scarce resources by medical need is equitable, so triage keeps justice intact.","incorrectas":{"B":"Autonomy concerns honoring individual patient choices, not resource allocation.","C":"Fidelity refers to loyalty and keeping commitments to the patient.","D":"Nonmaleficence is avoiding harm, which is a separate consideration from fair allocation."}}},{"id":"anking-ethics-004","fuente":"anking","tema":1,"anki":"1522553752122","q":"The dictum 'first, do no harm' expresses which ethical principle?","o":{"A":"Nonmaleficence","B":"Beneficence","C":"Justice","D":"Autonomy"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Nonmaleficence is the obligation to avoid causing harm to the patient.","incorrectas":{"B":"Beneficence is the positive duty to act for the patient's benefit — related but distinct from avoiding harm.","C":"Justice concerns fairness and equitable treatment.","D":"Autonomy concerns respecting patient self-determination."}}},{"id":"anking-ethics-005","fuente":"anking","tema":1,"anki":"1522553952175","q":"A physician's special fiduciary duty to act in the patient's best interest reflects which principle?","o":{"A":"Beneficence","B":"Nonmaleficence","C":"Autonomy","D":"Justice"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Beneficence is the positive obligation to act for the good of the patient.","incorrectas":{"B":"Nonmaleficence is the duty to avoid harm, not the duty to actively benefit.","C":"Autonomy is respecting the patient's own choices.","D":"Justice is fair, equitable treatment across patients."}}},{"id":"anking-ethics-006","fuente":"anking","tema":1,"anki":"1522554099595","q":"Respecting a competent patient's right to accept or refuse recommended treatment reflects which principle?","o":{"A":"Autonomy","B":"Beneficence","C":"Justice","D":"Nonmaleficence"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Autonomy is the obligation to respect patients as individuals and to honor their preferences regarding their own care.","incorrectas":{"B":"Beneficence would emphasize doing what benefits the patient, even over their stated wish.","C":"Justice concerns fairness across patients.","D":"Nonmaleficence concerns avoiding harm."}}},{"id":"anking-ethics-007","fuente":"anking","tema":1,"anki":"1525459102217","q":"When a patient lacks decision-making capacity, does a validly designated health-care proxy's decision override those of other family members?","o":{"A":"Yes — the proxy speaks with the patient's authority","B":"No — the next of kin always takes precedence","C":"No — the attending physician has final say","D":"Only if every family member agrees"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"A validly appointed health-care proxy (durable power of attorney for health care) legally stands in for the patient and overrules other family members, regardless of relationship.","incorrectas":{"B":"Next of kin are consulted only when no proxy has been designated.","C":"The physician provides information and options but is not the surrogate decision-maker.","D":"The proxy's authority does not require unanimous family consent."}}},{"id":"anking-ethics-008","fuente":"anking","tema":1,"anki":"1526341083111","q":"Which physician-placed advance directive specifies that a patient should NOT receive CPR, including both basic and advanced cardiac life support?","o":{"A":"Do-not-resuscitate (DNR) order","B":"Living will","C":"Durable power of attorney for health care","D":"Health-care proxy designation"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"A DNR is a physician order directing that CPR (BLS and ACLS) be withheld if the patient arrests.","incorrectas":{"B":"A living will is the patient's own written statement of future care preferences, not a physician order.","C":"A durable power of attorney appoints a surrogate decision-maker; it does not itself specify no-CPR.","D":"A health-care proxy names who decides, not the specific resuscitation instruction."}}},{"id":"anking-ethics-009","fuente":"anking","tema":1,"anki":"1524801828125","q":"You suspect child abuse in a pediatric patient. What is the most appropriate next step?","o":{"A":"Ask permission to interview the child alone","B":"Openly confront the parents with the accusation","C":"Discharge the child and arrange outpatient follow-up","D":"Wait for a police investigation before examining the child"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Interviewing the child alone allows a candid history and examination away from a possible abuser. (Suspected abuse must still be reported to Child Protective Services — reporting and interviewing are not mutually exclusive.)","incorrectas":{"B":"Confronting the parents can escalate danger to the child and compromise the evaluation.","C":"Discharging a child with suspected abuse fails the duty to protect.","D":"Mandatory evaluation and reporting should not be delayed while awaiting law enforcement."}}},{"id":"anking-ethics-010","fuente":"anking","tema":1,"anki":"1525915872970","q":"Which factor most strongly precipitates a patient's decision to file a malpractice lawsuit?","o":{"A":"The perception that the physician was uncaring","B":"The absolute severity of the medical outcome","C":"The physician's number of years in practice","D":"The size of the hospital"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"The strongest predictor of litigation is the patient's perception of a poor, uncaring physician relationship — communication matters more than the outcome itself.","incorrectas":{"B":"Bad outcomes alone poorly predict lawsuits; many severe outcomes never lead to suits when rapport is good.","C":"Experience level is not the primary driver.","D":"Institution size is unrelated to the individual decision to sue."}}},{"id":"anking-ethics-011","fuente":"anking","tema":1,"anki":"1525914380350","q":"What is the best setting in which to begin a conversation about advance care planning?","o":{"A":"The outpatient (ambulatory) setting","B":"During an active cardiac arrest","C":"In the ICU immediately after intubation","D":"Only once the patient is imminently terminal"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"The stable, unhurried outpatient setting is ideal for starting advance care planning; it should then be re-addressed at hospital admission.","incorrectas":{"B":"An arrest is far too late to begin a values-based discussion.","C":"Post-intubation, the patient often cannot participate.","D":"Waiting until imminent death eliminates the patient's chance to plan meaningfully."}}},{"id":"anking-resp-001","fuente":"anking","tema":2,"anki":"1474571576716","q":"Mast cells degranulate on antigen exposure once they have been sensitized by which antibody class bound to their surface?","o":{"A":"IgE","B":"IgG","C":"IgM","D":"IgA"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Allergen-specific IgE binds Fcε receptors on mast cells; cross-linking by antigen triggers degranulation (type I hypersensitivity).","incorrectas":{"B":"IgG mediates type II/III reactions and opsonization, not mast-cell sensitization.","C":"IgM is the first antibody in a primary response and activates complement, not mast cells.","D":"IgA protects mucosal surfaces and does not sensitize mast cells."}}},{"id":"anking-resp-002","fuente":"anking","tema":2,"anki":"1474571583341","q":"Allergic symptoms such as nasal itching, urticaria and bronchoconstriction are mediated by histamine acting primarily on which receptor?","o":{"A":"H1","B":"H2","C":"H3","D":"H4"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"H1 receptors mediate the classic allergic/inflammatory effects of histamine.","incorrectas":{"B":"H2 receptors mainly drive gastric acid secretion.","C":"H3 receptors are presynaptic autoreceptors in the CNS.","D":"H4 receptors modulate chemotaxis of immune cells and are not the main allergic mediator."}}},{"id":"anking-resp-003","fuente":"anking","tema":2,"anki":"1474571586703","q":"The H1 histamine receptor is coupled to which G protein?","o":{"A":"Gq","B":"Gs","C":"Gi","D":"G12/13"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"H1 → Gq → phospholipase C → IP3/DAG → increased intracellular Ca2+.","incorrectas":{"B":"Gs raises cAMP and is used by the H2 receptor.","C":"Gi lowers cAMP (e.g., H3/H4 receptors).","D":"G12/13 signals through Rho, not the histamine H1 pathway."}}},{"id":"anking-resp-004","fuente":"anking","tema":2,"anki":"1474571591865","q":"The H2 histamine receptor is coupled to which G protein?","o":{"A":"Gs","B":"Gq","C":"Gi","D":"G12/13"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"H2 → Gs → adenylyl cyclase → increased cAMP, which drives gastric acid secretion by parietal cells.","incorrectas":{"B":"Gq is the H1 pathway (PLC/Ca2+).","C":"Gi decreases cAMP.","D":"G12/13 uses the Rho pathway, unrelated to H2."}}},{"id":"anking-resp-005","fuente":"anking","tema":2,"anki":"1474571599295","q":"Which histamine receptor on gastric parietal cells mediates gastric acid secretion?","o":{"A":"H2","B":"H1","C":"H3","D":"H4"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"H2 receptors on parietal cells stimulate acid secretion — the target of H2 blockers such as ranitidine and famotidine.","incorrectas":{"B":"H1 mediates allergic and smooth-muscle effects, not acid secretion.","C":"H3 receptors are CNS presynaptic autoreceptors.","D":"H4 receptors are found mainly on hematopoietic cells."}}},{"id":"anking-resp-006","fuente":"anking","tema":2,"anki":"1474571653671","q":"Histamine acting on H1 receptors of bronchial smooth muscle — especially in asthmatic patients — causes:","o":{"A":"Bronchoconstriction","B":"Bronchodilation","C":"No change in airway tone","D":"Ciliary paralysis"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"H1 activation contracts bronchial smooth muscle, narrowing airways — clinically important in asthma.","incorrectas":{"B":"Bronchodilation is produced by beta-2 agonists, not histamine.","C":"Histamine clearly alters airway tone (constriction).","D":"Histamine does not paralyze cilia."}}},{"id":"anking-resp-007","fuente":"anking","tema":2,"anki":"1474571630796","q":"Histamine-induced urticaria (hives) results primarily from which H1-mediated vascular effect?","o":{"A":"Increased vascular permeability","B":"Vasoconstriction of dermal arterioles","C":"Decreased capillary hydrostatic pressure","D":"Platelet aggregation"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"H1 activation increases vascular permeability, causing fluid extravasation into the dermis — the wheal of urticaria.","incorrectas":{"B":"Histamine causes vasodilation, not vasoconstriction.","C":"Permeability and vasodilation raise, not lower, local fluid movement.","D":"Platelet aggregation is unrelated to the hives mechanism."}}},{"id":"anking-resp-008","fuente":"anking","tema":2,"anki":"1474571658398","q":"In the central nervous system, histamine regulates arousal and wakefulness mainly through which receptor — explaining the sedation caused by first-generation antihistamines?","o":{"A":"H1","B":"H2","C":"H3","D":"H4"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Central H1 receptors promote wakefulness; first-generation H1 blockers cross the blood–brain barrier and block them, causing sedation.","incorrectas":{"B":"H2 is primarily a peripheral (gastric) receptor.","C":"H3 is a presynaptic autoreceptor that regulates histamine release but is not the sedation target of antihistamines.","D":"H4 is mainly involved in immune-cell chemotaxis."}}},{"id":"anking-resp-009","fuente":"anking","tema":2,"anki":"1474571667626","q":"To which drug class do diphenhydramine and dimenhydrinate belong?","o":{"A":"First-generation H1 antagonists","B":"Second-generation H1 antagonists","C":"H2 antagonists","D":"Mast-cell stabilizers"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Diphenhydramine and dimenhydrinate are first-generation H1 blockers: lipophilic, cross the BBB (sedation) and have antimuscarinic effects.","incorrectas":{"B":"Second-generation agents (loratadine, fexofenadine, cetirizine) are non-sedating.","C":"H2 antagonists (famotidine) reduce gastric acid, not allergy symptoms.","D":"Mast-cell stabilizers (cromolyn) prevent degranulation rather than block H1 receptors."}}},{"id":"anking-resp-010","fuente":"anking","tema":2,"anki":"1474571672988","q":"Which of the following is a first-generation (sedating) H1 antihistamine?","o":{"A":"Chlorpheniramine","B":"Loratadine","C":"Fexofenadine","D":"Cetirizine"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Chlorpheniramine is a first-generation H1 antagonist and crosses the blood–brain barrier.","incorrectas":{"B":"Loratadine is second-generation and minimally sedating.","C":"Fexofenadine is second-generation and essentially non-sedating.","D":"Cetirizine is second-generation (though mildly more sedating than the others in its class)."}}},{"id":"anking-resp-011","fuente":"anking","tema":2,"anki":"1474571606722","q":"Histamine acting on H1 receptors changes nasal and bronchial mucus production in which direction?","o":{"A":"Increases secretion","B":"Decreases secretion","C":"No effect on secretion","D":"Thickens and halts secretion"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"H1 activation increases nasal and bronchial mucus production, contributing to rhinorrhea and airway symptoms.","incorrectas":{"B":"Antimuscarinic drugs, not histamine, dry secretions.","C":"Histamine clearly increases secretion.","D":"Histamine does not halt secretion."}}},{"id":"anking-repro-001","fuente":"anking","tema":3,"anki":"1462996334267","q":"Combined oral contraceptives prevent ovulation mainly by inhibiting the release of which pituitary hormones?","o":{"A":"FSH and LH","B":"GnRH and prolactin","C":"hCG and progesterone","D":"Oxytocin and estrogen"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Exogenous estrogen/progestin exert negative feedback on the anterior pituitary, suppressing FSH and LH and blocking the LH surge needed for ovulation.","incorrectas":{"B":"GnRH is hypothalamic; prolactin is not the ovulation trigger.","C":"hCG is a placental hormone of pregnancy; progesterone is a component of the pill, not a suppressed target.","D":"Oxytocin governs labor/letdown and is unrelated to ovulation suppression."}}},{"id":"anking-repro-002","fuente":"anking","tema":3,"anki":"1463080993949","q":"Penile erection is mediated by which substance released in response to parasympathetic (pelvic nerve) stimulation?","o":{"A":"Nitric oxide (increasing cGMP)","B":"Norepinephrine (increasing cAMP)","C":"Acetylcholine acting on nicotinic receptors","D":"Endothelin-1"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Parasympathetic input releases nitric oxide, which raises cGMP, relaxing corpus cavernosum smooth muscle → erection ('Point and Shoot': Parasympathetic = erection).","incorrectas":{"B":"Norepinephrine (sympathetic) promotes detumescence, not erection.","C":"Erection is mediated by NO, not a direct nicotinic effect.","D":"Endothelin-1 is a vasoconstrictor and does not drive erection."}}},{"id":"anking-repro-003","fuente":"anking","tema":3,"anki":"1474666117993","q":"Besides erectile dysfunction, sildenafil is also indicated to treat:","o":{"A":"Pulmonary arterial hypertension","B":"Hypertensive emergency","C":"Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction","D":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"As a PDE-5 inhibitor, sildenafil causes pulmonary vasodilation and is approved for pulmonary arterial hypertension.","incorrectas":{"B":"It is not used for hypertensive emergencies; combining it with nitrates can cause dangerous hypotension.","C":"It is not a therapy for systolic heart failure.","D":"BPH is treated with α1 blockers or 5α-reductase inhibitors, not sildenafil."}}},{"id":"anking-repro-004","fuente":"anking","tema":3,"anki":"1463346003581","q":"Tamsulosin, used for benign prostatic hyperplasia, is best classified as a:","o":{"A":"Selective α1A/α1D antagonist","B":"Non-selective α1 antagonist","C":"5α-reductase inhibitor","D":"β1-adrenergic antagonist"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Tamsulosin preferentially blocks α1A/α1D receptors in the prostate and bladder neck, relaxing smooth muscle with relatively little effect on blood pressure.","incorrectas":{"B":"Non-selective α1 blockers (terazosin, doxazosin) also lower systemic BP.","C":"5α-reductase inhibitors (finasteride) shrink the gland over months by blocking DHT synthesis.","D":"β1 blockade is unrelated to BPH treatment."}}},{"id":"anking-repro-005","fuente":"anking","tema":3,"anki":"1463345994680","q":"Terazosin, used for benign prostatic hyperplasia, acts as a:","o":{"A":"Non-selective α1 antagonist","B":"Selective α1A antagonist","C":"5α-reductase inhibitor","D":"GnRH analog"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Terazosin (like prazosin and doxazosin) non-selectively blocks α1 receptors, relaxing prostatic smooth muscle but also lowering blood pressure (first-dose orthostatic hypotension).","incorrectas":{"B":"Selective α1A blockade describes tamsulosin/silodosin.","C":"5α-reductase inhibitors act hormonally, not on adrenergic receptors.","D":"GnRH analogs (leuprolide) are used in prostate cancer, not BPH."}}},{"id":"anking-repro-006","fuente":"anking","tema":3,"anki":"1463346102353","q":"Prostatic adenocarcinoma may be treated with leuprolide. Leuprolide is a:","o":{"A":"GnRH analog","B":"Androgen-receptor antagonist","C":"5α-reductase inhibitor","D":"Aromatase inhibitor"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Leuprolide is a GnRH analog. Given continuously it downregulates pituitary GnRH receptors, lowering FSH/LH and testosterone (an initial 'flare' can occur).","incorrectas":{"B":"Androgen-receptor antagonists (flutamide) block the receptor directly.","C":"5α-reductase inhibitors block conversion of testosterone to DHT.","D":"Aromatase inhibitors block estrogen synthesis, used chiefly in breast cancer."}}},{"id":"anking-repro-007","fuente":"anking","tema":3,"anki":"1463346102353","q":"Flutamide, used in prostate cancer, works as a(n):","o":{"A":"Androgen-receptor inhibitor","B":"GnRH analog","C":"Aromatase inhibitor","D":"5α-reductase inhibitor"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Flutamide is a non-steroidal androgen-receptor antagonist that blocks testosterone/DHT signaling in prostate tissue.","incorrectas":{"B":"GnRH analogs (leuprolide) act at the pituitary.","C":"Aromatase inhibitors block estrogen synthesis.","D":"5α-reductase inhibitors (finasteride) reduce DHT formation but do not block the receptor."}}},{"id":"anking-repro-008","fuente":"anking","tema":3,"anki":"1463172352600","q":"Endometrial hyperplasia is most commonly caused by:","o":{"A":"Unopposed estrogen","B":"Excess progesterone","C":"Androgen excess","D":"Hyperprolactinemia"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Chronic estrogen stimulation without the opposing effect of progesterone drives endometrial proliferation and hyperplasia (a risk factor for endometrial carcinoma).","incorrectas":{"B":"Progesterone opposes estrogen and protects the endometrium.","C":"Androgen excess causes hirsutism/virilization, not endometrial hyperplasia directly.","D":"Hyperprolactinemia causes galactorrhea and amenorrhea, not hyperplasia."}}},{"id":"anking-repro-009","fuente":"anking","tema":3,"anki":"1475803210175","q":"In polycystic ovary syndrome, spironolactone reduces symptoms of androgen excess (e.g., hirsutism) mainly by:","o":{"A":"Antagonizing the androgen receptor","B":"Blocking aromatase","C":"Stimulating LH release","D":"Activating the estrogen receptor"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Spironolactone antagonizes the androgen receptor (and also inhibits 17α-hydroxylase, lowering androgen synthesis), improving hirsutism in PCOS.","incorrectas":{"B":"Aromatase converts androgens to estrogens; spironolactone is not an aromatase inhibitor.","C":"It does not stimulate LH; excess LH would worsen androgen production.","D":"Its benefit is anti-androgenic, not via direct estrogen-receptor activation."}}},{"id":"anking-repro-010","fuente":"anking","tema":3,"anki":"1475803214768","q":"Which anti-androgenic adverse effect may spironolactone cause in male patients?","o":{"A":"Gynecomastia","B":"Priapism","C":"Increased libido","D":"Worsening male-pattern baldness"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"By antagonizing androgen receptors, spironolactone can cause gynecomastia (as well as decreased libido and impotence) in men.","incorrectas":{"B":"Priapism is not a spironolactone effect; it is associated with PDE-5 inhibitors and certain antipsychotics.","C":"Its anti-androgen action tends to decrease, not increase, libido.","D":"Anti-androgen effects would not worsen androgenic alopecia."}}},{"id":"anking-repro-011","fuente":"anking","tema":3,"anki":"1477433035666","q":"Prazosin, terazosin, doxazosin and tamsulosin all belong to which drug class?","o":{"A":"α1-adrenergic antagonists (the '-osins')","B":"β-adrenergic antagonists","C":"5α-reductase inhibitors","D":"GnRH analogs"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"The '-osin' drugs are α1 antagonists. Non-selective ones (prazosin, terazosin, doxazosin) also lower blood pressure; tamsulosin is α1A/D-selective for the prostate.","incorrectas":{"B":"β-blockers end in '-olol' and are unrelated to this class.","C":"5α-reductase inhibitors (finasteride, dutasteride) act hormonally.","D":"GnRH analogs (leuprolide) act at the pituitary in prostate cancer."}}},{"id":"anking-antimicrobials-001","fuente":"anking","tema":4,"anki":"1402963462795","q":"What is the treatment of choice for Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia?","o":{"A":"TMP-SMX","B":"Vancomycin","C":"Fluconazole","D":"Isoniazid"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"TMP-SMX is first-line treatment for Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia; pentamidine is an alternative in patients who cannot tolerate sulfa drugs.","incorrectas":{"B":"Vancomycin treats gram-positive bacterial infections, not fungal PCP.","C":"Fluconazole is used for Candida and Cryptococcus, not Pneumocystis.","D":"Isoniazid is an anti-tuberculosis drug, not effective against Pneumocystis."}}},{"id":"anking-antimicrobials-002","fuente":"anking","tema":4,"anki":"1403470070980","q":"How do cephalosporin antibiotics affect the nephrotoxicity risk of aminoglycosides when co-administered?","o":{"A":"They increase nephrotoxicity","B":"They decrease nephrotoxicity","C":"They have no effect on nephrotoxicity","D":"They convert nephrotoxicity into ototoxicity"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Cephalosporins potentiate the nephrotoxic effects of aminoglycosides when used concurrently, increasing renal injury risk.","incorrectas":{"B":"Cephalosporins do not protect against aminoglycoside nephrotoxicity; they worsen it.","C":"There is a well-documented additive nephrotoxic interaction, not a neutral one.","D":"Ototoxicity is a separate aminoglycoside toxicity and is not converted from nephrotoxicity by cephalosporins."}}},{"id":"anking-antimicrobials-003","fuente":"anking","tema":4,"anki":"1403542843264","q":"Which bacterial ribosomal subunit does chloramphenicol target?","o":{"A":"50S subunit","B":"30S subunit","C":"40S subunit","D":"60S subunit"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Chloramphenicol binds the 50S ribosomal subunit and inhibits peptidyltransferase activity, blocking peptide bond formation.","incorrectas":{"B":"The 30S subunit is the target of aminoglycosides and tetracyclines, not chloramphenicol.","C":"The 40S subunit is a eukaryotic ribosomal subunit, not a bacterial target.","D":"The 60S subunit is also eukaryotic, not the bacterial ribosomal target of chloramphenicol."}}},{"id":"anking-antimicrobials-004","fuente":"anking","tema":4,"anki":"1403542915862","q":"Which bacterial ribosomal subunit does linezolid target?","o":{"A":"50S subunit","B":"30S subunit","C":"40S subunit","D":"70S subunit as a whole complex only"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Linezolid binds the 50S ribosomal subunit and inhibits formation of the initiation complex, preventing bacterial protein synthesis.","incorrectas":{"B":"The 30S subunit is targeted by drugs like tetracyclines and aminoglycosides, not linezolid.","C":"The 40S subunit is a eukaryotic ribosomal component, irrelevant to linezolid's bacterial target.","D":"Linezolid acts specifically at the 50S subunit during initiation complex formation, not by binding the assembled 70S ribosome nonspecifically."}}},{"id":"anking-antimicrobials-005","fuente":"anking","tema":4,"anki":"1403560526372","q":"What is the mechanism of action of chloramphenicol?","o":{"A":"Blocks peptidyltransferase at the 50S ribosomal subunit","B":"Inhibits DNA gyrase","C":"Inhibits cell wall peptidoglycan cross-linking","D":"Blocks the 30S subunit to prevent tRNA binding"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Chloramphenicol inhibits peptidyltransferase activity at the bacterial 50S ribosomal subunit, blocking peptide bond formation during protein synthesis.","incorrectas":{"B":"DNA gyrase inhibition is the mechanism of fluoroquinolones, not chloramphenicol.","C":"Cell wall cross-linking inhibition describes beta-lactams, not chloramphenicol.","D":"Blocking tRNA binding at the 30S subunit describes tetracyclines, not chloramphenicol."}}},{"id":"anking-antimicrobials-006","fuente":"anking","tema":4,"anki":"1403560979085","q":"What hematological complications are associated with chloramphenicol use, and how do they differ in dose dependence?","o":{"A":"Anemia (dose-dependent) and aplastic anemia (dose-independent)","B":"Thrombocytopenia (dose-dependent) and hemolysis (dose-independent)","C":"Neutropenia (dose-dependent) and megaloblastic anemia (dose-independent)","D":"Aplastic anemia (dose-dependent) and anemia (dose-independent)"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Chloramphenicol causes a reversible, dose-dependent anemia from bone marrow suppression, and a rare, dose-independent (idiosyncratic) aplastic anemia.","incorrectas":{"B":"Thrombocytopenia and hemolysis are not the classic paired hematologic toxicities described for chloramphenicol.","C":"Neutropenia and megaloblastic anemia are not the characteristic chloramphenicol toxicities taught in this context.","D":"The dose-dependence is reversed here: anemia is dose-dependent while aplastic anemia is the dose-independent reaction, not vice versa."}}},{"id":"anking-antimicrobials-007","fuente":"anking","tema":4,"anki":"1403561122259","q":"What is the mechanism of bacterial resistance to chloramphenicol?","o":{"A":"Plasmid-encoded acetyltransferases that inactivate the drug","B":"Efflux pumps that expel the drug from the 30S subunit","C":"Mutation of DNA gyrase preventing drug binding","D":"Beta-lactamase enzymes that hydrolyze the drug"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Resistance to chloramphenicol occurs via plasmid-encoded acetyltransferases that chemically inactivate the drug, preventing it from binding the ribosome.","incorrectas":{"B":"Efflux pumps affecting the 30S subunit are more characteristic of tetracycline resistance, not the classic chloramphenicol resistance mechanism.","C":"DNA gyrase mutations confer fluoroquinolone resistance, not chloramphenicol resistance.","D":"Beta-lactamases inactivate beta-lactam antibiotics, not chloramphenicol."}}},{"id":"anking-antimicrobials-008","fuente":"anking","tema":4,"anki":"1403571546141","q":"Which class of antibiotics can cause leg cramps, myalgias, and cartilage damage (tendinopathy)?","o":{"A":"Fluoroquinolones","B":"Macrolides","C":"Aminoglycosides","D":"Tetracyclines"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Fluoroquinolones are associated with tendinopathy, tendon rupture, myalgias, and cartilage damage, likely related to chelation of magnesium in cartilage.","incorrectas":{"B":"Macrolides are more known for GI upset and QT prolongation, not cartilage/tendon damage.","C":"Aminoglycosides are known for nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity, not cartilage damage.","D":"Tetracyclines cause tooth discoloration and bone growth inhibition in children, not the classic tendinopathy of fluoroquinolones."}}},{"id":"anking-antimicrobials-009","fuente":"anking","tema":4,"anki":"1403635238503","q":"Which viral enzyme monophosphorylates acyclovir, famciclovir, and valacyclovir, activating them (the rate-limiting step)?","o":{"A":"Thymidine kinase","B":"DNA polymerase","C":"Reverse transcriptase","D":"Neuraminidase"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Viral thymidine kinase phosphorylates acyclovir (and related prodrugs) to the monophosphate form, which is the rate-limiting step in its activation; host kinases complete conversion to the active triphosphate.","incorrectas":{"B":"Viral DNA polymerase is the enzyme these drugs ultimately inhibit, not the enzyme that activates them.","C":"Reverse transcriptase is relevant to retroviruses like HIV, not to herpesvirus activation of acyclovir.","D":"Neuraminidase is an influenza enzyme targeted by drugs like oseltamivir, unrelated to acyclovir activation."}}},{"id":"anking-antimicrobials-010","fuente":"anking","tema":4,"anki":"1403635898739","q":"What is the mechanism of viral resistance to acyclovir, famciclovir, and valacyclovir?","o":{"A":"Mutated or absent thymidine kinase","B":"Mutated viral DNA polymerase only, with normal thymidine kinase","C":"Increased efflux of the drug from infected cells","D":"Enzymatic degradation of the drug by viral proteases"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"The most common mechanism of resistance to acyclovir-family drugs is mutation or loss of viral thymidine kinase, preventing the initial phosphorylation needed for activation.","incorrectas":{"B":"While DNA polymerase mutations can also cause resistance, this is far less common than thymidine kinase mutations, making this not the best answer as stated.","C":"Efflux pump-mediated resistance is not the described mechanism for herpesvirus resistance to acyclovir.","D":"Viral proteases degrading the drug is not a recognized resistance mechanism for acyclovir-class drugs."}}},{"id":"anking-antimicrobials-011","fuente":"anking","tema":4,"anki":"1403636433207","q":"What is the treatment for CMV retinitis in immunocompromised patients when ganciclovir fails?","o":{"A":"Foscarnet or cidofovir","B":"Acyclovir or valacyclovir","C":"Amphotericin B or fluconazole","D":"Oseltamivir or zanamivir"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Foscarnet and cidofovir are second-line agents for CMV retinitis when ganciclovir fails or resistance develops, as they do not require viral kinase activation.","incorrectas":{"B":"Acyclovir and valacyclovir are not effective against CMV and are used for HSV/VZV instead.","C":"Amphotericin B and fluconazole are antifungals, not appropriate for a viral CMV infection.","D":"Oseltamivir and zanamivir are anti-influenza drugs, not used for CMV retinitis."}}},{"id":"anking-antimicrobials-012","fuente":"anking","tema":4,"anki":"1403636465989","q":"What is the treatment for acyclovir-resistant HSV infection?","o":{"A":"Foscarnet or cidofovir","B":"Ganciclovir or valganciclovir","C":"TMP-SMX or pentamidine","D":"Ribavirin or interferon"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Foscarnet and cidofovir do not require thymidine kinase activation, making them effective against acyclovir-resistant HSV strains with mutated or absent thymidine kinase.","incorrectas":{"B":"Ganciclovir and valganciclovir are primarily used for CMV, not as the standard for acyclovir-resistant HSV.","C":"TMP-SMX and pentamidine treat Pneumocystis jirovecii, not HSV.","D":"Ribavirin and interferon are used for other viral infections like hepatitis C or RSV, not acyclovir-resistant HSV."}}},{"id":"anking-antimicrobials-013","fuente":"anking","tema":4,"anki":"1403636599345","q":"What is the mechanism of action of cidofovir?","o":{"A":"Preferential direct inhibition of viral DNA polymerase without requiring viral kinase activation","B":"Inhibition of viral thymidine kinase","C":"Inhibition of viral protease","D":"Inhibition of viral neuraminidase"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Cidofovir is a nucleotide analog that directly inhibits viral DNA polymerase; it does not require phosphorylation by viral thymidine kinase, so it remains active against thymidine kinase-deficient resistant strains.","incorrectas":{"B":"Cidofovir does not act by inhibiting thymidine kinase; rather it bypasses the need for that enzyme.","C":"Protease inhibition is the mechanism of drugs like HIV protease inhibitors, not cidofovir.","D":"Neuraminidase inhibition describes anti-influenza drugs, not cidofovir's mechanism against DNA viruses."}}},{"id":"anking-antimicrobials-014","fuente":"anking","tema":4,"anki":"1403663273810","q":"Which class of antibiotics is generally avoided in pregnancy due to risk of cartilage damage in the fetus?","o":{"A":"Fluoroquinolones","B":"Penicillins","C":"Cephalosporins","D":"Macrolides"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Fluoroquinolones are avoided in pregnancy because of their potential to damage developing cartilage, based on animal studies showing arthropathy.","incorrectas":{"B":"Penicillins are generally considered safe in pregnancy and are not associated with cartilage damage.","C":"Cephalosporins are also generally considered safe in pregnancy, without the cartilage toxicity concern.","D":"Macrolides (aside from some specific concerns) are not classically linked to fetal cartilage damage the way fluoroquinolones are."}}},{"id":"anking-antimicrobials-015","fuente":"anking","tema":4,"anki":"1403663585680","q":"Which antibiotic is avoided in pregnancy/neonates because it can cause \"Gray Baby\" syndrome?","o":{"A":"Chloramphenicol","B":"Tetracycline","C":"Sulfonamides","D":"Fluoroquinolones"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Chloramphenicol causes Gray Baby syndrome in neonates due to their immature liver UDP-glucuronyltransferase, which is needed to conjugate and excrete the drug, leading to toxic accumulation.","incorrectas":{"B":"Tetracyclines cause discoloration of teeth and inhibited bone growth, not Gray Baby syndrome.","C":"Sulfonamides can cause kernicterus in neonates by displacing bilirubin, a different toxicity than Gray Baby syndrome.","D":"Fluoroquinolones are associated with cartilage damage, not Gray Baby syndrome."}}},{"id":"anking-antimicrobials-016","fuente":"anking","tema":4,"anki":"1461794799767","q":"Which two factors determine whether a patient with hepatitis C infection should be treated and what regimen to use?","o":{"A":"HCV genotype and stage of liver disease (fibrosis)","B":"Patient age and viral load only","C":"HCV genotype and presence of hepatitis B coinfection","D":"Stage of liver disease and patient's blood type"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Treatment decisions and regimen selection for chronic HCV are guided primarily by the HCV genotype and the stage of liver disease (degree of fibrosis).","incorrectas":{"B":"Age and viral load alone are not the two defining factors used to select HCV treatment regimens.","C":"Hepatitis B coinfection status is not one of the two classic determining factors for HCV treatment selection.","D":"Blood type has no bearing on HCV treatment decisions."}}},{"id":"anking-antimicrobials-017","fuente":"anking","tema":4,"anki":"1461797573387","q":"All patients with virologic evidence of chronic HCV infection should be considered for treatment except those with which condition?","o":{"A":"Limited life expectancy","B":"Mild elevation in liver enzymes","C":"Genotype 1 infection","D":"History of prior interferon therapy"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Treatment is not recommended for patients with limited life expectancy due to non-liver-related comorbid conditions, since the benefits of curing HCV would not be realized.","incorrectas":{"B":"Mild liver enzyme elevation alone is not a contraindication to considering HCV treatment.","C":"Genotype 1 is a common and treatable genotype, not an exclusion criterion.","D":"Prior interferon therapy does not exclude a patient from being considered for current HCV treatment regimens."}}},{"id":"anking-antimicrobials-018","fuente":"anking","tema":4,"anki":"1461797583285","q":"What is the term for the inability to detect HCV RNA by PCR for 6 months after stopping treatment, which is the goal of HCV therapy?","o":{"A":"Sustained virologic response (SVR)","B":"Complete remission","C":"Seroconversion","D":"Functional cure"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Sustained virologic response (SVR) is defined as undetectable HCV RNA by PCR 6 months (or per newer definitions, 12 weeks) after completing treatment, and is considered a virologic cure.","incorrectas":{"B":"\"Complete remission\" is a term more commonly used in oncology, not the standard HCV treatment outcome term.","C":"Seroconversion refers to the development of antibodies, not the endpoint used to define HCV treatment success.","D":"\"Functional cure\" is a term more associated with hepatitis B or HIV management, not the specific defined HCV treatment endpoint."}}},{"id":"anking-endocrine-pathology-019","fuente":"anking","tema":5,"anki":"1461878049146","q":"A child presents with failure to grow, short stature, mild obesity, and delayed puberty. Which hormone deficiency is most likely responsible?","o":{"A":"Growth hormone deficiency","B":"Thyroid hormone deficiency","C":"Cortisol deficiency","D":"Insulin deficiency"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Growth hormone deficiency in childhood classically presents with short stature, failure to grow, mild obesity, and delayed puberty due to inadequate GH-driven growth and metabolic effects.","incorrectas":{"B":"Thyroid hormone deficiency (cretinism) causes mental retardation and developmental delay, not this classic growth pattern.","C":"Cortisol deficiency presents with fatigue, hypotension, and hyperpigmentation (if primary), not this growth phenotype.","D":"Insulin deficiency causes hyperglycemia and diabetic symptoms, not short stature with delayed puberty."}}},{"id":"anking-endocrine-pathology-020","fuente":"anking","tema":5,"anki":"1461878103457","q":"Excess growth hormone in adults is most often caused by which of the following?","o":{"A":"A growth hormone-secreting pituitary adenoma","B":"A thyroid-stimulating hormone-secreting adenoma","C":"An adrenal cortical adenoma","D":"A prolactin-secreting adenoma"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Acromegaly (excess GH in adults) is most often caused by a GH-secreting pituitary adenoma, typically a somatotroph adenoma.","incorrectas":{"B":"A TSH-secreting adenoma causes secondary hyperthyroidism, not GH excess.","C":"Adrenal cortical adenomas cause Cushing syndrome or hyperaldosteronism, not GH excess.","D":"Prolactin-secreting adenomas cause galactorrhea/amenorrhea, not GH excess (though mixed tumors exist, isolated GH excess is due to somatotroph adenomas)."}}},{"id":"anking-endocrine-pathology-021","fuente":"anking","tema":5,"anki":"1461878128995","q":"When excess growth hormone occurs after the epiphyseal growth plates have closed (i.e., after puberty), what condition results, causing increased periosteal bone growth, organ enlargement, glucose intolerance, and coarsened facial features?","o":{"A":"Acromegaly","B":"Gigantism","C":"Cushing syndrome","D":"Hyperthyroidism"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"When GH excess occurs after puberty (closed growth plates), it causes acromegaly, characterized by periosteal (appositional) bone growth, organomegaly, glucose intolerance, and coarse facial features rather than increased height.","incorrectas":{"B":"Gigantism results from GH excess before puberty, when growth plates are still open, leading to increased linear height.","C":"Cushing syndrome results from cortisol excess, causing central obesity, striae, and moon facies, not coarsened features from bone growth.","D":"Hyperthyroidism causes weight loss, tachycardia, and heat intolerance, not coarsened facial features from bone overgrowth."}}},{"id":"anking-endocrine-pathology-022","fuente":"anking","tema":5,"anki":"1461978083695","q":"Which ADH-related disorder is characterized by low circulating ADH levels resulting in large volumes of dilute urine?","o":{"A":"Central diabetes insipidus","B":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","C":"Syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion (SIADH)","D":"Primary polydipsia"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Central diabetes insipidus results from decreased ADH secretion by the posterior pituitary, leading to decreased water reabsorption, large volumes of dilute urine, and increased serum osmolarity/Na+.","incorrectas":{"B":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus has high, not low, ADH levels, with renal resistance to its action.","C":"SIADH is characterized by excess, not deficient, ADH secretion, leading to concentrated urine and hyponatremia.","D":"Primary polydipsia results from excessive water intake with appropriately suppressed ADH, not a primary ADH deficiency."}}},{"id":"anking-endocrine-pathology-023","fuente":"anking","tema":5,"anki":"1461978108106","q":"Which type of diabetes insipidus results from an inability of the posterior pituitary to secrete adequate ADH?","o":{"A":"Central diabetes insipidus","B":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","C":"Gestational diabetes insipidus","D":"Dipsogenic diabetes insipidus"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Central diabetes insipidus arises from a deficiency of ADH secretion from the posterior pituitary (or hypothalamic damage), causing impaired water reabsorption.","incorrectas":{"B":"Nephrogenic DI is due to renal resistance to ADH, with ADH secretion intact or elevated.","C":"Gestational DI is caused by placental vasopressinase degrading ADH, not a pituitary secretion defect.","D":"Dipsogenic DI (primary polydipsia) is due to excessive thirst/fluid intake, not impaired ADH secretion."}}},{"id":"anking-endocrine-pathology-024","fuente":"anking","tema":5,"anki":"1461978112272","q":"Which type of diabetes insipidus is characterized by renal unresponsiveness to ADH despite its secretion by the posterior pituitary?","o":{"A":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","B":"Central diabetes insipidus","C":"SIADH","D":"Diabetes mellitus type 1"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus is caused by collecting duct resistance to ADH, so despite normal or elevated ADH levels, the kidney cannot concentrate urine appropriately.","incorrectas":{"B":"Central DI is due to inadequate ADH secretion from the pituitary, not renal resistance.","C":"SIADH involves excess ADH action with concentrated urine, the opposite clinical picture.","D":"Diabetes mellitus type 1 is due to insulin deficiency and hyperglycemia, unrelated to ADH signaling."}}},{"id":"anking-endocrine-pathology-025","fuente":"anking","tema":5,"anki":"1461978118121","q":"Which type of diabetes insipidus is associated with elevated circulating ADH levels (due to compensatory stimulation by increased serum osmolarity)?","o":{"A":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","B":"Central diabetes insipidus","C":"SIADH","D":"Cerebral salt wasting"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"In nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, renal resistance to ADH leads to persistent water loss and increased serum osmolarity, which stimulates further ADH secretion, so ADH levels are elevated.","incorrectas":{"B":"Central DI has low ADH levels because the defect is in pituitary secretion itself.","C":"SIADH has elevated ADH but with concentrated urine and hyponatremia, a different mechanism and presentation.","D":"Cerebral salt wasting causes hyponatremia via renal sodium loss, not primarily an ADH resistance disorder."}}},{"id":"anking-endocrine-pathology-026","fuente":"anking","tema":5,"anki":"1461978145086","q":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus is treated with which class of diuretics, which paradoxically decreases urine output?","o":{"A":"Thiazide diuretics","B":"Loop diuretics","C":"Potassium-sparing diuretics","D":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Thiazide diuretics are used to treat nephrogenic DI; they cause mild volume depletion that increases proximal Na+ and water reabsorption, paradoxically decreasing urine output.","incorrectas":{"B":"Loop diuretics impair the countercurrent multiplier and would worsen the kidney's diluting/concentrating problems in DI.","C":"Potassium-sparing diuretics are not the mainstay treatment for nephrogenic DI.","D":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors are used for conditions like glaucoma or altitude sickness, not nephrogenic DI."}}},{"id":"anking-endocrine-pathology-027","fuente":"anking","tema":5,"anki":"1461978156443","q":"In which syndrome is excess ADH secreted from an autonomous, ectopic source, such as a small cell lung carcinoma?","o":{"A":"Syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion (SIADH)","B":"Central diabetes insipidus","C":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","D":"Cushing syndrome"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"SIADH occurs when ADH is secreted autonomously from an ectopic or abnormal source, classically small cell carcinoma of the lung, leading to water retention and hyponatremia.","incorrectas":{"B":"Central DI involves ADH deficiency, the opposite of SIADH.","C":"Nephrogenic DI involves renal resistance to ADH, not autonomous ADH oversecretion.","D":"Cushing syndrome involves excess cortisol, not ADH, though ectopic ACTH can also occur with small cell lung cancer separately."}}},{"id":"anking-endocrine-pathology-028","fuente":"anking","tema":5,"anki":"1461978165029","q":"Which ADH disorder is characterized by high circulating ADH levels causing inappropriately concentrated urine, diluted body fluids, and hyponatremia?","o":{"A":"Syndrome of inappropriate ADH (SIADH)","B":"Central diabetes insipidus","C":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","D":"Primary hyperaldosteronism"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"SIADH features high ADH levels causing excess water reabsorption, concentrated urine, diluted body fluids, and hyponatremia.","incorrectas":{"B":"Central DI produces dilute urine and hypernatremia from ADH deficiency, the opposite picture.","C":"Nephrogenic DI also produces dilute urine due to renal ADH resistance, not concentrated urine.","D":"Primary hyperaldosteronism causes hypertension and hypokalemia via aldosterone excess, not an ADH-mediated water problem."}}},{"id":"anking-endocrine-pathology-029","fuente":"anking","tema":5,"anki":"1461978191219","q":"Which endocrine pathology is treated with demeclocycline, which acts as an ADH antagonist at the collecting duct?","o":{"A":"SIADH","B":"Central diabetes insipidus","C":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","D":"Hyperaldosteronism"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Demeclocycline induces a state of nephrogenic diabetes insipidus by antagonizing ADH action at the renal collecting duct, making it useful to treat the water retention of SIADH.","incorrectas":{"B":"Central DI already has deficient ADH action; giving an ADH antagonist would worsen it.","C":"Nephrogenic DI already involves ADH resistance, so an ADH antagonist would not be therapeutic.","D":"Hyperaldosteronism is treated with aldosterone antagonists like spironolactone, not demeclocycline."}}},{"id":"anking-endocrine-pathology-030","fuente":"anking","tema":5,"anki":"1462038674313","q":"Excess thyroid hormone can lead to which cardiac complication due to increased metabolic demand and cardiac output?","o":{"A":"High-output heart failure","B":"Low-output heart failure","C":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy","D":"Constrictive pericarditis"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Excess thyroid hormone increases metabolic rate and peripheral oxygen demand, driving a compensatory increase in cardiac output that can progress to high-output heart failure.","incorrectas":{"B":"Low-output heart failure is typically due to reduced contractility or ischemic damage, not thyrotoxicosis.","C":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy is usually due to infiltrative diseases like amyloidosis, unrelated to thyroid hormone excess.","D":"Constrictive pericarditis results from pericardial scarring/calcification, not thyroid hormone excess."}}},{"id":"anking-endocrine-pathology-031","fuente":"anking","tema":5,"anki":"1462038745473","q":"In thyroid hormone deficiency, how does bone age compare to chronologic age?","o":{"A":"Bone age is less than chronologic age","B":"Bone age is greater than chronologic age","C":"Bone age equals chronologic age","D":"Bone age cannot be assessed"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Thyroid hormone is required for normal bone maturation, so in hypothyroidism, bone age lags behind chronologic age.","incorrectas":{"B":"Advanced bone age relative to chronologic age is more typical of conditions with excess growth-promoting hormones or precocious puberty, not hypothyroidism.","C":"Bone age does not equal chronologic age in hypothyroidism because maturation is delayed.","D":"Bone age is readily assessed radiographically and is a standard clinical measure in hypothyroidism."}}},{"id":"anking-endocrine-pathology-032","fuente":"anking","tema":5,"anki":"1462038777530","q":"Hypothyroidism occurring in the perinatal period, if untreated, causes what irreversible outcome?","o":{"A":"Mental retardation (cretinism)","B":"Precocious puberty","C":"Gigantism","D":"Adrenal insufficiency"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Perinatal hypothyroidism causes irreversible mental retardation (cretinism) if not treated promptly, which is why neonatal thyroid screening is mandatory.","incorrectas":{"B":"Precocious puberty is not a consequence of perinatal hypothyroidism.","C":"Gigantism results from GH excess before puberty, unrelated to thyroid hormone deficiency.","D":"Adrenal insufficiency is a distinct endocrine disorder not caused by perinatal hypothyroidism."}}},{"id":"anking-endocrine-pathology-033","fuente":"anking","tema":5,"anki":"1462038801103","q":"In adulthood, which thyroid disorder presents with listlessness, slowed speech, somnolence, impaired memory, and decreased mental capacity?","o":{"A":"Hypothyroidism","B":"Hyperthyroidism","C":"Hyperparathyroidism","D":"Hypoparathyroidism"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Adult hypothyroidism slows metabolism throughout the body, producing listlessness, slowed speech, somnolence, and impaired cognition.","incorrectas":{"B":"Hyperthyroidism causes hyperexcitability, hyperreflexia, and irritability, the opposite presentation.","C":"Hyperparathyroidism causes hypercalcemia symptoms (bones, stones, groans, psychiatric overtones) rather than this specific hypothyroid picture.","D":"Hypoparathyroidism causes hypocalcemia symptoms like tetany and paresthesias, not this cognitive slowing picture."}}},{"id":"anking-endocrine-pathology-034","fuente":"anking","tema":5,"anki":"1462038815493","q":"Which thyroid disorder causes hyperexcitability, hyperreflexia, and irritability in adults?","o":{"A":"Hyperthyroidism","B":"Hypothyroidism","C":"Hypoparathyroidism","D":"Adrenal insufficiency"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Hyperthyroidism increases the metabolic and sympathetic tone of tissues, producing hyperexcitability, hyperreflexia, and irritability.","incorrectas":{"B":"Hypothyroidism causes listlessness and slowed mentation, the opposite of hyperexcitability.","C":"Hypoparathyroidism causes hypocalcemic tetany and neuromuscular irritability but is not classically described this way in relation to thyroid status.","D":"Adrenal insufficiency causes fatigue and hypotension, not hyperexcitability."}}},{"id":"anking-endocrine-pathology-035","fuente":"anking","tema":5,"anki":"1462038872057","q":"What is the most common cause of hyperthyroidism?","o":{"A":"Graves disease","B":"Toxic multinodular goiter","C":"Subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis","D":"Pituitary TSH-secreting adenoma"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Graves disease, an autoimmune condition with TSH receptor-stimulating antibodies, is the most common cause of hyperthyroidism.","incorrectas":{"B":"Toxic multinodular goiter is a cause of hyperthyroidism but is less common than Graves disease.","C":"Subacute thyroiditis typically causes transient hyperthyroidism followed by hypothyroidism and is less common than Graves disease as an overall cause.","D":"TSH-secreting pituitary adenomas are a rare cause of hyperthyroidism."}}},{"id":"anking-endocrine-pathology-036","fuente":"anking","tema":5,"anki":"1462038885820","q":"What are the TSH levels in hyperthyroidism due to Graves disease or a thyroid neoplasm, and why?","o":{"A":"Decreased, due to negative feedback from increased free T3/T4","B":"Increased, due to loss of negative feedback","C":"Normal, because the pituitary is unaffected","D":"Increased, due to a TSH-secreting pituitary tumor driving the process"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"In primary hyperthyroidism (Graves disease or thyroid neoplasm), elevated free thyroid hormone exerts negative feedback on the pituitary, suppressing TSH secretion.","incorrectas":{"B":"TSH is decreased, not increased, because negative feedback is intact and functioning appropriately in primary hyperthyroidism.","C":"TSH is measurably decreased, not normal, reflecting appropriate pituitary response to excess thyroid hormone.","D":"A TSH-secreting pituitary tumor would be a separate, rare cause of secondary hyperthyroidism with elevated (not decreased) TSH, not the mechanism in Graves disease or thyroid neoplasm."}}},{"id":"anking-hematology-oncology-pathology-037","fuente":"anking","tema":6,"anki":"1463279905770","q":"A pregnant patient presents with HELLP syndrome. Which finding is characteristic on peripheral blood smear?","o":{"A":"Schistocytes","B":"Target cells","C":"Howell-Jolly bodies","D":"Heinz bodies"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"HELLP syndrome (Hemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets) causes microangiopathic hemolytic anemia due to thrombotic microangiopathy, producing schistocytes (fragmented RBCs) as blood cells are sheared passing through fibrin strands in damaged small vessels.","incorrectas":{"A":"","B":"Target cells are seen in liver disease, thalassemia, and hemoglobinopathies, not thrombotic microangiopathy.","C":"Howell-Jolly bodies indicate asplenia/hyposplenism, unrelated to HELLP syndrome.","D":"Heinz bodies are seen in G6PD deficiency and oxidative hemolysis, not HELLP syndrome."}}},{"id":"anking-hematology-oncology-pathology-038","fuente":"anking","tema":6,"anki":"1473385315636","q":"In polycythemia, what are the typical changes in SaO2, O2 content, and PaO2?","o":{"A":"Normal SaO2 / increased O2 content / normal PaO2","B":"Decreased SaO2 / decreased O2 content / decreased PaO2","C":"Increased SaO2 / normal O2 content / increased PaO2","D":"Normal SaO2 / decreased O2 content / normal PaO2"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"In polycythemia (e.g., primary polycythemia vera or appropriate secondary polycythemia), SaO2 and PaO2 remain normal because oxygenation of hemoglobin is unaffected, but total O2 content increases because there is more hemoglobin available to carry oxygen.","incorrectas":{"B":"Decreased SaO2 and PaO2 would suggest hypoxemia, which is not required for polycythemia and does not describe the typical isolated increase in red cell mass.","C":"O2 content increases (not stays normal) due to increased hemoglobin mass, and SaO2/PaO2 typically stay normal, not increased.","D":"O2 content increases rather than decreases because there are more oxygen-carrying red cells."}}},{"id":"anking-hematology-oncology-pathology-039","fuente":"anking","tema":6,"anki":"1473385319290","q":"How does hemoglobin concentration change in polycythemia?","o":{"A":"It increases","B":"It decreases","C":"It remains unchanged","D":"It fluctuates unpredictably"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Polycythemia is defined by an increase in red blood cell mass, which directly raises hemoglobin concentration and hematocrit.","incorrectas":{"B":"A decrease in hemoglobin would indicate anemia, the opposite of polycythemia.","C":"By definition, polycythemia involves a change (increase), not a stable hemoglobin level.","D":"Polycythemia produces a consistent increase in hemoglobin, not unpredictable fluctuation."}}},{"id":"anking-hematology-oncology-pathology-040","fuente":"anking","tema":6,"anki":"1473466761107","q":"Chronic hypoxia from living at high altitude increases synthesis of which hormone, leading to polycythemia?","o":{"A":"Erythropoietin","B":"Thrombopoietin","C":"Angiotensin II","D":"Aldosterone"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Chronic hypoxia is sensed via HIF-1a, which stimulates renal peritubular interstitial fibroblasts to secrete erythropoietin (EPO). EPO increases hematocrit and hemoglobin to improve O2 transport, though it also increases blood viscosity.","incorrectas":{"B":"Thrombopoietin stimulates platelet production, not red blood cell production.","C":"Angiotensin II regulates blood pressure and aldosterone release, not erythropoiesis.","D":"Aldosterone regulates sodium and water balance, not red blood cell production."}}},{"id":"anking-hematology-oncology-pathology-041","fuente":"anking","tema":6,"anki":"1473986126890","q":"Disorders of primary hemostasis are classically divided into which two categories?","o":{"A":"Qualitative and quantitative disorders","B":"Congenital and acquired disorders","C":"Intrinsic and extrinsic pathway disorders","D":"Arterial and venous disorders"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Primary hemostasis disorders are divided into qualitative disorders (platelets are normal in number but dysfunctional) and quantitative disorders (abnormal platelet numbers, i.e., thrombocytopenia).","incorrectas":{"B":"Congenital vs. acquired is a valid distinction for many diseases but is not the primary classification scheme used for primary hemostasis disorders.","C":"Intrinsic/extrinsic pathways refer to the coagulation cascade (secondary hemostasis), not primary hemostasis.","D":"Arterial vs. venous classification applies to thrombosis location, not primary hemostasis disorders."}}},{"id":"anking-hematology-oncology-pathology-042","fuente":"anking","tema":6,"anki":"1473986130048","q":"Disorders of primary hemostasis characteristically present with which type of symptoms?","o":{"A":"Mucosal and skin bleeding","B":"Deep muscle hematomas","C":"Hemarthroses","D":"Delayed rebleeding after trauma"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Primary hemostasis (platelet plug formation) disorders classically cause mucosal and skin bleeding, such as epistaxis, gum bleeding, petechiae, and easy bruising.","incorrectas":{"B":"Deep muscle hematomas are more characteristic of secondary hemostasis (coagulation factor) disorders like hemophilia.","C":"Hemarthroses (joint bleeding) are classic for coagulation factor deficiencies (e.g., hemophilia A/B), not primary hemostasis disorders.","D":"Delayed rebleeding after trauma is typical of coagulation cascade defects, not platelet disorders."}}},{"id":"anking-hematology-oncology-pathology-043","fuente":"anking","tema":6,"anki":"1473986132369","q":"What is the most common overall symptom of mucosal bleeding in primary hemostasis disorders?","o":{"A":"Epistaxis","B":"Hemoptysis","C":"Hematuria","D":"Menorrhagia"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Epistaxis (nosebleed) is the most common overall symptom of mucosal bleeding in primary hemostasis disorders, though hemoptysis, GI bleeding, hematuria, and menorrhagia can also occur.","incorrectas":{"B":"Hemoptysis is a recognized mucosal bleeding symptom but is not the most common one overall.","C":"Hematuria can occur in primary hemostasis disorders but is less common than epistaxis.","D":"Menorrhagia is a recognized symptom in affected women but is not the most common overall symptom across the general population."}}},{"id":"anking-hematology-oncology-pathology-044","fuente":"anking","tema":6,"anki":"1473986139292","q":"What is a feared complication of severe thrombocytopenia?","o":{"A":"Intracranial bleeding","B":"Deep vein thrombosis","C":"Pulmonary embolism","D":"Ischemic stroke"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Severe thrombocytopenia can lead to spontaneous intracranial hemorrhage, a life-threatening complication that is the most feared consequence of very low platelet counts.","incorrectas":{"B":"Deep vein thrombosis is a clotting disorder, the opposite problem from thrombocytopenia-related bleeding.","C":"Pulmonary embolism results from thrombus formation, not from a bleeding tendency due to low platelets.","D":"Ischemic stroke results from vascular occlusion, not from the bleeding tendency seen in thrombocytopenia."}}},{"id":"anking-hematology-oncology-pathology-045","fuente":"anking","tema":6,"anki":"1473986144525","q":"Quantitative disorders of primary hemostasis (thrombocytopenia) present with which small (1-2 mm) skin bleeding finding?","o":{"A":"Petechiae","B":"Purpura","C":"Ecchymoses","D":"Hematoma"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Petechiae are 1-2 mm pinpoint hemorrhages that are a hallmark sign of thrombocytopenia (a quantitative platelet disorder); they are not typically seen in qualitative platelet disorders.","incorrectas":{"B":"Purpura refers to larger skin bleeding (>3 mm), not the 1-2 mm lesions described.","C":"Ecchymoses are larger bruises (>1 cm), much bigger than petechiae.","D":"A hematoma is a larger, palpable collection of blood, not a 1-2 mm lesion."}}},{"id":"anking-hematology-oncology-pathology-046","fuente":"anking","tema":6,"anki":"1473986157280","q":"What term describes skin bleeding lesions greater than 3 mm seen in primary hemostasis disorders?","o":{"A":"Purpura","B":"Petechiae","C":"Ecchymoses","D":"Telangiectasia"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Purpura refers to skin hemorrhages greater than 3 mm in size, intermediate between petechiae (1-2 mm) and ecchymoses (>1 cm).","incorrectas":{"B":"Petechiae are smaller (1-2 mm), not the >3 mm lesions described.","C":"Ecchymoses are larger still, typically greater than 1 cm.","D":"Telangiectasias are dilated superficial blood vessels, not hemorrhagic skin lesions from platelet disorders."}}},{"id":"anking-hematology-oncology-pathology-047","fuente":"anking","tema":6,"anki":"1473986160718","q":"What term describes skin bleeding lesions greater than 1 cm in primary hemostasis disorders?","o":{"A":"Ecchymoses","B":"Petechiae","C":"Purpura","D":"Spider angiomata"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Ecchymoses are larger bruises, greater than 1 cm in size, representing the largest category of skin hemorrhage in this size-based classification.","incorrectas":{"B":"Petechiae are the smallest lesions (1-2 mm), not >1 cm.","C":"Purpura describes intermediate-sized lesions (>3 mm), smaller than ecchymoses.","D":"Spider angiomata are vascular lesions associated with liver disease/hyperestrogenism, not hemostasis disorders."}}},{"id":"anking-hematology-oncology-pathology-048","fuente":"anking","tema":6,"anki":"1473986163852","q":"Disorders of primary hemostasis present with which easily-occurring skin finding?","o":{"A":"Easy bruising","B":"Hyperpigmentation","C":"Urticaria","D":"Jaundice"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Easy bruising is a classic skin manifestation of primary hemostasis disorders, reflecting impaired platelet plug formation that allows minor trauma to cause visible hemorrhage.","incorrectas":{"B":"Hyperpigmentation is not a typical feature of primary hemostasis disorders.","C":"Urticaria (hives) is an allergic skin reaction, unrelated to platelet function.","D":"Jaundice reflects hyperbilirubinemia (e.g., from hemolysis or liver disease), not a bleeding disorder finding."}}},{"id":"anking-hematology-oncology-pathology-049","fuente":"anking","tema":6,"anki":"1473986166644","q":"Petechiae are a sign of thrombocytopenia and are typically NOT seen in which category of platelet disorders?","o":{"A":"Qualitative platelet disorders","B":"Quantitative platelet disorders","C":"Coagulation factor disorders","D":"Vitamin K deficiency"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Petechiae specifically indicate thrombocytopenia (a quantitative disorder); qualitative platelet disorders (normal platelet count but abnormal function) do not typically cause petechiae.","incorrectas":{"B":"Quantitative disorders (thrombocytopenia) are precisely the category that DOES cause petechiae.","C":"Coagulation factor disorders (secondary hemostasis) more typically cause deep bleeding like hemarthrosis rather than petechiae specifically.","D":"Vitamin K deficiency affects coagulation factors (secondary hemostasis), a distinct category from the qualitative/quantitative platelet distinction being tested."}}},{"id":"anking-hematology-oncology-pathology-050","fuente":"anking","tema":6,"anki":"1473986169357","q":"Below what platelet count does thrombocytopenia typically become symptomatic?","o":{"A":"50,000/uL","B":"150,000/uL","C":"10,000/uL","D":"100,000/uL"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Symptoms of thrombocytopenia (such as petechiae and easy bruising) typically begin to appear when the platelet count falls below approximately 50,000/uL.","incorrectas":{"B":"150,000/uL is near the lower limit of the normal platelet range and is not the symptomatic threshold.","C":"10,000/uL is a much lower threshold associated with risk of spontaneous severe/life-threatening bleeding, not the general onset of symptoms.","D":"100,000/uL is above the level at which symptoms classically begin to appear."}}},{"id":"anking-hematology-oncology-pathology-051","fuente":"anking","tema":6,"anki":"1473986182575","q":"What is the most common cause of thrombocytopenia in both children and adults?","o":{"A":"Immune thrombocytopenic purpura","B":"Disseminated intravascular coagulation","C":"Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","D":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) is the most common cause of thrombocytopenia in both children and adults, resulting from autoantibody-mediated platelet destruction.","incorrectas":{"B":"DIC is a less common, typically acute and critically ill-associated cause of thrombocytopenia, not the most common overall cause.","C":"TTP is a rare cause of thrombocytopenia involving microangiopathic hemolysis, much less common than ITP.","D":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia occurs only in the specific context of heparin exposure, making it far less common overall than ITP."}}},{"id":"anking-hematology-oncology-pathology-052","fuente":"anking","tema":6,"anki":"1473986200310","q":"In ITP, antibody-bound platelets are consumed by which cells, resulting in thrombocytopenia?","o":{"A":"Splenic macrophages","B":"Kupffer cells","C":"Neutrophils","D":"Renal mesangial cells"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"In ITP, platelets coated with autoantibodies (typically IgG against platelet glycoproteins) are recognized and phagocytosed by splenic macrophages, leading to accelerated platelet destruction and thrombocytopenia.","incorrectas":{"B":"Kupffer cells are hepatic macrophages; the spleen is the primary and classically tested site of antibody-coated platelet clearance in ITP.","C":"Neutrophils are not the primary phagocytic cells responsible for antibody-coated platelet clearance in ITP.","D":"Renal mesangial cells are involved in glomerular immune complex clearance, not platelet destruction."}}},{"id":"anking-hematology-oncology-pathology-053","fuente":"anking","tema":6,"anki":"1473986202821","q":"Immune thrombocytopenic purpura commonly follows what type of preceding infection?","o":{"A":"Viral infection","B":"Bacterial infection","C":"Fungal infection","D":"Parasitic infection"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"ITP, especially in children, commonly follows a viral infection, which is thought to trigger the autoimmune antibody response against platelets.","incorrectas":{"B":"Bacterial infections are not the classic preceding trigger for ITP; viral infections are far more strongly associated.","C":"Fungal infections are not a recognized common antecedent trigger for ITP.","D":"Parasitic infections are not the classic preceding trigger for ITP."}}},{"id":"anking-autonomic-drugs-054","fuente":"anking","tema":7,"anki":"1461615074564","q":"Histamine released from enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells acts on which receptor, and to which G-protein is it coupled, on the basolateral membrane of gastric parietal cells?","o":{"A":"H2 receptor / Gs","B":"H1 receptor / Gq","C":"M3 receptor / Gq","D":"CCK-B receptor / Gq"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Histamine acts on H2 receptors coupled to Gs proteins on parietal cells, activating adenylyl cyclase and increasing cAMP, which stimulates the H+/K+ ATPase to increase acid secretion.","incorrectas":{"B":"H1 receptors are Gq-coupled and mediate allergic/inflammatory responses, not parietal cell acid secretion via this pathway.","C":"M3 receptors mediate acetylcholine's stimulatory effect on parietal cells but are Gq-coupled, not the histamine receptor described.","D":"CCK-B (gastrin) receptors are Gq-coupled and respond to gastrin, not histamine."}}},{"id":"anking-autonomic-drugs-055","fuente":"anking","tema":7,"anki":"1461806819909","q":"Misoprostol is a synthetic analog of which endogenous compound?","o":{"A":"PGE1","B":"PGE2","C":"Thromboxane A2","D":"Leukotriene B4"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Misoprostol is a synthetic prostaglandin E1 (PGE1) analog used to protect the gastric mucosa and induce labor.","incorrectas":{"B":"PGE2 is a different prostaglandin involved in labor and fever but is not what misoprostol mimics.","C":"Thromboxane A2 promotes platelet aggregation and vasoconstriction, unrelated to misoprostol's mechanism.","D":"Leukotriene B4 is a chemotactic mediator in inflammation, not a prostaglandin analog target of misoprostol."}}},{"id":"anking-autonomic-drugs-056","fuente":"anking","tema":7,"anki":"1461806838245","q":"Misoprostol protects the stomach by increasing production/secretion of which barrier and decreasing production of what substance?","o":{"A":"Increased mucous barrier / decreased acid production","B":"Increased bicarbonate barrier / decreased pepsin production","C":"Increased mucous barrier / decreased gastrin production","D":"Increased surfactant barrier / decreased acid production"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Misoprostol increases gastric mucous production and secretion while decreasing gastric acid production, making it useful in preventing NSAID-induced peptic ulcers.","incorrectas":{"B":"Bicarbonate and pepsin are not the specific barrier/substance pair described for misoprostol's mechanism.","C":"Misoprostol's acid-reducing effect is on gastric acid, not gastrin secretion directly.","D":"Surfactant is unrelated to gastric mucosal protection; this is a distractor from pulmonary physiology."}}},{"id":"anking-autonomic-drugs-057","fuente":"anking","tema":7,"anki":"1461806919056","q":"Misoprostol promotes what type of contraction, making it useful for which obstetric indication?","o":{"A":"Uterine contraction / induction of labor (cervical ripening)","B":"Uterine contraction / prevention of preterm labor","C":"Vascular smooth muscle contraction / treatment of hypotension","D":"Bronchial smooth muscle contraction / treatment of asthma"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Misoprostol stimulates uterine smooth muscle contraction and ripens the cervix, making it useful for labor induction and management of postpartum hemorrhage.","incorrectas":{"B":"Misoprostol promotes labor rather than preventing it, the opposite effect.","C":"It does not act on vascular smooth muscle to treat hypotension; this is an unrelated indication.","D":"It causes uterine, not bronchial, smooth muscle contraction, and would not treat asthma."}}},{"id":"anking-autonomic-drugs-058","fuente":"anking","tema":7,"anki":"1461806938449","q":"Misoprostol is contraindicated in which population due to what type of effect?","o":{"A":"Women of childbearing potential / abortifacient effects","B":"Elderly patients / nephrotoxic effects","C":"Children / hepatotoxic effects","D":"Men with peptic ulcers / androgenic effects"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Because misoprostol can induce uterine contractions and cervical ripening, it has abortifacient potential and is contraindicated in women who could be pregnant.","incorrectas":{"B":"Misoprostol is not primarily known for nephrotoxicity in the elderly.","C":"Hepatotoxicity in children is not the described contraindication for misoprostol.","D":"Misoprostol has no androgenic effects; this option is fabricated for this context."}}},{"id":"anking-autonomic-drugs-059","fuente":"anking","tema":7,"anki":"1461806954057","q":"Misoprostol use is limited in practice mainly due to which common side effect?","o":{"A":"Diarrhea","B":"Constipation","C":"Hepatotoxicity","D":"Agranulocytosis"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Diarrhea is a common and often limiting side effect of misoprostol due to its prostaglandin-mediated effects on GI motility.","incorrectas":{"B":"Misoprostol causes increased GI motility and diarrhea, not constipation.","C":"Hepatotoxicity is not a characteristic side effect of misoprostol.","D":"Agranulocytosis is associated with other drugs (e.g., clozapine), not misoprostol."}}},{"id":"anking-autonomic-drugs-060","fuente":"anking","tema":7,"anki":"1461884871184","q":"ADH acts on principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct via which receptor?","o":{"A":"V2 receptor","B":"V1 receptor","C":"Oxytocin receptor","D":"Angiotensin II type 1 receptor"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"ADH (vasopressin) binds V2 receptors on renal principal cells, promoting water reabsorption via aquaporin-2 channel insertion.","incorrectas":{"B":"V1 receptors are located on vascular smooth muscle, not renal principal cells.","C":"Oxytocin receptors mediate uterine contraction and milk ejection, unrelated to renal water handling.","D":"AT1 receptors respond to angiotensin II and mediate vasoconstriction/aldosterone release, not ADH's renal action."}}},{"id":"anking-autonomic-drugs-061","fuente":"anking","tema":7,"anki":"1461884917022","q":"Which second messenger propagates signaling from the ADH-bound V2 receptor?","o":{"A":"cAMP","B":"IP3/Ca2+","C":"cGMP","D":"DAG"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"V2 receptors are Gs-coupled, activating adenylyl cyclase to increase cAMP, which drives aquaporin-2 translocation.","incorrectas":{"B":"IP3/Ca2+ is the second messenger pathway for V1 receptors (Gq-coupled), not V2.","C":"cGMP is used in nitric oxide/natriuretic peptide signaling, not V2 receptor signaling.","D":"DAG is generated alongside IP3 in Gq signaling, which applies to V1, not V2 receptors."}}},{"id":"anking-autonomic-drugs-062","fuente":"anking","tema":7,"anki":"1461884968628","q":"The receptor for ADH on vascular smooth muscle is which type?","o":{"A":"V1 receptor","B":"V2 receptor","C":"Alpha-1 receptor","D":"Beta-2 receptor"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"ADH acts on V1 receptors located on vascular smooth muscle to cause vasoconstriction.","incorrectas":{"B":"V2 receptors are found in the renal collecting duct, not vascular smooth muscle.","C":"Alpha-1 receptors also cause vasoconstriction but are activated by catecholamines, not ADH's primary vascular receptor.","D":"Beta-2 receptors cause vasodilation and are not the ADH receptor on vascular smooth muscle."}}},{"id":"anking-autonomic-drugs-063","fuente":"anking","tema":7,"anki":"1461884982922","q":"Which second messenger(s) propagate signaling from the ADH-bound V1 receptor?","o":{"A":"IP3/Ca2+","B":"cAMP","C":"cGMP","D":"Arachidonic acid alone"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"V1 receptors are Gq-coupled, activating phospholipase C to generate IP3 and increase intracellular Ca2+, leading to smooth muscle contraction.","incorrectas":{"B":"cAMP is the second messenger for V2 receptor signaling (Gs-coupled), not V1.","C":"cGMP is not part of the Gq/PLC pathway used by V1 receptors.","D":"Arachidonic acid alone is not the defining second messenger system of V1 receptor signaling."}}},{"id":"anking-autonomic-drugs-064","fuente":"anking","tema":7,"anki":"1461884987103","q":"What is the end result of ADH binding to V1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle?","o":{"A":"Contraction of vascular smooth muscle (vasoconstriction)","B":"Relaxation of vascular smooth muscle (vasodilation)","C":"Increased renal water reabsorption","D":"Decreased total peripheral resistance"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"V1 receptor activation on vascular smooth muscle leads to contraction, arteriolar constriction, and increased total peripheral resistance.","incorrectas":{"B":"V1 activation causes contraction, not relaxation, of vascular smooth muscle.","C":"Increased water reabsorption is the result of V2, not V1, receptor activation.","D":"V1 activation increases, rather than decreases, total peripheral resistance."}}},{"id":"anking-autonomic-drugs-065","fuente":"anking","tema":7,"anki":"1461885006895","q":"Which receptor does ADH (vasopressin) bind to regulate serum osmolarity?","o":{"A":"V2 receptors","B":"V1 receptors","C":"Oxytocin receptors","D":"Aldosterone receptors"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"ADH regulates serum osmolarity primarily through V2 receptors in the renal collecting duct, promoting free water reabsorption.","incorrectas":{"B":"V1 receptors regulate vascular tone and blood pressure, not primarily osmolarity.","C":"Oxytocin receptors are unrelated to osmolarity regulation.","D":"Aldosterone acts on mineralocorticoid receptors to regulate sodium/potassium, not via ADH's osmolarity-regulating receptor."}}},{"id":"anking-autonomic-drugs-066","fuente":"anking","tema":7,"anki":"1463844488060","q":"The ADH-activated V1 receptor is coupled to which G-protein?","o":{"A":"Gq","B":"Gs","C":"Gi","D":"G12/13"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"V1 receptors are coupled to Gq, which activates phospholipase C to increase IP3/Ca2+, causing vascular smooth muscle contraction.","incorrectas":{"B":"Gs coupling and cAMP generation describe the V2 receptor pathway, not V1.","C":"Gi coupling inhibits adenylyl cyclase and is not the pathway used by V1 receptors.","D":"G12/13 mediates Rho-GTPase signaling and is not the classic V1 receptor pathway described in this context."}}},{"id":"anking-autonomic-drugs-067","fuente":"anking","tema":7,"anki":"1463844501109","q":"ADH activation of which receptor on vascular smooth muscle causes vasoconstriction and increased blood pressure?","o":{"A":"V1 receptor","B":"V2 receptor","C":"Beta-1 receptor","D":"Angiotensin II type 2 receptor"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"V1 receptor activation on vascular smooth muscle mediates vasoconstriction, raising blood pressure.","incorrectas":{"B":"V2 receptors act on the kidney to increase water reabsorption, not directly causing vasoconstriction.","C":"Beta-1 receptors increase cardiac contractility and heart rate, not the ADH vasoconstrictor receptor.","D":"AT2 receptors generally oppose AT1-mediated vasoconstriction and are not ADH receptors."}}},{"id":"anking-autonomic-drugs-068","fuente":"anking","tema":7,"anki":"1463844507593","q":"The ADH-activated V2 receptor is coupled to which G-protein?","o":{"A":"Gs","B":"Gq","C":"Gi","D":"Golf"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"V2 receptors couple to Gs, activating adenylyl cyclase to increase cAMP, promoting aquaporin-2 insertion into the apical membrane.","incorrectas":{"B":"Gq coupling with IP3/Ca2+ signaling describes the V1 receptor, not V2.","C":"Gi inhibits adenylyl cyclase and is not the V2 receptor pathway.","D":"Golf is specific to olfactory receptor signaling, unrelated to V2 receptors."}}},{"id":"anking-autonomic-drugs-069","fuente":"anking","tema":7,"anki":"1463844517299","q":"The V2 receptor for ADH is located on which membrane of the collecting duct principal cell?","o":{"A":"Basolateral membrane","B":"Apical membrane","C":"Luminal brush border","D":"Tight junction complex"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"V2 receptors are located on the basolateral membrane, where ADH binds to trigger a cAMP cascade that causes aquaporin-2 channels to translocate to the apical membrane.","incorrectas":{"B":"Aquaporin-2 channels translocate to the apical membrane, but the V2 receptor itself is on the basolateral membrane.","C":"The brush border refers to proximal tubule microvilli, not the collecting duct principal cell membrane where V2 resides.","D":"Tight junctions are cell-cell adhesion structures, not the location of the V2 receptor."}}},{"id":"anking-autonomic-drugs-070","fuente":"anking","tema":7,"anki":"1463844524624","q":"ADH-activated V2 receptors cause translocation of which channels to the renal apical membrane, increasing free water reabsorption?","o":{"A":"Aquaporin 2 channels","B":"Aquaporin 1 channels","C":"ENaC sodium channels","D":"ROMK potassium channels"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"V2 receptor activation increases cAMP, causing aquaporin-2 channels to translocate to the apical membrane, allowing water reabsorption.","incorrectas":{"B":"Aquaporin 1 channels are constitutively expressed in the proximal tubule and are not regulated by ADH/V2 signaling.","C":"ENaC channels mediate sodium reabsorption and are regulated by aldosterone, not directly by ADH's aquaporin effect.","D":"ROMK channels mediate potassium secretion in the collecting duct and are not the ADH-regulated water channel."}}},{"id":"anking-autonomic-drugs-071","fuente":"anking","tema":7,"anki":"1463845075159","q":"Desmopressin (DDAVP) has high specificity for which ADH receptor?","o":{"A":"V2 receptor","B":"V1 receptor","C":"Oxytocin receptor","D":"Angiotensin II receptor"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Desmopressin is a selective V2 receptor agonist, used to treat central diabetes insipidus and nocturnal enuresis by promoting renal water reabsorption.","incorrectas":{"B":"Desmopressin has minimal V1 activity compared to natural ADH, distinguishing it from vasopressin's vasoconstrictive effects.","C":"Desmopressin does not target oxytocin receptors; it is selective for vasopressin V2 receptors.","D":"Angiotensin II receptors are unrelated to desmopressin's mechanism of action."}}},{"id":"anking-reproductive-system-pathology-072","fuente":"anking","tema":8,"anki":"1462981993876","q":"Androgen insensitivity syndrome in genotypic males (46,XY) is caused by a deficiency of which receptor in target tissues?","o":{"A":"Androgen receptors","B":"Estrogen receptors","C":"5-alpha-reductase enzyme","D":"LH receptors"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Androgen insensitivity syndrome results from a defective/absent androgen receptor, so target tissues cannot respond to testosterone/DHT despite normal or elevated levels, producing a 46,XY genotype with female external genitalia.","incorrectas":{"B":"Estrogen receptor deficiency is not the mechanism; these patients actually have unopposed estrogen effects due to lack of androgen action.","C":"5-alpha-reductase deficiency is a distinct disorder impairing conversion of testosterone to DHT, causing ambiguous genitalia rather than complete receptor unresponsiveness.","D":"LH receptor defects (e.g., Leydig cell hypoplasia) impair testosterone synthesis itself, not the tissue response to androgens."}}},{"id":"anking-reproductive-system-pathology-073","fuente":"anking","tema":8,"anki":"1463093900462","q":"A cystic dilation of the Bartholin gland is known by what name?","o":{"A":"Bartholin cyst","B":"Skene gland cyst","C":"Nabothian cyst","D":"Gartner duct cyst"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"A Bartholin cyst is a cystic dilation of the Bartholin gland, often associated with Neisseria gonorrhoeae infection.","incorrectas":{"B":"Skene gland cysts arise from the periurethral Skene glands, not the Bartholin glands.","C":"Nabothian cysts are mucus-filled cysts on the cervix, unrelated to the Bartholin gland.","D":"Gartner duct cysts arise from remnants of the mesonephric (Wolffian) duct along the lateral vaginal wall."}}},{"id":"anking-reproductive-system-pathology-074","fuente":"anking","tema":8,"anki":"1463093911940","q":"Which gland, located on each side of the vaginal canal, produces a mucus-like fluid that drains via ducts into the lower vestibule?","o":{"A":"Bartholin gland","B":"Skene gland","C":"Cowper gland","D":"Adrenal gland"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"The Bartholin glands are paired glands at the vaginal introitus that secrete mucus-like fluid via ducts into the lower vestibule, aiding lubrication.","incorrectas":{"B":"Skene glands are periurethral glands, not located adjacent to the vaginal canal.","C":"Cowper (bulbourethral) glands are male accessory glands, not present in females.","D":"The adrenal gland is an endocrine gland unrelated to vaginal/vulvar lubrication."}}},{"id":"anking-reproductive-system-pathology-075","fuente":"anking","tema":8,"anki":"1463093944484","q":"Bartholin cysts arise due to inflammation and what process affecting the Bartholin gland?","o":{"A":"Obstruction of the gland duct","B":"Neoplastic transformation","C":"Autoimmune destruction","D":"Ischemic infarction"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Bartholin cysts form when inflammation causes obstruction of the gland's duct, leading to accumulation of secretions and cystic dilation.","incorrectas":{"B":"Neoplastic transformation is not the mechanism of simple Bartholin cyst formation.","C":"Autoimmune destruction is not implicated in Bartholin cyst pathogenesis.","D":"Ischemic infarction does not cause the cystic dilation seen in Bartholin cysts."}}},{"id":"anking-reproductive-system-pathology-076","fuente":"anking","tema":8,"anki":"1463093952878","q":"Bartholin cysts are most commonly seen in which demographic?","o":{"A":"Women of reproductive age","B":"Postmenopausal women","C":"Prepubertal girls","D":"Elderly men"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Bartholin cysts most commonly occur in women of reproductive age, when the glands are hormonally active.","incorrectas":{"B":"Postmenopausal women are more classically associated with lichen sclerosus, not Bartholin cysts.","C":"Prepubertal girls rarely develop Bartholin cysts since the glands are not yet hormonally active.","D":"Bartholin glands are female structures, so this condition does not occur in men."}}},{"id":"anking-reproductive-system-pathology-077","fuente":"anking","tema":8,"anki":"1463093959932","q":"A unilateral, painful cystic lesion at the lower vestibule adjacent to the vaginal canal, which may develop into an abscess, describes what condition?","o":{"A":"Bartholin cyst","B":"Vulvar condyloma","C":"Lichen sclerosus","D":"Vaginal fibroma"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"A Bartholin cyst classically presents as a unilateral, painful cystic lesion near the vaginal introitus and can become a Bartholin abscess if infected.","incorrectas":{"B":"Vulvar condyloma presents as warty, verrucous lesions rather than a cystic mass.","C":"Lichen sclerosus presents with thinned, whitish, atrophic vulvar skin, not a discrete cystic lesion.","D":"Vaginal fibroma is a rare benign tumor, not typically presenting as a painful unilateral cyst."}}},{"id":"anking-reproductive-system-pathology-078","fuente":"anking","tema":8,"anki":"1463093999696","q":"A warty neoplasm of genital skin, often caused by low-risk HPV strains and often large, is called what?","o":{"A":"Condyloma","B":"Melanoma","C":"Squamous cell carcinoma","D":"Lipoma"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Condyloma (condyloma acuminatum) is a warty outgrowth of genital skin caused by low-risk HPV types, most commonly types 6 and 11.","incorrectas":{"B":"Melanoma is a pigmented malignant neoplasm of melanocytes, not a warty HPV-related lesion.","C":"Squamous cell carcinoma is a malignant lesion, typically linked to high-risk HPV types, not the benign warty condyloma.","D":"Lipoma is a benign fatty tumor unrelated to HPV infection."}}},{"id":"anking-reproductive-system-pathology-079","fuente":"anking","tema":8,"anki":"1463094017212","q":"What is the most common cause of condyloma acuminata?","o":{"A":"HPV types 6 and 11","B":"HPV types 16 and 18","C":"HSV-2","D":"Treponema pallidum"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Condyloma acuminata is most commonly caused by low-risk HPV types 6 and 11.","incorrectas":{"B":"HPV types 16 and 18 are high-risk types associated with cervical and other carcinomas, not typical condyloma.","C":"HSV-2 causes genital herpes with painful vesicles/ulcers, not warty condylomas.","D":"Treponema pallidum causes syphilis, whose secondary stage produces condyloma lata, a different flat, moist lesion."}}},{"id":"anking-reproductive-system-pathology-080","fuente":"anking","tema":8,"anki":"1463094049348","q":"What are the three areas of the female lower genital tract most commonly affected by HPV?","o":{"A":"Vulva, vaginal canal, and cervix","B":"Ovary, fallopian tube, and uterus","C":"Bladder, urethra, and vulva","D":"Cervix, endometrium, and myometrium"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"HPV most commonly affects the lower genital tract structures: the vulva, vaginal canal, and cervix.","incorrectas":{"B":"The ovary, fallopian tube, and uterus are upper genital tract structures not typically the primary sites of HPV infection.","C":"The bladder and urethra are not classic sites of HPV-related genital lesions.","D":"The endometrium and myometrium are uterine layers not typically involved by HPV."}}},{"id":"anking-reproductive-system-pathology-081","fuente":"anking","tema":8,"anki":"1463094084820","q":"Histologically, HPV-associated condylomas are characterized by which cellular finding?","o":{"A":"Koilocytes","B":"Reed-Sternberg cells","C":"Psammoma bodies","D":"Councilman bodies"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Koilocytes, squamous cells with raisin-shaped nuclei and perinuclear clearing, are the classic histologic finding in HPV-associated condylomas.","incorrectas":{"B":"Reed-Sternberg cells are characteristic of Hodgkin lymphoma, not HPV lesions.","C":"Psammoma bodies are seen in papillary carcinomas (e.g., papillary thyroid, serous ovarian), not condylomas.","D":"Councilman bodies are apoptotic hepatocytes seen in viral hepatitis and yellow fever."}}},{"id":"anking-reproductive-system-pathology-082","fuente":"anking","tema":8,"anki":"1463094128418","q":"How often do condylomas (caused by low-risk HPV types 6/11) progress to carcinoma?","o":{"A":"Rarely","B":"Almost always","C":"In about 50% of cases","D":"Only in immunocompromised men"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Condylomas caused by low-risk HPV types 6 and 11 rarely progress to carcinoma, unlike lesions caused by high-risk HPV types such as 16 and 18.","incorrectas":{"B":"Malignant progression is uncommon with low-risk HPV types; this is more typical of high-risk types.","C":"A 50% malignant transformation rate is far higher than what is actually observed with condyloma.","D":"Progression risk is related to HPV type/immune status broadly, not restricted specifically to immunocompromised men."}}},{"id":"anking-reproductive-system-pathology-083","fuente":"anking","tema":8,"anki":"1463094144885","q":"Lichen sclerosus is characterized by thinning of the epidermis and what change in the dermis?","o":{"A":"Fibrosis (sclerosis)","B":"Granuloma formation","C":"Amyloid deposition","D":"Fatty infiltration"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Lichen sclerosus shows epidermal thinning combined with dermal fibrosis (sclerosis), giving the vulvar skin a whitish, parchment-like appearance.","incorrectas":{"B":"Granuloma formation is not a feature of lichen sclerosus; it is more typical of infections like TB or sarcoidosis.","C":"Amyloid deposition characterizes amyloidosis, unrelated to lichen sclerosus.","D":"Fatty infiltration is not a histologic feature of lichen sclerosus."}}},{"id":"anking-reproductive-system-pathology-084","fuente":"anking","tema":8,"anki":"1463094181362","q":"Lichen sclerosus is most commonly seen in which demographic?","o":{"A":"Postmenopausal women","B":"Prepubertal girls only","C":"Young reproductive-age men","D":"Adolescent males"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Lichen sclerosus most commonly affects postmenopausal women and may have an autoimmune etiology.","incorrectas":{"B":"While lichen sclerosus can rarely occur in prepubertal girls, it is not the classic or most common demographic.","C":"Lichen sclerosus is primarily a vulvar condition seen in women, not typically young men.","D":"Adolescent males are not the characteristic demographic for this vulvar dermatosis."}}},{"id":"anking-reproductive-system-pathology-085","fuente":"anking","tema":8,"anki":"1463094190367","q":"Does lichen sclerosus carry an increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma?","o":{"A":"Yes, a slightly increased risk","B":"No increased risk at all","C":"Yes, a markedly increased risk of over 50%","D":"It causes basal cell carcinoma instead"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Lichen sclerosus is associated with a slightly increased risk of vulvar squamous cell carcinoma, so patients require monitoring.","incorrectas":{"B":"There is a small but real increased risk, so 'no increased risk' is incorrect.","C":"The increased risk is only slight, not on the order of 50%.","D":"Lichen sclerosus is linked to squamous cell carcinoma, not basal cell carcinoma."}}},{"id":"anking-reproductive-system-pathology-086","fuente":"anking","tema":8,"anki":"1463094195758","q":"Does lichen simplex chronicus carry an increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma?","o":{"A":"No","B":"Yes, markedly increased","C":"Yes, slightly increased","D":"Only if HPV co-infection is present"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Unlike lichen sclerosus, lichen simplex chronicus does not carry an increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma.","incorrectas":{"B":"Lichen simplex chronicus is not associated with a markedly increased malignancy risk.","C":"It is lichen sclerosus, not lichen simplex chronicus, that carries a slightly increased risk.","D":"HPV co-infection is not a recognized factor linking lichen simplex chronicus to carcinoma."}}},{"id":"anking-reproductive-system-pathology-087","fuente":"anking","tema":8,"anki":"1463094201489","q":"Lichen sclerosus and lichen simplex chronicus are both generally what kind of condition?","o":{"A":"Benign","B":"Malignant","C":"Premalignant in all cases","D":"Infectious"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Both lichen sclerosus and lichen simplex chronicus are generally benign vulvar conditions, though lichen sclerosus carries a small malignancy risk.","incorrectas":{"B":"Neither condition is malignant in itself.","C":"Only lichen sclerosus carries a small increased malignancy risk; neither is universally premalignant.","D":"Both are inflammatory/reactive dermatologic conditions, not infectious diseases."}}},{"id":"anking-reproductive-system-pathology-088","fuente":"anking","tema":8,"anki":"1463094212090","q":"Hyperplasia of the vulvar squamous epithelium describes which condition?","o":{"A":"Lichen simplex chronicus","B":"Lichen sclerosus","C":"Condyloma acuminatum","D":"Vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Lichen simplex chronicus is characterized by hyperplasia (thickening) of the vulvar squamous epithelium, typically from chronic rubbing or scratching.","incorrectas":{"B":"Lichen sclerosus features epidermal thinning, the opposite of hyperplasia.","C":"Condyloma acuminatum is an HPV-induced warty growth, not simple epithelial hyperplasia.","D":"Vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia is a precancerous dysplastic lesion, not simple hyperplasia."}}},{"id":"anking-reproductive-system-pathology-089","fuente":"anking","tema":8,"anki":"1463094263813","q":"Lichen simplex chronicus is associated with chronic irritation/inflammation and what behavior?","o":{"A":"Scratching","B":"Sexual intercourse","C":"Tampon use","D":"Douching"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Lichen simplex chronicus results from chronic irritation and repetitive scratching, leading to epithelial hyperplasia (thickened, leathery skin).","incorrectas":{"B":"Sexual intercourse is not the classic association driving lichen simplex chronicus.","C":"Tampon use is not specifically linked to this condition's pathogenesis.","D":"Douching is not the recognized behavioral trigger for lichen simplex chronicus."}}},{"id":"anking-neurology-special-senses-pathology-090","fuente":"anking","tema":9,"anki":"1480519696929","q":"Hemisection of the spinal cord (loss of one lateral half) produces which classic clinical syndrome?","o":{"A":"Brown-Sequard syndrome","B":"Anterior cord syndrome","C":"Central cord syndrome","D":"Werdnig-Hoffmann disease"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Brown-Sequard syndrome results from hemisection of the spinal cord, producing a characteristic combination of ipsilateral and contralateral deficits below the lesion.","incorrectas":{"B":"Anterior cord syndrome results from occlusion of the anterior spinal artery, causing bilateral loss of pain/temperature and motor function, not hemisection.","C":"Central cord syndrome typically results from hyperextension injury or syringomyelia, causing bilateral upper extremity weakness/sensory loss.","D":"Werdnig-Hoffmann disease is an infantile motor neuron disease (SMA type 1), unrelated to spinal cord hemisection."}}},{"id":"anking-neurology-special-senses-pathology-091","fuente":"anking","tema":9,"anki":"1480519732488","q":"In Brown-Sequard syndrome, loss of vibration, proprioception, and touch (dorsal column damage) occurs on which side relative to the lesion?","o":{"A":"Ipsilateral","B":"Contralateral","C":"Bilateral","D":"None of the above (unaffected)"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"The dorsal columns carry vibration, proprioception, and fine touch information that ascends ipsilaterally before decussating in the medulla, so damage causes ipsilateral loss below the lesion.","incorrectas":{"B":"Contralateral loss below the lesion applies to the spinothalamic tract (pain/temperature), not the dorsal columns.","C":"The deficit is unilateral (ipsilateral), not bilateral, in a hemisection injury.","D":"Dorsal column function is definitely impaired ipsilaterally after hemisection."}}},{"id":"anking-neurology-special-senses-pathology-092","fuente":"anking","tema":9,"anki":"1480519737086","q":"In Brown-Sequard syndrome, loss of pain and temperature sensation (spinothalamic tract damage) occurs on which side relative to the lesion?","o":{"A":"Ipsilateral","B":"Contralateral","C":"Bilateral","D":"There is no sensory loss"},"r":"B","e":{"correcta":"The spinothalamic tract decussates near the level of entry, so fibers ascending in the tract on the side of the lesion actually carry pain/temperature information from the opposite side of the body, producing contralateral loss below the lesion.","incorrectas":{"A":"Ipsilateral loss below the lesion is characteristic of dorsal column damage, not the spinothalamic tract.","C":"The deficit is unilateral (contralateral), not bilateral.","D":"Pain and temperature sensation is lost contralaterally due to the crossed spinothalamic tract."}}},{"id":"anking-neurology-special-senses-pathology-093","fuente":"anking","tema":9,"anki":"1480519741494","q":"In Brown-Sequard syndrome, loss of ALL sensory modalities occurring specifically AT the level of the lesion (rather than below it) is on which side?","o":{"A":"Ipsilateral","B":"Contralateral","C":"Bilateral","D":"No sensory loss occurs at the level of the lesion"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"At the level of the lesion, the damage affects the entering dorsal root and gray matter on the same side, causing complete ipsilateral loss of all sensory modalities (vibration, proprioception, touch, pain, temperature) at that segmental level.","incorrectas":{"B":"Contralateral loss occurs below the lesion for pain/temperature due to the crossed spinothalamic tract, not at the level of the lesion.","C":"The loss at the level of the lesion is unilateral (ipsilateral), reflecting local segmental damage.","D":"There is in fact a band of complete ipsilateral sensory loss at the level of the lesion."}}},{"id":"anking-neurology-special-senses-pathology-094","fuente":"anking","tema":9,"anki":"1480545346945","q":"Poliomyelitis is caused by infection with which pathogen?","o":{"A":"Poliovirus","B":"Coxsackievirus","C":"Rabies virus","D":"West Nile virus"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Poliomyelitis is caused directly by poliovirus, an enterovirus that infects and destroys anterior horn motor neurons.","incorrectas":{"B":"Coxsackievirus is another enterovirus causing distinct diseases (e.g., hand-foot-mouth disease, myocarditis), not classic poliomyelitis.","C":"Rabies virus causes a fatal encephalitis transmitted by animal bites, not poliomyelitis.","D":"West Nile virus can cause a poliomyelitis-like flaccid paralysis but is a distinct flavivirus, not the cause of classic poliomyelitis."}}},{"id":"anking-neurology-special-senses-pathology-095","fuente":"anking","tema":9,"anki":"1480549817498","q":"Poliovirus is transmitted primarily via which route?","o":{"A":"Fecal-oral route","B":"Respiratory droplets","C":"Sexual contact","D":"Vector-borne (mosquito)"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Poliovirus spreads via the fecal-oral route, replicating initially in the oropharynx and gut before potentially reaching the CNS.","incorrectas":{"B":"Respiratory droplet transmission is characteristic of viruses like influenza, not poliovirus.","C":"Sexual transmission is not a route for poliovirus spread.","D":"Poliovirus is not vector-borne; it is transmitted through contaminated food/water via the fecal-oral route."}}},{"id":"anking-neurology-special-senses-pathology-096","fuente":"anking","tema":9,"anki":"1480549839004","q":"Which region of the spinal cord gray matter is most affected by poliovirus infection?","o":{"A":"Ventral (anterior) horn","B":"Dorsal (posterior) horn","C":"Lateral horn","D":"Dorsal columns"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Poliovirus has a tropism for anterior (ventral) horn motor neurons, leading to their destruction and resultant flaccid paralysis.","incorrectas":{"B":"The dorsal horn contains sensory neurons, which are typically spared in poliomyelitis (sensation is preserved).","C":"The lateral horn contains preganglionic autonomic neurons, not the primary site of poliovirus damage.","D":"The dorsal columns are white matter sensory tracts, not gray matter motor neurons, and are not the target of poliovirus."}}},{"id":"anking-neurology-special-senses-pathology-097","fuente":"anking","tema":9,"anki":"1480549844650","q":"What spinal cord level is most commonly affected by poliovirus?","o":{"A":"Lumbar levels","B":"Cervical levels","C":"Thoracic levels","D":"Sacral levels"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Poliovirus most commonly affects the lumbar spinal cord levels, which is why lower extremity weakness is a classic presentation.","incorrectas":{"B":"Cervical levels can be involved but are not the most commonly affected site.","C":"Thoracic levels are less characteristically affected compared to lumbar levels.","D":"Sacral levels are not the most commonly cited site of involvement in poliomyelitis."}}},{"id":"anking-neurology-special-senses-pathology-098","fuente":"anking","tema":9,"anki":"1480549851019","q":"Does poliomyelitis classically present with symmetric or asymmetric weakness?","o":{"A":"Asymmetric weakness","B":"Symmetric weakness","C":"Purely proximal symmetric weakness","D":"Purely distal symmetric weakness"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Poliomyelitis classically presents with asymmetric weakness, which is a key feature distinguishing it from Werdnig-Hoffmann disease (infantile SMA), which causes symmetric weakness.","incorrectas":{"B":"Symmetric weakness is characteristic of Werdnig-Hoffmann disease (SMA), not poliomyelitis.","C":"Poliomyelitis weakness is not specifically described as purely proximal and symmetric.","D":"Poliomyelitis weakness is asymmetric, not a symmetric distal pattern."}}},{"id":"anking-neurology-special-senses-pathology-099","fuente":"anking","tema":9,"anki":"1480549859622","q":"Poliomyelitis presents with weakness that is asymmetric and affecting which distribution of the body?","o":{"A":"Bilateral (though asymmetric between sides)","B":"Strictly unilateral, sparing the other side entirely","C":"Only the upper extremities","D":"Only the cranial nerves"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Poliomyelitis causes bilateral involvement, but the weakness is asymmetric in severity between the two sides, distinguishing it from a symmetric bilateral pattern.","incorrectas":{"B":"Poliomyelitis is not strictly unilateral; it typically involves both sides, just asymmetrically.","C":"Weakness is not limited to the upper extremities; the lumbar (lower extremity) region is most classically affected.","D":"While bulbar/cranial nerve involvement can occur in severe cases, the classic finding is bilateral asymmetric limb weakness, not isolated cranial nerve involvement."}}},{"id":"anking-neurology-special-senses-pathology-100","fuente":"anking","tema":9,"anki":"1480549890498","q":"Werdnig-Hoffmann disease is the infantile (type 1) form of which disease?","o":{"A":"Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA)","B":"Poliomyelitis","C":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)","D":"Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Werdnig-Hoffmann disease is the severe infantile form (type 1) of spinal muscular atrophy, caused by mutation of the SMN1 gene leading to loss of anterior horn motor neurons.","incorrectas":{"B":"Poliomyelitis is a viral infection of anterior horn cells, a distinct entity from the genetic disease SMA.","C":"ALS is an adult-onset motor neuron disease affecting both upper and lower motor neurons, not the infantile SMA.","D":"Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease is a hereditary peripheral neuropathy, not a motor neuron disease like SMA."}}},{"id":"anking-neurology-special-senses-pathology-101","fuente":"anking","tema":9,"anki":"1480549898334","q":"Spinal muscular atrophy damages which type of motor neurons?","o":{"A":"Lower motor neurons","B":"Upper motor neurons","C":"Both upper and lower motor neurons equally","D":"Neither; it damages sensory neurons"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Spinal muscular atrophy selectively destroys lower motor neurons (anterior horn cells), producing flaccid paralysis, hyporeflexia, and fasciculations.","incorrectas":{"B":"Upper motor neuron damage would cause spasticity and hyperreflexia, which is not the pattern seen in SMA.","C":"SMA does not involve upper motor neurons; only lower motor neurons are affected.","D":"SMA is a motor neuron disease; sensory neurons are characteristically spared."}}},{"id":"anking-neurology-special-senses-pathology-102","fuente":"anking","tema":9,"anki":"1480549901442","q":"What is the median age of death for infants with untreated Werdnig-Hoffmann disease (SMA type 1)?","o":{"A":"7 months","B":"7 years","C":"2 months","D":"18 years"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Werdnig-Hoffmann disease (SMA type 1) has a median age of death around 7 months without treatment, reflecting its severe, rapidly progressive course; newer therapies like nusinersen can prolong survival.","incorrectas":{"B":"7 years overstates typical untreated survival for this severe infantile form.","C":"2 months understates the typical median survival age.","D":"18 years is far beyond the natural history of untreated SMA type 1 and would be more consistent with milder SMA subtypes."}}},{"id":"anking-neurology-special-senses-pathology-103","fuente":"anking","tema":9,"anki":"1480549909273","q":"Does spinal muscular atrophy present with symmetric or asymmetric weakness?","o":{"A":"Symmetric weakness","B":"Asymmetric weakness","C":"Weakness limited to one limb","D":"No weakness, only sensory loss"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Spinal muscular atrophy presents with symmetric weakness, an important distinguishing feature from poliomyelitis, which presents with asymmetric weakness.","incorrectas":{"B":"Asymmetric weakness is characteristic of poliomyelitis, not SMA.","C":"SMA is not limited to one limb; it causes diffuse symmetric weakness.","D":"SMA causes motor weakness, not primary sensory loss, since it is a motor neuron disease."}}},{"id":"anking-neurology-special-senses-pathology-104","fuente":"anking","tema":9,"anki":"1480549920521","q":"Spinal muscular atrophy presents with weakness affecting which distribution?","o":{"A":"Bilateral, symmetric weakness","B":"Bilateral, asymmetric weakness","C":"Unilateral weakness only","D":"Weakness confined to the cranial nerves"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"SMA causes bilateral and symmetric weakness, in contrast to the bilateral but asymmetric weakness pattern seen in poliomyelitis.","incorrectas":{"B":"Bilateral asymmetric weakness is the pattern seen in poliomyelitis, not SMA.","C":"SMA is not unilateral; it affects both sides symmetrically.","D":"Bulbar weakness can occur in severe SMA, but the hallmark is symmetric bilateral limb weakness, not isolated cranial nerve findings."}}},{"id":"anking-neurology-special-senses-pathology-105","fuente":"anking","tema":9,"anki":"1480549940609","q":"Which neurologic complication is classically associated with tertiary syphilis?","o":{"A":"Tabes dorsalis","B":"Guillain-Barre syndrome","C":"Multiple sclerosis","D":"Wernicke encephalopathy"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Tabes dorsalis is a classic neurologic manifestation of tertiary (late) syphilis, resulting from Treponema pallidum-induced degeneration of the dorsal columns and dorsal roots.","incorrectas":{"B":"Guillain-Barre syndrome is an autoimmune polyneuropathy often triggered by antecedent infection (e.g., Campylobacter), not a manifestation of syphilis.","C":"Multiple sclerosis is a demyelinating autoimmune disease of the CNS unrelated to syphilis infection.","D":"Wernicke encephalopathy results from thiamine (B1) deficiency, not from syphilis infection."}}},{"id":"anking-neurology-special-senses-pathology-106","fuente":"anking","tema":9,"anki":"1480549946177","q":"Tabes dorsalis involves progressive demyelination of which two structures?","o":{"A":"Dorsal columns and dorsal roots","B":"Corticospinal tracts and anterior horn cells","C":"Spinothalamic tracts and ventral roots","D":"Cerebellar peduncles and dentate nucleus"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Tabes dorsalis is characterized by progressive demyelination of the dorsal columns and dorsal (posterior) roots, leading to loss of proprioception and vibration sense.","incorrectas":{"B":"The corticospinal tracts and anterior horn cells are affected in motor neuron diseases like ALS, not tabes dorsalis.","C":"The spinothalamic tracts and ventral roots are not the primary structures damaged in tabes dorsalis.","D":"The cerebellar peduncles and dentate nucleus are cerebellar structures unrelated to tabes dorsalis pathology."}}},{"id":"anking-neurology-special-senses-pathology-107","fuente":"anking","tema":9,"anki":"1480549960718","q":"Tabes dorsalis may present with progressive sensory ataxia due to impairment of which sensory modality?","o":{"A":"Proprioception","B":"Pain sensation","C":"Temperature sensation","D":"Auditory sensation"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Impaired proprioception due to dorsal column degeneration in tabes dorsalis leads to poor coordination and a characteristic high-stepping, slapping gait (sensory ataxia).","incorrectas":{"B":"Pain sensation is carried by the spinothalamic tract, which is not the primary tract damaged in tabes dorsalis.","C":"Temperature sensation is also carried by the spinothalamic tract, not the dorsal columns affected in tabes dorsalis.","D":"Auditory sensation is unrelated to the dorsal column pathology causing sensory ataxia in tabes dorsalis."}}},{"id":"anking-gastrointestinal-pathology-108","fuente":"anking","tema":10,"anki":"1463150148468","q":"What is the most common cause of hematochezia (lower GI bleeding) in adults?","o":{"A":"Diverticulosis","B":"Angiodysplasia","C":"Hemorrhoids","D":"Colorectal carcinoma"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Diverticulosis is the most common cause of hematochezia in adults, due to rupture of the vasa recta at the neck or dome of a diverticulum.","incorrectas":{"B":"Angiodysplasia is the second most common cause of hematochezia, not the most common.","C":"Hemorrhoids typically cause minor bright red bleeding but are not the leading overall cause.","D":"Colorectal carcinoma can cause bleeding but is a less common cause of significant hematochezia than diverticulosis."}}},{"id":"anking-gastrointestinal-pathology-109","fuente":"anking","tema":10,"anki":"1464478560360","q":"Ursodeoxycholic acid (ursodiol) is used clinically for the prevention or dissolution of which condition?","o":{"A":"Gallstones","B":"Renal calculi","C":"Peptic ulcers","D":"Esophageal varices"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Ursodeoxycholic acid reduces cholesterol saturation of bile, helping to prevent and dissolve cholesterol gallstones.","incorrectas":{"B":"Ursodiol is not used for renal stones, which are managed with other agents (e.g., thiazides, citrate).","C":"Peptic ulcers are treated with acid suppression (PPIs, H2 blockers), not ursodiol.","D":"Esophageal varices are managed with beta-blockers or endoscopic therapy, not ursodiol."}}},{"id":"anking-gastrointestinal-pathology-110","fuente":"anking","tema":10,"anki":"1464478566239","q":"Ursodeoxycholic acid promotes gallstone dissolution primarily by which mechanism?","o":{"A":"Increasing bile secretion and decreasing cholesterol secretion/reabsorption","B":"Increasing cholesterol secretion into bile","C":"Inhibiting bile acid synthesis entirely","D":"Increasing gallbladder contraction via CCK stimulation"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Ursodeoxycholic acid increases bile flow and decreases cholesterol secretion and intestinal reabsorption, lowering the cholesterol saturation index of bile.","incorrectas":{"B":"It decreases, not increases, cholesterol secretion into bile.","C":"It does not eliminate bile acid synthesis; it alters the bile acid pool composition.","D":"Its mechanism is biochemical (bile composition), not primarily related to gallbladder motility."}}},{"id":"anking-gastrointestinal-pathology-111","fuente":"anking","tema":10,"anki":"1471825802842","q":"Hemangiomas are benign tumors composed of which cell/tissue type?","o":{"A":"Blood vessels","B":"Smooth muscle","C":"Adipocytes","D":"Lymphatic channels"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Hemangiomas are benign vascular tumors composed of proliferating blood vessels, most commonly found in the skin and liver.","incorrectas":{"B":"Smooth muscle tumors are leiomyomas, not hemangiomas.","C":"Adipocyte tumors are lipomas.","D":"Tumors of lymphatic channels are lymphangiomas, a distinct entity."}}},{"id":"anking-gastrointestinal-pathology-112","fuente":"anking","tema":10,"anki":"1471825973147","q":"Hemangiomas most often involve which two organs?","o":{"A":"Skin and liver","B":"Lung and spleen","C":"Kidney and pancreas","D":"Brain and bone"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Hemangiomas most commonly arise in the skin and liver, and blanch with pressure (distinguishing them from purpura).","incorrectas":{"B":"Lung and spleen are not the classic sites for hemangiomas.","C":"Kidney and pancreas are not typical hemangioma locations.","D":"Brain and bone are less typical sites; cavernous hemangiomas can rarely occur in brain but skin/liver are classic."}}},{"id":"anking-gastrointestinal-pathology-113","fuente":"anking","tema":10,"anki":"1471825985658","q":"Hemangiomas commonly present at birth and often do what during childhood?","o":{"A":"Regress spontaneously","B":"Rapidly metastasize","C":"Transform into angiosarcoma","D":"Calcify completely"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Infantile hemangiomas typically appear at birth or shortly after and often regress spontaneously during childhood.","incorrectas":{"B":"Hemangiomas are benign and do not metastasize.","C":"Malignant transformation to angiosarcoma from a hemangioma is not a typical natural history.","D":"Complete calcification is not the typical course; regression is the classic pattern."}}},{"id":"anking-gastrointestinal-pathology-114","fuente":"anking","tema":10,"anki":"1471825988252","q":"Which tumor is a highly aggressive malignant proliferation of endothelial cells, commonly affecting skin, breast, and liver?","o":{"A":"Angiosarcoma","B":"Hemangioma","C":"Kaposi sarcoma","D":"Lymphangiosarcoma"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Angiosarcomas are highly aggressive malignant tumors of endothelial cell origin, commonly involving skin, breast, and liver (the latter associated with vinyl chloride, arsenic, and Thorotrast exposure).","incorrectas":{"B":"Hemangiomas are benign, not aggressive malignant tumors.","C":"Kaposi sarcoma is associated with HHV-8 and is a distinct endothelial malignancy, classically in HIV/immunosuppressed patients, not the general description given.","D":"Lymphangiosarcoma arises from lymphatic endothelium, classically after chronic lymphedema (e.g., post-mastectomy), not the classic answer here."}}},{"id":"anking-gastrointestinal-pathology-115","fuente":"anking","tema":10,"anki":"1473628000924","q":"Which liver pathology is associated with alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency?","o":{"A":"Cirrhosis","B":"Hepatic adenoma","C":"Primary biliary cholangitis","D":"Hemochromatosis"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency causes cirrhosis due to accumulation of misfolded, polymerized alpha-1-antitrypsin protein within hepatocytes.","incorrectas":{"B":"Hepatic adenoma is associated with oral contraceptive use, not alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency.","C":"Primary biliary cholangitis is an autoimmune disease of intrahepatic bile ducts, unrelated to alpha-1-antitrypsin.","D":"Hemochromatosis is caused by iron overload, a distinct genetic disorder (HFE gene)."}}},{"id":"anking-gastrointestinal-pathology-116","fuente":"anking","tema":10,"anki":"1473628007883","q":"In alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency, where does the mutant protein polymerize and accumulate, causing liver damage?","o":{"A":"Endoplasmic reticulum","B":"Golgi apparatus","C":"Mitochondria","D":"Nucleus"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Mutant alpha-1-antitrypsin misfolds and accumulates in the endoplasmic reticulum of hepatocytes, visible as PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules.","incorrectas":{"B":"The Golgi is not the site of accumulation; the protein never properly progresses beyond the ER.","C":"Mitochondria are not involved in alpha-1-antitrypsin protein accumulation.","D":"The nucleus is not the site of accumulation of the misfolded protein."}}},{"id":"anking-gastrointestinal-pathology-117","fuente":"anking","tema":10,"anki":"1473628016236","q":"What is the normal (wild-type) allele for alpha-1-antitrypsin?","o":{"A":"PiM","B":"PiZ","C":"PiS","D":"PiNull"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"PiM is the normal, most common allele for alpha-1-antitrypsin, associated with normal enzyme levels and function.","incorrectas":{"B":"PiZ is the most clinically significant mutant allele, associated with disease.","C":"PiS is a variant allele associated with intermediate reduction in AAT levels, not the normal allele.","D":"PiNull alleles produce no AAT protein at all and are associated with severe emphysema without liver disease."}}},{"id":"anking-gastrointestinal-pathology-118","fuente":"anking","tema":10,"anki":"1473628019626","q":"What is the most clinically significant mutant allele for alpha-1-antitrypsin?","o":{"A":"PiZ","B":"PiM","C":"PiS","D":"PiF"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"PiZ is the most clinically significant mutant allele; it is a codominant trait, so even PiMZ heterozygotes carry risk, especially with smoking.","incorrectas":{"B":"PiM is the normal allele, not the pathogenic mutant.","C":"PiS is associated with a milder reduction in AAT and is less clinically significant than PiZ.","D":"PiF is not a recognized major clinically significant allele in alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency."}}},{"id":"anking-gastrointestinal-pathology-119","fuente":"anking","tema":10,"anki":"1473628022683","q":"Which genotype is associated with an asymptomatic phenotype with decreased alpha-1-antitrypsin levels?","o":{"A":"PiMZ heterozygotes","B":"PiZZ homozygotes","C":"PiMM homozygotes","D":"PiSS homozygotes"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"PiMZ heterozygotes have decreased but still substantial alpha-1-antitrypsin levels and are typically asymptomatic unless additional risk factors (e.g., smoking) are present.","incorrectas":{"B":"PiZZ homozygotes have severely reduced AAT and are at high risk for both emphysema and liver cirrhosis, not asymptomatic.","C":"PiMM homozygotes are the normal genotype with normal AAT levels.","D":"PiSS homozygotes have a milder deficiency phenotype but are not the classic asymptomatic heterozygote answer."}}},{"id":"anking-gastrointestinal-pathology-120","fuente":"anking","tema":10,"anki":"1473628025370","q":"PiMZ heterozygotes with alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency are at significant risk for emphysema if they do what?","o":{"A":"Smoke","B":"Drink alcohol","C":"Take NSAIDs","D":"Have high dietary iron intake"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Smoking significantly increases the risk of emphysema in PiMZ heterozygotes by overwhelming their already reduced antiprotease reserve.","incorrectas":{"B":"Alcohol use is a risk factor for liver disease broadly but is not the classic trigger described for AAT-related emphysema.","C":"NSAID use is not linked to increased emphysema risk in AAT deficiency.","D":"Dietary iron intake relates to hemochromatosis, not alpha-1-antitrypsin-associated emphysema."}}},{"id":"anking-gastrointestinal-pathology-121","fuente":"anking","tema":10,"anki":"1473628028243","q":"Which genotype is associated with significant risk for panacinar emphysema and liver cirrhosis in alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency?","o":{"A":"PiZZ homozygotes","B":"PiMZ heterozygotes","C":"PiMM homozygotes","D":"PiMS heterozygotes"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"PiZZ homozygotes have markedly reduced alpha-1-antitrypsin levels, leading to significant risk of panacinar emphysema (often in young, nonsmoking patients) and liver cirrhosis.","incorrectas":{"B":"PiMZ heterozygotes have a milder phenotype, with risk mainly if they smoke.","C":"PiMM homozygotes are normal with no increased risk.","D":"PiMS heterozygotes have only mildly reduced AAT and low disease risk."}}},{"id":"anking-gastrointestinal-pathology-122","fuente":"anking","tema":10,"anki":"1474503557098","q":"Oropharyngeal carcinoma is most strongly associated with which virus?","o":{"A":"HPV-16","B":"Epstein-Barr virus","C":"HSV-1","D":"HHV-8"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"HPV-16, a high-risk HPV subtype, is strongly associated with oropharyngeal (tonsillar/base of tongue) squamous cell carcinoma.","incorrectas":{"B":"Epstein-Barr virus is associated with nasopharyngeal carcinoma, not oropharyngeal carcinoma.","C":"HSV-1 causes oral herpes (cold sores), not oropharyngeal carcinoma.","D":"HHV-8 is associated with Kaposi sarcoma, not oropharyngeal carcinoma."}}},{"id":"anking-gastrointestinal-pathology-123","fuente":"anking","tema":10,"anki":"1478834249508","q":"Which condition presents with a triad of iron deficiency anemia, dysphagia, and a beefy red tongue?","o":{"A":"Plummer-Vinson syndrome","B":"Zenker diverticulum","C":"Achalasia","D":"Eosinophilic esophagitis"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Plummer-Vinson syndrome classically presents with the triad of iron deficiency anemia, esophageal webs causing dysphagia, and glossitis (beefy red tongue).","incorrectas":{"B":"Zenker diverticulum causes dysphagia and halitosis but not the anemia/glossitis triad.","C":"Achalasia presents with dysphagia to both solids and liquids due to LES dysfunction, not this triad.","D":"Eosinophilic esophagitis presents with dysphagia and food impaction, often with allergy history, not this classic triad."}}},{"id":"anking-gastrointestinal-pathology-124","fuente":"anking","tema":10,"anki":"1482191360472","q":"Painful, shallow, red ulcers involving the oral mucosa, commonly called \"cold sores,\" describe which condition?","o":{"A":"Oral herpes","B":"Aphthous ulcers","C":"Oral candidiasis","D":"Herpangina"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Oral herpes (cold sores) presents as painful, shallow, red ulcers of the oral mucosa and lips, typically due to HSV-1 reactivation.","incorrectas":{"B":"Aphthous ulcers (canker sores) are non-infectious, painful ulcers not caused by a virus.","C":"Oral candidiasis (thrush) presents as white plaques, not vesicular ulcers.","D":"Herpangina, caused by coxsackievirus, causes posterior oropharyngeal ulcers, distinct from herpes labialis."}}},{"id":"anking-gastrointestinal-pathology-125","fuente":"anking","tema":10,"anki":"1482191366011","q":"Oral herpes (cold sores) is usually caused by infection with which virus?","o":{"A":"HSV-1","B":"HSV-2","C":"VZV","D":"CMV"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Oral herpes (herpes labialis) is usually caused by HSV-1, which establishes latency in the trigeminal ganglion and reactivates to cause cold sores.","incorrectas":{"B":"HSV-2 is more classically associated with genital herpes, though overlap can occur.","C":"VZV causes chickenpox and shingles, not typical cold sores.","D":"CMV causes mononucleosis-like illness and congenital infection, not oral cold sores."}}},{"id":"anking-immune-responses-126","fuente":"anking","tema":11,"anki":"1461794583601","q":"Upregulation of interferon synthesis in virus-infected cells is primarily accomplished by which class of signaling molecules?","o":{"A":"Interleukins","B":"Complement proteins","C":"Immunoglobulins","D":"Histamine"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Interleukins (particularly type I interferon-inducing cytokine signals) upregulate interferon synthesis in infected cells, enhancing the antiviral state of surrounding cells.","incorrectas":{"B":"Complement proteins mediate opsonization and lysis, not interferon gene upregulation.","C":"Immunoglobulins are antibodies that neutralize antigens; they do not directly upregulate interferon synthesis.","D":"Histamine is a mediator of allergic/inflammatory responses, not interferon induction."}}},{"id":"anking-immune-responses-127","fuente":"anking","tema":11,"anki":"1471806482193","q":"Which laboratory finding is classically associated with granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener granulomatosis)?","o":{"A":"Positive c-ANCA","B":"Positive p-ANCA","C":"Positive ANA","D":"Positive anti-dsDNA"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis is associated with positive c-ANCA (cytoplasmic ANCA), also known as PR3-ANCA or anti-proteinase 3 antibody.","incorrectas":{"B":"p-ANCA is associated with microscopic polyangiitis and Churg-Strauss syndrome, not Wegener.","C":"ANA is associated with lupus and other connective tissue diseases, not vasculitis of this type.","D":"Anti-dsDNA is specific for systemic lupus erythematosus, not vasculitis."}}},{"id":"anking-immune-responses-128","fuente":"anking","tema":11,"anki":"1471806505942","q":"How does microscopic polyangiitis present differently from granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener)?","o":{"A":"Microscopic polyangiitis lacks nasopharyngeal involvement and granulomas","B":"Microscopic polyangiitis always involves asthma and eosinophilia","C":"Microscopic polyangiitis is associated with c-ANCA rather than p-ANCA","D":"Microscopic polyangiitis causes granulomatous inflammation of the upper respiratory tract"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Microscopic polyangiitis lacks nasopharyngeal involvement and granuloma formation, distinguishing it from granulomatosis with polyangiitis, which is p-ANCA vs c-ANCA respectively.","incorrectas":{"B":"Asthma and eosinophilia are features of Churg-Strauss syndrome (eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis), not microscopic polyangiitis.","C":"Microscopic polyangiitis is associated with p-ANCA, while Wegener is associated with c-ANCA — this option reverses the association.","D":"Granulomatous inflammation of the upper respiratory tract is characteristic of Wegener, not microscopic polyangiitis."}}},{"id":"anking-immune-responses-129","fuente":"anking","tema":11,"anki":"1471806519835","q":"Which serologic marker is classically positive in microscopic polyangiitis?","o":{"A":"p-ANCA","B":"c-ANCA","C":"Anti-GBM antibody","D":"Rheumatoid factor"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Microscopic polyangiitis is associated with positive p-ANCA (perinuclear ANCA), also known as MPO-ANCA or anti-myeloperoxidase antibody.","incorrectas":{"B":"c-ANCA is associated with granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener), not microscopic polyangiitis.","C":"Anti-GBM antibody is characteristic of Goodpasture syndrome, a distinct disease.","D":"Rheumatoid factor is associated with rheumatoid arthritis, not ANCA-associated vasculitis."}}},{"id":"anking-immune-responses-130","fuente":"anking","tema":11,"anki":"1471806647224","q":"Which serologic marker is classically positive in Churg-Strauss syndrome (eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis)?","o":{"A":"p-ANCA","B":"c-ANCA","C":"Anti-Smith antibody","D":"Anti-centromere antibody"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Churg-Strauss syndrome is associated with positive p-ANCA (perinuclear ANCA), also known as MPO-ANCA or anti-myeloperoxidase antibody.","incorrectas":{"B":"c-ANCA is characteristic of granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener), a different vasculitis.","C":"Anti-Smith antibody is specific for systemic lupus erythematosus.","D":"Anti-centromere antibody is associated with limited systemic sclerosis (CREST syndrome)."}}},{"id":"anking-immune-responses-131","fuente":"anking","tema":11,"anki":"1473388953084","q":"Which two vasculitides are classically associated with a positive serum p-ANCA?","o":{"A":"Churg-Strauss syndrome and microscopic polyangiitis","B":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis and Kawasaki disease","C":"Polyarteritis nodosa and Takayasu arteritis","D":"Giant cell arteritis and Behçet disease"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Churg-Strauss syndrome and microscopic polyangiitis are both associated with positive p-ANCA (MPO-ANCA); microscopic polyangiitis additionally lacks the asthma and eosinophilia seen in Churg-Strauss.","incorrectas":{"B":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis is associated with c-ANCA, not p-ANCA, and Kawasaki disease is not an ANCA-associated vasculitis.","C":"Polyarteritis nodosa and Takayasu arteritis are not typically ANCA-associated vasculitides.","D":"Giant cell arteritis and Behçet disease are large/variable vessel vasculitides not associated with ANCA."}}},{"id":"anking-immune-responses-132","fuente":"anking","tema":11,"anki":"1473986197639","q":"In immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP), the autoantibodies against platelets are produced by plasma cells located primarily in which organ?","o":{"A":"Spleen","B":"Liver","C":"Bone marrow","D":"Thymus"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"In ITP, autoantibodies against platelet antigens (e.g., GpIIb/IIIa) are produced by plasma cells in the spleen, which is also the primary site of antibody-coated platelet destruction.","incorrectas":{"B":"The liver is not the primary site of autoantibody production in ITP, though it can contribute to platelet clearance.","C":"Bone marrow is the site of platelet production, not the main source of the pathogenic autoantibodies in ITP.","D":"The thymus is involved in T-cell maturation and central tolerance, not plasma cell antibody production in ITP."}}},{"id":"anking-immune-responses-133","fuente":"anking","tema":11,"anki":"1475376623320","q":"Which cause of pauci-immune glomerulonephritis is associated with c-ANCA (PR3-ANCA)?","o":{"A":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener)","B":"Microscopic polyangiitis","C":"Churg-Strauss syndrome","D":"Goodpasture syndrome"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener granulomatosis) is the cause of pauci-immune glomerulonephritis associated with c-ANCA (PR3-ANCA), showing no significant Ig/C3 deposition on biopsy.","incorrectas":{"B":"Microscopic polyangiitis causes pauci-immune GN but is associated with p-ANCA, not c-ANCA.","C":"Churg-Strauss syndrome causes pauci-immune GN but is associated with p-ANCA, not c-ANCA.","D":"Goodpasture syndrome is an immune-complex (anti-GBM) mediated GN, not pauci-immune, and is not ANCA-related by definition."}}},{"id":"anking-immune-responses-134","fuente":"anking","tema":11,"anki":"1475376628335","q":"Which causes of pauci-immune glomerulonephritis are associated with p-ANCA (MPO-ANCA)?","o":{"A":"Microscopic polyangiitis and Churg-Strauss syndrome","B":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis and Goodpasture syndrome","C":"IgA nephropathy and lupus nephritis","D":"Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis and membranous nephropathy"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Microscopic polyangiitis and Churg-Strauss syndrome are the pauci-immune glomerulonephritides associated with p-ANCA (MPO-ANCA), both showing no Ig/C3 deposition on immunofluorescence.","incorrectas":{"B":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis is associated with c-ANCA, and Goodpasture syndrome is an immune-complex-mediated disease, not pauci-immune or ANCA-associated.","C":"IgA nephropathy and lupus nephritis are immune-complex-mediated glomerulonephritides, not pauci-immune.","D":"Post-streptococcal GN and membranous nephropathy are immune-complex-mediated, not pauci-immune ANCA-associated diseases."}}},{"id":"anking-immune-responses-135","fuente":"anking","tema":11,"anki":"1478994988572","q":"Decay accelerating factor (DAF/CD55) and membrane inhibitor of reactive lysis (MIRL/CD59) are anchored to the RBC membrane by which glycolipid anchor?","o":{"A":"GPI (glycosylphosphatidylinositol)","B":"Phosphatidylserine","C":"Sphingomyelin","D":"Cholesterol"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"DAF (CD55) and MIRL (CD59) are anchored to the cell membrane via a GPI (glycosylphosphatidylinositol) anchor, which tethers these complement-regulatory proteins to the outer membrane leaflet.","incorrectas":{"B":"Phosphatidylserine is a membrane phospholipid involved in apoptotic signaling, not a protein anchor.","C":"Sphingomyelin is a structural membrane lipid, not an anchoring molecule for these proteins.","D":"Cholesterol is a membrane stabilizing lipid but does not serve as the anchor for CD55/CD59."}}},{"id":"anking-immune-responses-136","fuente":"anking","tema":11,"anki":"1478994993913","q":"Absence of GPI anchors on blood cells (with resulting loss of DAF and MIRL) renders cells susceptible to destruction mediated by which system?","o":{"A":"Complement","B":"Cell-mediated cytotoxicity (NK cells)","C":"Antibody-dependent phagocytosis","D":"Oxidative stress from neutrophils"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Without GPI-anchored DAF and MIRL, blood cells lose protection against complement-mediated lysis, resulting in paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH).","incorrectas":{"B":"NK cell-mediated cytotoxicity is not the primary mechanism of cell destruction in PNH.","C":"Antibody-dependent phagocytosis is not the mechanism; PNH involves unchecked complement activation, not antibody-mediated clearance.","D":"Neutrophil oxidative burst is not the underlying mechanism of hemolysis in PNH."}}},{"id":"anking-immune-responses-137","fuente":"anking","tema":11,"anki":"1478995007169","q":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH) classically causes which type of anemia (based on red cell size)?","o":{"A":"Normocytic anemia","B":"Macrocytic anemia","C":"Microcytic anemia (as the primary/classic type)","D":"Anemia of chronic disease pattern only"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"PNH is classically a normocytic anemia due to complement-mediated intravascular hemolysis, although chronic iron loss can secondarily cause microcytosis.","incorrectas":{"B":"Macrocytic anemia is not the classic pattern seen in PNH; it is more typical of B12/folate deficiency.","C":"While iron deficiency (microcytic) can occur secondarily from chronic hemoglobinuria, the classic/primary anemia of PNH is normocytic.","D":"PNH is a hemolytic process, not simply anemia of chronic disease."}}},{"id":"anking-immune-responses-138","fuente":"anking","tema":11,"anki":"1478995010616","q":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria is primarily characterized by which type of hemolysis?","o":{"A":"Intravascular hemolysis","B":"Extravascular hemolysis","C":"Hemolysis confined to the spleen only","D":"Hemolysis mediated exclusively by IgE antibodies"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"PNH is primarily characterized by intravascular hemolysis due to unchecked complement-mediated lysis of RBCs lacking protective GPI-anchored proteins.","incorrectas":{"B":"Extravascular hemolysis (in spleen/liver macrophages) is characteristic of conditions like hereditary spherocytosis or warm AIHA, not the primary mechanism in PNH.","C":"Hemolysis in PNH occurs within the vasculature itself, not confined to splenic destruction.","D":"PNH hemolysis is complement-mediated, not IgE/mast cell mediated."}}},{"id":"anking-immune-responses-139","fuente":"anking","tema":11,"anki":"1478995013918","q":"The intravascular hemolysis of paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria occurs episodically, often during which activity/state?","o":{"A":"Sleeping","B":"Exercise","C":"Eating","D":"Sun exposure"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Hemolytic episodes in PNH often occur during sleep, likely due to mild respiratory acidosis during sleep that can activate the complement system.","incorrectas":{"B":"Exercise is not the classic trigger described for PNH hemolytic episodes.","C":"Eating is not associated with triggering complement activation in PNH.","D":"Sun exposure triggers hemolysis in conditions like erythropoietic protoporphyria, not PNH."}}},{"id":"anking-immune-responses-140","fuente":"anking","tema":11,"anki":"1478995020091","q":"Which test is classically used to screen for paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria?","o":{"A":"Sucrose test","B":"Coombs test (direct antiglobulin test)","C":"Osmotic fragility test","D":"Ham's acid hemolysis test as the initial screen only, never sucrose"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"The sucrose test is used to screen for PNH; sucrose promotes complement activation and binding to RBCs, causing hemolysis of complement-sensitive PNH cells in a low-ionic-strength solution.","incorrectas":{"B":"The Coombs test detects antibody-mediated hemolysis (autoimmune hemolytic anemia), not complement-mediated PNH.","C":"The osmotic fragility test is used to diagnose hereditary spherocytosis, not PNH.","D":"While the acidified serum (Ham) test is a confirmatory test for PNH, the sucrose test is the classic screening test described here."}}},{"id":"anking-immune-responses-141","fuente":"anking","tema":11,"anki":"1478995028457","q":"Patients with paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria can develop abdominal pain, dysphagia, and erectile dysfunction because free hemoglobin acts as a scavenger of which molecule?","o":{"A":"Nitric oxide","B":"Prostaglandin E2","C":"Serotonin","D":"Acetylcholine"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Free hemoglobin released during intravascular hemolysis scavenges nitric oxide, a smooth muscle relaxant, leading to smooth muscle dystonia manifesting as abdominal pain, dysphagia, and erectile dysfunction.","incorrectas":{"B":"Prostaglandin E2 is not the molecule scavenged by free hemoglobin in this mechanism.","C":"Serotonin scavenging is not the mechanism implicated in PNH smooth muscle symptoms.","D":"Acetylcholine is not scavenged by hemoglobin; it is a neurotransmitter unrelated to this mechanism."}}},{"id":"anking-immune-responses-142","fuente":"anking","tema":11,"anki":"1478995055944","q":"What is the main cause of death in patients with paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria?","o":{"A":"Venous thrombosis","B":"Sepsis from neutropenia","C":"Acute renal failure from hemoglobin cast nephropathy","D":"Hemorrhagic stroke"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Venous thrombosis (e.g., hepatic, portal, or cerebral vein thrombosis) is the leading cause of death in PNH, as destroyed platelets release procoagulant cytoplasmic contents into circulation.","incorrectas":{"B":"While cytopenias occur in PNH, sepsis from neutropenia is not the main cause of death.","C":"Renal impairment can occur from chronic hemoglobinuria but is not the primary cause of death.","D":"Hemorrhagic stroke is not the characteristic complication; thrombotic events are far more common in PNH."}}},{"id":"anking-immune-responses-143","fuente":"anking","tema":11,"anki":"1478995063743","q":"Which type of anemia, characterized by microcytosis, may develop as a complication of paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria?","o":{"A":"Iron deficiency anemia","B":"Thalassemia","C":"Sideroblastic anemia","D":"Anemia of chronic kidney disease"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Chronic loss of hemoglobin in the urine (hemoglobinuria) in PNH leads to iron loss over time, resulting in iron deficiency anemia, which is microcytic.","incorrectas":{"B":"Thalassemia is an inherited hemoglobinopathy unrelated to PNH's iron-loss mechanism.","C":"Sideroblastic anemia results from defective heme synthesis, not iron loss via hemoglobinuria.","D":"Anemia of chronic kidney disease results from reduced erythropoietin production, not iron loss."}}},{"id":"anking-renal-physiology-144","fuente":"anking","tema":12,"anki":"1462054352273","q":"Which hormone's secretion from the zona glomerulosa is under tonic, but not exclusive, control by ACTH?","o":{"A":"Aldosterone","B":"Cortisol","C":"Renin","D":"Angiotensin II"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Aldosterone secretion from the zona glomerulosa requires ACTH as an essential permissive first step, though its primary regulators are angiotensin II and potassium.","incorrectas":{"B":"Cortisol is secreted from the zona fasciculata and is under direct, primary (not just tonic) ACTH control.","C":"Renin is produced by the juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney, not the adrenal cortex.","D":"Angiotensin II is a circulating peptide hormone, not a secretory product of the zona glomerulosa."}}},{"id":"anking-renal-physiology-145","fuente":"anking","tema":12,"anki":"1462054842563","q":"Which hormone increases renal Na+ reabsorption, thereby restoring ECF volume and blood volume to normal?","o":{"A":"Aldosterone","B":"Atrial natriuretic peptide","C":"Cortisol","D":"Erythropoietin"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Aldosterone acts on the distal tubule and collecting duct to increase Na+ (and secondarily water) reabsorption, restoring ECF and blood volume.","incorrectas":{"B":"ANP promotes natriuresis and volume loss, the opposite effect of aldosterone.","C":"Cortisol has some mineralocorticoid activity but is not the primary regulator of Na+ reabsorption for volume homeostasis.","D":"Erythropoietin stimulates red blood cell production and has no direct role in Na+ reabsorption."}}},{"id":"anking-renal-physiology-146","fuente":"anking","tema":12,"anki":"1462054857059","q":"To which receptor does angiotensin II bind to exert its main physiologic effects?","o":{"A":"AT1 receptor","B":"AT2 receptor","C":"V1 receptor","D":"V2 receptor"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Angiotensin II binds the AT1 receptor, mediating vasoconstriction, aldosterone secretion, and increased Na+ reabsorption.","incorrectas":{"B":"AT2 receptors generally oppose AT1 effects (vasodilation, antiproliferative) and are not the primary mediator of angiotensin II's classic actions.","C":"V1 receptors are targets of vasopressin (ADH), not angiotensin II.","D":"V2 receptors are also ADH receptors, located on the collecting duct, unrelated to angiotensin II."}}},{"id":"anking-renal-physiology-147","fuente":"anking","tema":12,"anki":"1462054869905","q":"Through which secondary messenger system does the angiotensin II-bound AT1 receptor propagate its signal?","o":{"A":"IP3/Ca2+","B":"cAMP","C":"cGMP","D":"Tyrosine kinase"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"The AT1 receptor is Gq-coupled, activating phospholipase C to generate IP3 and increase intracellular Ca2+.","incorrectas":{"B":"cAMP is the second messenger for Gs-coupled receptors such as the V2 receptor for ADH, not AT1.","C":"cGMP is used by natriuretic peptide and nitric oxide signaling pathways, not AT1.","D":"Tyrosine kinase signaling is characteristic of receptors like the insulin receptor, not AT1, which is a GPCR."}}},{"id":"anking-renal-physiology-148","fuente":"anking","tema":12,"anki":"1462054872908","q":"How does hyperkalemia influence aldosterone secretion?","o":{"A":"Increased aldosterone secretion","B":"Decreased aldosterone secretion","C":"No effect on aldosterone secretion","D":"Increased renin secretion but no change in aldosterone"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Increased K+ depolarizes zona glomerulosa cells, causing Ca2+ influx that stimulates aldosterone synthesis, similarly to angiotensin II signaling.","incorrectas":{"B":"Hyperkalemia stimulates, rather than suppresses, aldosterone release as a compensatory mechanism to excrete excess potassium.","C":"Potassium is one of the primary physiologic regulators of aldosterone, so it clearly affects its secretion.","D":"Hyperkalemia acts directly on the adrenal zona glomerulosa; it does not require renin release to increase aldosterone."}}},{"id":"anking-renal-physiology-149","fuente":"anking","tema":12,"anki":"1462381603797","q":"Aldosterone increases reabsorption of which ion (along with H2O) at the distal tubules and collecting duct?","o":{"A":"Na+","B":"K+","C":"Ca2+","D":"PO43-"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Aldosterone upregulates Na+ channels (ENaC) and the Na+/K+-ATPase in principal cells, increasing Na+ (and water) reabsorption, which can cause hypertension.","incorrectas":{"B":"Aldosterone promotes K+ secretion (loss), not reabsorption.","C":"Ca2+ reabsorption is primarily regulated by PTH and vitamin D, not aldosterone.","D":"Phosphate handling is mainly controlled by PTH and FGF23, not aldosterone."}}},{"id":"anking-renal-physiology-150","fuente":"anking","tema":12,"anki":"1471456202579","q":"Which system is a slow, hormonally mediated mechanism for regulating arterial pressure?","o":{"A":"Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","B":"Baroreceptor reflex","C":"Chemoreceptor reflex","D":"Cushing reflex"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"The RAAS is a slow-acting, hormone-based system (renin, angiotensin II, aldosterone) that regulates arterial pressure primarily via blood volume changes over hours to days.","incorrectas":{"B":"The baroreceptor reflex is a fast, neurally mediated mechanism acting within seconds, not a slow hormonal one.","C":"Chemoreceptors primarily respond to changes in blood gases and pH, providing rapid neural regulation, not slow hormonal control of pressure.","D":"The Cushing reflex is an acute response to rising intracranial pressure, not a slow hormonal blood pressure regulator."}}},{"id":"anking-renal-physiology-151","fuente":"anking","tema":12,"anki":"1471457755859","q":"The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system regulates mean arterial pressure primarily by adjusting what parameter?","o":{"A":"Blood volume","B":"Heart rate","C":"Vascular compliance","D":"Cardiac contractility"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"RAAS chiefly regulates arterial pressure by modulating renal Na+ and water handling, thereby adjusting circulating blood volume.","incorrectas":{"B":"Heart rate is primarily regulated by the autonomic nervous system, not RAAS.","C":"Vascular compliance is a structural/passive vessel property not directly set by RAAS.","D":"Cardiac contractility is modulated mainly by sympathetic tone and catecholamines, not primarily by RAAS."}}},{"id":"anking-renal-physiology-152","fuente":"anking","tema":12,"anki":"1471457759434","q":"The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system is activated in response to what change in mean arterial pressure?","o":{"A":"A decrease","B":"An increase","C":"No change, only changes in serum osmolarity","D":"Only changes in serum potassium"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"A decrease in mean arterial pressure (sensed by the juxtaglomerular apparatus) triggers renin release, activating the RAAS cascade to restore pressure.","incorrectas":{"B":"An increase in arterial pressure would suppress, not activate, renin release.","C":"While osmolarity changes affect ADH release, RAAS activation is primarily driven by pressure/volume changes, not osmolarity alone.","D":"Potassium directly affects aldosterone at the adrenal gland, but RAAS activation as a whole is triggered by decreased arterial pressure."}}},{"id":"anking-renal-physiology-153","fuente":"anking","tema":12,"anki":"1471457767523","q":"Which enzyme catalyzes the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II?","o":{"A":"Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE)","B":"Renin","C":"Chymase","D":"Aldosterone synthase"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"ACE cleaves angiotensin I to form the active peptide angiotensin II, primarily in the pulmonary vasculature.","incorrectas":{"B":"Renin converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, not angiotensin I to angiotensin II.","C":"Chymase is an alternative (ACE-independent) enzyme that can generate angiotensin II but is not the primary/classic enzyme referenced here.","D":"Aldosterone synthase catalyzes the final steps of aldosterone synthesis in the adrenal cortex, unrelated to angiotensin conversion."}}},{"id":"anking-renal-physiology-154","fuente":"anking","tema":12,"anki":"1471457770358","q":"In which organ does ACE primarily catalyze the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II?","o":{"A":"Lungs","B":"Kidneys","C":"Liver","D":"Adrenal glands"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"The pulmonary vascular endothelium has abundant ACE, making the lungs the primary site of angiotensin II generation.","incorrectas":{"B":"The kidneys are the primary source of renin, not the main site of ACE-mediated conversion.","C":"The liver produces angiotensinogen, the precursor protein, not the site of ACE activity.","D":"The adrenal glands are the target organ for angiotensin II (stimulating aldosterone), not the site of its formation."}}},{"id":"anking-renal-physiology-155","fuente":"anking","tema":12,"anki":"1471457773070","q":"Angiotensin II stimulates the synthesis and secretion of which hormone by the adrenal cortex?","o":{"A":"Aldosterone","B":"Cortisol","C":"DHEA","D":"Epinephrine"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Angiotensin II acts on the zona glomerulosa to stimulate aldosterone synthesis and secretion, increasing Na+ reabsorption.","incorrectas":{"B":"Cortisol secretion from the zona fasciculata is primarily driven by ACTH, not angiotensin II.","C":"DHEA is produced in the zona reticularis under ACTH control, not primarily by angiotensin II.","D":"Epinephrine is produced by the adrenal medulla in response to sympathetic stimulation, unrelated to angiotensin II."}}},{"id":"anking-renal-physiology-156","fuente":"anking","tema":12,"anki":"1471457792149","q":"Angiotensin II increases which exchange process in the proximal convoluted tubule?","o":{"A":"Na+-H+ exchange","B":"K+-H+ exchange","C":"Cl−-HCO3− exchange","D":"Na+-Ca2+ exchange"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Angiotensin II directly stimulates the Na+-H+ exchanger in the proximal tubule, increasing Na+ reabsorption independent of its aldosterone-mediated effects.","incorrectas":{"B":"K+-H+ exchange is not a major angiotensin II target in the proximal tubule.","C":"Cl−-HCO3− exchange is involved in acid-base handling in other nephron segments, not the primary angiotensin II effect described.","D":"Na+-Ca2+ exchange is not the mechanism by which angiotensin II increases proximal tubular Na+ reabsorption."}}},{"id":"anking-renal-physiology-157","fuente":"anking","tema":12,"anki":"1471457806709","q":"Angiotensin II increases which sensation via its action on the hypothalamus?","o":{"A":"Thirst","B":"Hunger","C":"Satiety","D":"Sleepiness"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Angiotensin II acts on the hypothalamus (via the organum vasculosum) to stimulate thirst, promoting water intake to help restore blood volume.","incorrectas":{"B":"Hunger regulation involves hypothalamic centers responsive to leptin and ghrelin, not primarily angiotensin II.","C":"Satiety is regulated by different hypothalamic signals (e.g., leptin, POMC neurons), not angiotensin II.","D":"Angiotensin II is not known to promote sleepiness; its hypothalamic effect is on thirst."}}},{"id":"anking-renal-physiology-158","fuente":"anking","tema":12,"anki":"1471457814120","q":"What effect does angiotensin II have on arterioles?","o":{"A":"Constriction","B":"Dilation","C":"No effect","D":"Selective venodilation only"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Angiotensin II is a potent vasoconstrictor of arterioles, increasing total peripheral resistance and arterial pressure.","incorrectas":{"B":"Dilation is the opposite of angiotensin II's known vasoconstrictive effect.","C":"Angiotensin II has a well-established, potent hemodynamic effect on arterioles, not a neutral one.","D":"Angiotensin II acts on arterioles causing constriction, not selectively on veins causing dilation."}}},{"id":"anking-renal-physiology-159","fuente":"anking","tema":12,"anki":"1473385338969","q":"Decreased O2 delivery to the kidneys due to hypoxia causes increased production of which factor, which then stimulates EPO synthesis?","o":{"A":"Hypoxia-inducible factor 1α (HIF-1α)","B":"Angiotensin II","C":"Vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF)","D":"Renin"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Renal hypoxia stabilizes HIF-1α in peritubular interstitial fibroblasts, which then drives transcription of the erythropoietin gene.","incorrectas":{"B":"Angiotensin II is part of the RAAS and is not the transcription factor responsible for EPO induction under hypoxia.","C":"VEGF is a downstream target of HIF signaling important in angiogenesis, but it is not what stimulates EPO synthesis in this pathway.","D":"Renin initiates the RAAS cascade for blood pressure regulation, unrelated to hypoxia-driven EPO production."}}},{"id":"anking-renal-physiology-160","fuente":"anking","tema":12,"anki":"1473385372338","q":"Erythropoietin (EPO) causes differentiation of which cell type, which undergoes further development into mature erythrocytes?","o":{"A":"Proerythroblasts","B":"Megakaryocytes","C":"Myeloblasts","D":"Reticulocytes"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"EPO binds receptors on early erythroid progenitors, promoting their differentiation into proerythroblasts, which mature further into erythrocytes.","incorrectas":{"B":"Megakaryocytes are the precursors to platelets, regulated by thrombopoietin, not EPO.","C":"Myeloblasts are precursors of the granulocyte lineage, not the erythroid lineage stimulated by EPO.","D":"Reticulocytes are already a later maturation stage of the erythroid line, downstream of proerythroblasts, not the cell EPO first differentiates."}}},{"id":"anking-renal-physiology-161","fuente":"anking","tema":12,"anki":"1473957534401","q":"Aldosterone increases secretion of which two ions at the distal tubules and collecting duct?","o":{"A":"K+ and H+","B":"Na+ and Ca2+","C":"Cl− and HCO3−","D":"Mg2+ and PO43-"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Aldosterone stimulates principal cells to secrete K+ and intercalated cells to secrete H+, while promoting Na+ reabsorption.","incorrectas":{"B":"Aldosterone promotes Na+ reabsorption (not secretion), and it does not directly regulate Ca2+ secretion.","C":"Cl− and HCO3− handling in the collecting duct is not the primary ion secretion effect attributed to aldosterone.","D":"Mg2+ and PO43- secretion are regulated by other hormonal systems (e.g., PTH, FGF23), not aldosterone."}}},{"id":"anking-metabolism-162","fuente":"anking","tema":13,"anki":"1464371990594","q":"Which enzyme metabolizes sorbitol into fructose?","o":{"A":"Sorbitol dehydrogenase","B":"Aldose reductase","C":"Fructokinase","D":"Hexokinase"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Sorbitol dehydrogenase converts sorbitol to fructose; tissues lacking this enzyme (e.g., Schwann cells, lens, retina) are prone to osmotic damage when sorbitol accumulates, as in diabetes.","incorrectas":{"B":"Aldose reductase converts glucose to sorbitol, the opposite reaction.","C":"Fructokinase phosphorylates fructose to fructose-1-phosphate, acting downstream of sorbitol dehydrogenase.","D":"Hexokinase phosphorylates glucose to glucose-6-phosphate and is unrelated to sorbitol metabolism."}}},{"id":"anking-metabolism-163","fuente":"anking","tema":13,"anki":"1472423439563","q":"Which stereoisomeric form of amino acids is found in mammalian proteins?","o":{"A":"D-amino acids","B":"L-amino acids","C":"Racemic mixtures","D":"Meso forms"},"r":"B","e":{"correcta":"Only L-amino acids are incorporated into proteins by ribosomes; D-amino acids occur in bacterial cell walls but not in human proteins.","incorrectas":{"A":"D-amino acids are found in bacterial peptidoglycan, not in human proteins.","C":"Racemic mixtures contain equal D and L forms, which does not describe protein synthesis.","D":"Meso compounds are achiral molecules with internal symmetry, not a relevant category here."}}},{"id":"anking-metabolism-164","fuente":"anking","tema":13,"anki":"1472426358537","q":"Which amino acids are the branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs)?","o":{"A":"Valine, Leucine, Isoleucine","B":"Alanine, Glycine, Serine","C":"Lysine, Arginine, Histidine","D":"Phenylalanine, Tyrosine, Tryptophan"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Valine, leucine, and isoleucine are the branched-chain amino acids, all essential and metabolized primarily in skeletal muscle.","incorrectas":{"B":"Alanine, glycine, and serine have small, unbranched side chains.","C":"Lysine, arginine, and histidine are basic amino acids, not branched-chain.","D":"Phenylalanine, tyrosine, and tryptophan are aromatic amino acids, not branched-chain."}}},{"id":"anking-metabolism-165","fuente":"anking","tema":13,"anki":"1472426367341","q":"Which amino acids serve as precursors to catecholamines and melanin?","o":{"A":"Tyrosine, Phenylalanine","B":"Tryptophan, Histidine","C":"Glutamate, Aspartate","D":"Lysine, Arginine"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Phenylalanine is converted to tyrosine, which is the precursor for dopamine, norepinephrine, epinephrine, and melanin.","incorrectas":{"B":"Tryptophan gives rise to serotonin and niacin, not catecholamines or melanin; histidine gives rise to histamine.","C":"Glutamate and aspartate are involved in neurotransmission and the urea cycle, not catecholamine or melanin synthesis.","D":"Lysine and arginine are basic amino acids involved in collagen crosslinking and the urea cycle/nitric oxide synthesis, not catecholamine synthesis."}}},{"id":"anking-metabolism-166","fuente":"anking","tema":13,"anki":"1472426371617","q":"Which amino acid is the precursor to serotonin and niacin?","o":{"A":"Tryptophan","B":"Tyrosine","C":"Histidine","D":"Glycine"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Tryptophan is converted to serotonin (and subsequently melatonin) and can also be used to synthesize niacin (vitamin B3); this pathway requires BH4.","incorrectas":{"B":"Tyrosine is the precursor to catecholamines and melanin, not serotonin or niacin.","C":"Histidine is the precursor to histamine, not serotonin or niacin.","D":"Glycine is used in heme, purine, and creatine synthesis, not serotonin or niacin production."}}},{"id":"anking-metabolism-167","fuente":"anking","tema":13,"anki":"1472426375356","q":"Which amino acid is the precursor to histamine?","o":{"A":"Histidine","B":"Tryptophan","C":"Tyrosine","D":"Arginine"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Histidine is decarboxylated by histidine decarboxylase to form histamine; this enzyme remains active at room temperature, contributing to scombroid poisoning in improperly stored fish.","incorrectas":{"B":"Tryptophan produces serotonin and niacin, not histamine.","C":"Tyrosine produces catecholamines and melanin, not histamine.","D":"Arginine is a substrate for nitric oxide and urea cycle intermediates, not histamine."}}},{"id":"anking-metabolism-168","fuente":"anking","tema":13,"anki":"1472426379400","q":"Which two amino acids are negatively charged (acidic) at body pH?","o":{"A":"Aspartate and Glutamate","B":"Lysine and Arginine","C":"Serine and Threonine","D":"Cysteine and Methionine"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Aspartate and glutamate have acidic side chains with carboxylic acid groups that are deprotonated and negatively charged at physiologic pH.","incorrectas":{"B":"Lysine and arginine are basic amino acids that carry a positive charge at body pH.","C":"Serine and threonine are neutral, hydroxyl-containing amino acids and targets of kinase phosphorylation.","D":"Cysteine and methionine are neutral, sulfur-containing amino acids."}}},{"id":"anking-metabolism-169","fuente":"anking","tema":13,"anki":"1472426383267","q":"Which two amino acids are positively charged (basic) at body pH?","o":{"A":"Lysine and Arginine","B":"Aspartate and Glutamate","C":"Valine and Leucine","D":"Serine and Threonine"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Lysine and arginine have basic side chains that are protonated and positively charged at physiologic pH, with arginine being the most basic amino acid.","incorrectas":{"B":"Aspartate and glutamate are acidic amino acids, negatively charged at body pH.","C":"Valine and leucine are branched-chain, nonpolar amino acids with no charge at body pH.","D":"Serine and threonine are neutral, hydroxyl-containing amino acids."}}},{"id":"anking-metabolism-170","fuente":"anking","tema":13,"anki":"1472426393413","q":"What is the charge of the amino acid histidine at body pH?","o":{"A":"No charge","B":"Strongly negative","C":"Strongly positive","D":"Zwitterionic with net negative charge"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Histidine's imidazole side chain has a pKa near physiologic pH, so it is largely uncharged at body pH, though it is still classified as a basic amino acid.","incorrectas":{"B":"Histidine's side chain is basic, not acidic, so it does not carry a strongly negative charge.","C":"At body pH histidine's imidazole ring is mostly deprotonated/neutral rather than strongly positive, unlike arginine or lysine.","D":"While all amino acids exist as zwitterions in solution, histidine's side chain itself is essentially uncharged at body pH, not net negative."}}},{"id":"anking-metabolism-171","fuente":"anking","tema":13,"anki":"1472426400291","q":"Which amino acids are the target residues phosphorylated by protein kinase A and protein kinase C?","o":{"A":"Serine, Threonine","B":"Tyrosine, Histidine","C":"Lysine, Arginine","D":"Cysteine, Methionine"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Protein kinase A and protein kinase C are serine/threonine kinases that phosphorylate serine and threonine residues on target proteins.","incorrectas":{"B":"Tyrosine is phosphorylated by tyrosine kinases (e.g., insulin receptor), not PKA/PKC; histidine is not a typical kinase target in mammalian signaling.","C":"Lysine and arginine are basic amino acids not phosphorylated by these kinases.","D":"Cysteine and methionine are sulfur-containing amino acids not targeted by PKA/PKC phosphorylation."}}},{"id":"anking-metabolism-172","fuente":"anking","tema":13,"anki":"1472426404020","q":"Which amino acids contain sulfur in their side chains?","o":{"A":"Cysteine, Methionine","B":"Serine, Threonine","C":"Aspartate, Glutamate","D":"Valine, Isoleucine"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Cysteine and methionine both contain sulfur atoms in their side chains; cysteine's thiol group also allows disulfide bond formation.","incorrectas":{"B":"Serine and threonine contain hydroxyl groups, not sulfur.","C":"Aspartate and glutamate are acidic amino acids with carboxylic acid side chains, no sulfur.","D":"Valine and isoleucine are branched-chain nonpolar amino acids without sulfur."}}},{"id":"anking-metabolism-173","fuente":"anking","tema":13,"anki":"1472426408050","q":"Which amino acids are considered essential/required during periods of rapid growth (e.g., in children)?","o":{"A":"Arginine, Histidine","B":"Alanine, Glutamine","C":"Tyrosine, Cysteine","D":"Glycine, Serine"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Arginine and histidine are generally classified as non-essential in adults but become essential during periods of rapid growth, such as childhood, due to increased demand.","incorrectas":{"B":"Alanine and glutamine are non-essential amino acids not classically singled out for growth-related essentiality.","C":"Tyrosine and cysteine are conditionally non-essential, synthesized from phenylalanine and methionine respectively, but not specifically tied to growth requirements.","D":"Glycine and serine are non-essential amino acids readily synthesized by the body at all ages."}}},{"id":"anking-metabolism-174","fuente":"anking","tema":13,"anki":"1472426420971","q":"Which amino acids are purely ketogenic (not glucogenic)?","o":{"A":"Leucine, Lysine","B":"Valine, Methionine","C":"Alanine, Glycine","D":"Aspartate, Glutamate"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Leucine and lysine are the only two amino acids that are purely ketogenic, being degraded solely into acetyl-CoA/acetoacetate without contributing to gluconeogenesis.","incorrectas":{"B":"Valine and methionine are glucogenic amino acids, converted to succinyl-CoA.","C":"Alanine and glycine are glucogenic, feeding into pyruvate for gluconeogenesis.","D":"Aspartate and glutamate are glucogenic, feeding into oxaloacetate and alpha-ketoglutarate respectively."}}},{"id":"anking-metabolism-175","fuente":"anking","tema":13,"anki":"1472426425105","q":"Are the essential amino acids methionine, valine, and histidine ketogenic or glucogenic?","o":{"A":"Glucogenic","B":"Ketogenic","C":"Both ketogenic and glucogenic","D":"Neither (nitrogen-only metabolism)"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Methionine, valine, and histidine are purely glucogenic amino acids, their carbon skeletons being converted into intermediates that can enter gluconeogenesis.","incorrectas":{"B":"Purely ketogenic amino acids are only leucine and lysine, not this group.","C":"Amino acids that are both ketogenic and glucogenic include phenylalanine, tyrosine, tryptophan, isoleucine, and threonine, not this trio.","D":"All amino acids undergo carbon skeleton metabolism beyond just nitrogen handling; these three do contribute carbon to glucose synthesis."}}},{"id":"anking-metabolism-176","fuente":"anking","tema":13,"anki":"1472426435478","q":"Are the amino acids phenylalanine, isoleucine, threonine, tryptophan, and tyrosine ketogenic, glucogenic, or both?","o":{"A":"Both ketogenic and glucogenic","B":"Purely ketogenic","C":"Purely glucogenic","D":"Neither, they are only used for protein synthesis"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Phenylalanine, isoleucine, threonine, tryptophan, and tyrosine are classified as both ketogenic and glucogenic since their metabolism yields intermediates usable for both ketone body and glucose synthesis.","incorrectas":{"B":"Purely ketogenic amino acids are limited to leucine and lysine, not this group.","C":"Purely glucogenic amino acids include methionine, valine, and histidine, distinct from this group.","D":"These amino acids are catabolized for energy and metabolic intermediates in addition to their role in protein synthesis."}}},{"id":"anking-metabolism-177","fuente":"anking","tema":13,"anki":"1472435618038","q":"What happens to the insulin receptor upon insulin binding that leads to activation of its tyrosine kinase activity?","o":{"A":"It dimerizes","B":"It undergoes proteolytic cleavage","C":"It is internalized into a coated vesicle immediately","D":"It dissociates into monomeric subunits"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Insulin binding causes the insulin receptor to dimerize (or its pre-existing dimer to undergo conformational change), triggering autophosphorylation and activation of its intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity.","incorrectas":{"B":"Proteolytic cleavage is not the mechanism of insulin receptor activation.","C":"Receptor internalization occurs later for signal termination/recycling, not as the initial activation step.","D":"The insulin receptor is normally a disulfide-linked dimer (alpha2-beta2) already; activation involves conformational change and autophosphorylation, not dissociation into monomers."}}},{"id":"anking-metabolism-178","fuente":"anking","tema":13,"anki":"1472435632014","q":"Following binding of insulin receptor substrate 1 (IRS-1) to the insulin receptor, proteins with which domain bind to IRS-1?","o":{"A":"SH2 domain","B":"PDZ domain","C":"Death domain","D":"Bromodomain"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"SH2 (Src homology 2) domain-containing proteins recognize phosphotyrosine residues on IRS-1, allowing them to bind and propagate the insulin signaling cascade.","incorrectas":{"B":"PDZ domains mediate protein-protein interactions in scaffolding complexes, typically at cell junctions, not in IRS-1 signaling.","C":"Death domains are involved in apoptotic signaling complexes (e.g., Fas/TNF receptor signaling), not insulin signaling.","D":"Bromodomains recognize acetylated lysines in chromatin-associated proteins, unrelated to IRS-1 signaling."}}},{"id":"anking-metabolism-179","fuente":"anking","tema":13,"anki":"1472435639812","q":"Which SH2-domain protein, when activated by binding IRS-1, results in translocation of GLUT-4 to the membrane in adipose and muscle tissue?","o":{"A":"PI3 kinase","B":"Protein kinase A","C":"MAP kinase","D":"Adenylate cyclase"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"PI3 kinase (phosphoinositide 3-kinase) is recruited via its SH2 domains to phosphorylated IRS-1, activating downstream signaling (via Akt) that drives GLUT-4 translocation to the plasma membrane in muscle and adipose tissue.","incorrectas":{"B":"Protein kinase A is primarily activated by cAMP-dependent pathways (e.g., glucagon signaling), not by IRS-1 binding.","C":"MAP kinase is involved in the mitogenic/growth effects of insulin signaling, not primarily GLUT-4 translocation.","D":"Adenylate cyclase generates cAMP in G-protein coupled receptor signaling and is not part of the insulin receptor SH2-domain cascade."}}},{"id":"anking-neoplasia-180","fuente":"anking","tema":14,"anki":"1462229092555","q":"Detection of a germline RET oncogene mutation (MEN 2A/2B) warrants which prophylactic procedure?","o":{"A":"Thyroidectomy","B":"Adrenalectomy","C":"Parathyroidectomy","D":"Pancreatectomy"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"A germline RET mutation confers a very high lifetime risk of medullary thyroid carcinoma, so prophylactic thyroidectomy is recommended in these patients.","incorrectas":{"B":"Adrenalectomy is performed for pheochromocytoma (also seen in MEN2) once diagnosed, not as prophylaxis for the RET mutation itself.","C":"Parathyroidectomy addresses hyperparathyroidism (MEN2A) but is not the prophylactic procedure indicated by RET mutation detection.","D":"Pancreatectomy is not associated with RET mutations; it relates to MEN1 pancreatic tumors instead."}}},{"id":"anking-neoplasia-181","fuente":"anking","tema":14,"anki":"1462818332334","q":"Amplification/overexpression of which oncogene is classically associated with neuroblastoma?","o":{"A":"N-myc","B":"C-myc","C":"L-myc","D":"HER2/neu"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"N-myc amplification is the classic oncogenic driver in neuroblastoma and correlates with poor prognosis.","incorrectas":{"B":"C-myc is associated with Burkitt lymphoma, not neuroblastoma.","C":"L-myc is associated with small cell lung carcinoma, not neuroblastoma.","D":"HER2/neu amplification is associated with breast and gastric adenocarcinoma, not neuroblastoma."}}},{"id":"anking-neoplasia-182","fuente":"anking","tema":14,"anki":"1463106007081","q":"HPV infection is an established risk factor for which subtype(s) of cervical carcinoma?","o":{"A":"Both squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma","B":"Only squamous cell carcinoma","C":"Only adenocarcinoma","D":"Neither subtype (HPV-independent for both)"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"HPV (especially high-risk types 16 and 18) is associated with both squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma of the cervix, making it the driving etiologic agent for cervical cancer overall.","incorrectas":{"B":"This is incomplete since adenocarcinoma is also HPV-associated (particularly type 18).","C":"This is incomplete since squamous cell carcinoma, the more common subtype, is also strongly HPV-associated.","D":"HPV is the major driver of cervical carcinogenesis for both histologic subtypes, not unrelated to either."}}},{"id":"anking-neoplasia-183","fuente":"anking","tema":14,"anki":"1463172510273","q":"Which histological finding is classically seen in serous papillary endometrial carcinoma (the sporadic, non-estrogen-driven pathway)?","o":{"A":"Psammoma bodies","B":"Reinke crystals","C":"Call-Exner bodies","D":"Auer rods"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Serous papillary endometrial carcinoma often shows psammoma bodies, which are concentric, laminated calcified structures seen on histology.","incorrectas":{"B":"Reinke crystals are found in Leydig cell tumors of the testis, not endometrial carcinoma.","C":"Call-Exner bodies are characteristic of granulosa cell tumors of the ovary.","D":"Auer rods are seen in acute myeloid leukemia blasts, unrelated to endometrial carcinoma."}}},{"id":"anking-neoplasia-184","fuente":"anking","tema":14,"anki":"1473536796723","q":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is strongly associated with infection by which virus?","o":{"A":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)","B":"Human papillomavirus (HPV)","C":"Hepatitis B virus (HBV)","D":"Human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"EBV is strongly linked to nasopharyngeal carcinoma, particularly in populations of Southern Chinese and African descent, and to Burkitt lymphoma.","incorrectas":{"B":"HPV is associated with cervical, oropharyngeal, and anal carcinomas, not nasopharyngeal carcinoma.","C":"HBV is associated with hepatocellular carcinoma, not nasopharyngeal carcinoma.","D":"HHV-8 is associated with Kaposi sarcoma, not nasopharyngeal carcinoma."}}},{"id":"anking-neoplasia-185","fuente":"anking","tema":14,"anki":"1473638489682","q":"Berylliosis results from prolonged occupational exposure to which agent?","o":{"A":"Beryllium","B":"Asbestos","C":"Silica","D":"Coal dust"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Berylliosis is a granulomatous lung disease caused by chronic exposure to beryllium, seen in workers in aerospace and electronics manufacturing.","incorrectas":{"B":"Asbestos exposure causes asbestosis and mesothelioma, a distinct entity from berylliosis.","C":"Silica exposure causes silicosis, a different pneumoconiosis.","D":"Coal dust exposure causes coal workers' pneumoconiosis, not berylliosis."}}},{"id":"anking-neoplasia-186","fuente":"anking","tema":14,"anki":"1473732886065","q":"Which radioactive gas is formed by the decay of uranium present in soil?","o":{"A":"Radon","B":"Thoron","C":"Argon","D":"Krypton"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Radon is a radioactive noble gas produced by the decay chain of uranium naturally present in soil and rock.","incorrectas":{"B":"Thoron is a decay product of thorium, a related but distinct decay series from uranium.","C":"Argon is an inert atmospheric gas not produced by uranium decay.","D":"Krypton is an inert atmospheric gas unrelated to uranium decay chains."}}},{"id":"anking-neoplasia-187","fuente":"anking","tema":14,"anki":"1473732889878","q":"Radon gas tends to accumulate in which type of location?","o":{"A":"Basements","B":"Rooftops","C":"Open fields","D":"High-altitude offices"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Being denser than air and seeping up from soil, radon accumulates in enclosed, low-lying spaces such as basements.","incorrectas":{"B":"Rooftops are open, elevated spaces where radon would readily disperse rather than accumulate.","C":"Open fields lack the enclosure needed for radon to concentrate.","D":"High-altitude offices are far from the soil source and well-ventilated, unlike basements."}}},{"id":"anking-neoplasia-188","fuente":"anking","tema":14,"anki":"1473732893483","q":"Which source is responsible for most public exposure to ionizing radiation?","o":{"A":"Radon","B":"Medical X-rays","C":"Nuclear power plants","D":"Cosmic radiation"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Radon gas from natural uranium decay in soil is the largest contributor to background ionizing radiation exposure in the general population.","incorrectas":{"B":"Medical X-rays contribute significantly but are not the largest source of public radiation exposure overall.","C":"Nuclear power plants contribute a very small fraction of public radiation exposure under normal operation.","D":"Cosmic radiation contributes to background exposure but is less than radon's contribution."}}},{"id":"anking-neoplasia-189","fuente":"anking","tema":14,"anki":"1473732897393","q":"Exposure to which agent is the second most frequent cause of lung carcinoma in the United States?","o":{"A":"Radon","B":"Asbestos","C":"Secondhand smoke","D":"Air pollution"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Radon exposure is the second leading cause of lung cancer in the U.S. after cigarette smoking.","incorrectas":{"B":"Asbestos increases lung cancer risk, especially synergistically with smoking, but is not the second most frequent cause overall.","C":"Secondhand smoke is a risk factor for lung cancer but ranks below radon as a cause.","D":"General air pollution contributes to lung cancer risk but is not the second most frequent cause in the U.S."}}},{"id":"anking-neoplasia-190","fuente":"anking","tema":14,"anki":"1473732900803","q":"Increased risk of lung cancer due to radon exposure is particularly notable in which occupational group?","o":{"A":"Uranium miners","B":"Coal miners","C":"Shipyard workers","D":"Farmers"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Uranium miners have markedly increased radon exposure underground, leading to elevated lung cancer risk.","incorrectas":{"B":"Coal miners are at risk for coal workers' pneumoconiosis, not primarily radon-related lung cancer.","C":"Shipyard workers have classically elevated asbestos exposure, not radon.","D":"Farmers are not a classically described high-radon-exposure occupational group."}}},{"id":"anking-neoplasia-191","fuente":"anking","tema":14,"anki":"1473733443878","q":"Which histological finding may be associated with mesothelioma?","o":{"A":"Psammoma bodies","B":"Councilman bodies","C":"Mallory bodies","D":"Negri bodies"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Mesothelioma, especially the papillary subtype, may show psammoma bodies (laminated calcific concretions) on histology.","incorrectas":{"B":"Councilman bodies are apoptotic hepatocytes seen in viral hepatitis and yellow fever, unrelated to mesothelioma.","C":"Mallory bodies are seen in alcoholic liver disease, not mesothelioma.","D":"Negri bodies are cytoplasmic inclusions seen in rabies infection, unrelated to mesothelioma."}}},{"id":"anking-neoplasia-192","fuente":"anking","tema":14,"anki":"1475462985903","q":"Which renal neoplasm is classically associated with paraneoplastic syndromes?","o":{"A":"Renal cell carcinoma","B":"Wilms tumor","C":"Renal oncocytoma","D":"Angiomyolipoma"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Renal cell carcinoma is well known for producing paraneoplastic syndromes through ectopic hormone secretion, including polycythemia, hypercalcemia, and Stauffer syndrome.","incorrectas":{"B":"Wilms tumor is a pediatric renal tumor not classically known for paraneoplastic hormone syndromes.","C":"Renal oncocytoma is a benign tumor not associated with paraneoplastic syndromes.","D":"Angiomyolipoma is a benign renal tumor associated with tuberous sclerosis, not paraneoplastic syndromes."}}},{"id":"anking-neoplasia-193","fuente":"anking","tema":14,"anki":"1475462988602","q":"Renal cell carcinoma may present with which paraneoplastic hematologic finding due to ectopic EPO production?","o":{"A":"Secondary polycythemia","B":"Anemia of chronic disease","C":"Thrombocytopenia","D":"Neutropenia"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Renal cell carcinoma can ectopically secrete erythropoietin, causing secondary polycythemia (elevated red cell mass).","incorrectas":{"B":"Anemia of chronic disease can occur in malignancy generally but is not the classic EPO-driven paraneoplastic finding here.","C":"Thrombocytopenia is not the classic paraneoplastic effect of ectopic EPO; EPO raises red cell counts, not lowers platelets.","D":"Neutropenia is unrelated to ectopic EPO secretion in renal cell carcinoma."}}},{"id":"anking-neoplasia-194","fuente":"anking","tema":14,"anki":"1475463024437","q":"Loss of the VHL tumor suppressor gene in renal cell carcinoma leads to increased levels of which growth-promoting factor?","o":{"A":"IGF-1","B":"TGF-beta","C":"EGFR","D":"FGF23"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"VHL loss stabilizes HIF, which among other effects increases IGF-1 signaling that promotes tumor cell growth.","incorrectas":{"B":"TGF-beta is typically a growth inhibitor in normal cells and is not the growth factor classically increased by VHL loss in this pathway.","C":"EGFR overexpression is important in other cancers (e.g., lung, colorectal) but is not the classic VHL-HIF pathway growth factor.","D":"FGF23 regulates phosphate homeostasis and is not part of the VHL-HIF-driven growth pathway in RCC."}}},{"id":"anking-neoplasia-195","fuente":"anking","tema":14,"anki":"1475463026584","q":"Loss of the VHL tumor suppressor gene in renal cell carcinoma leads to increased activity of which transcription factor, which in turn increases VEGF and PDGF?","o":{"A":"HIF (hypoxia-inducible factor)","B":"NF-kB","C":"p53","D":"STAT3"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"VHL normally targets HIF for degradation; its loss causes HIF accumulation, driving transcription of VEGF and PDGF that promote angiogenesis and tumor growth.","incorrectas":{"B":"NF-kB is involved in inflammatory and immune signaling, not the classic VHL-associated pathway in RCC.","C":"p53 is a tumor suppressor itself and is not upregulated by VHL loss; it is typically inactivated in other cancers.","D":"STAT3 is involved in various cytokine signaling pathways but is not the transcription factor classically linked to VHL loss in RCC."}}},{"id":"anking-neoplasia-196","fuente":"anking","tema":14,"anki":"1480647032473","q":"HTLV-1 infection (the cause of adult T-cell leukemia/lymphoma, ATLL) is classically associated with which population?","o":{"A":"IV drug users","B":"Organ transplant recipients","C":"Agricultural workers","D":"Healthcare workers"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"HTLV-1 is transmitted via blood, sexual contact, and breastfeeding, and is classically associated with IV drug users due to needle-sharing transmission.","incorrectas":{"B":"Organ transplant recipients are at risk for other infections (e.g., CMV, PTLD) but are not the classic HTLV-1-associated population.","C":"Agricultural workers are not the classic risk group for HTLV-1/ATLL.","D":"Healthcare workers are not the classic population associated with HTLV-1 transmission."}}},{"id":"anking-neoplasia-197","fuente":"anking","tema":14,"anki":"1481335519028","q":"Gastric MALT lymphomas (MALTomas) may regress with eradication treatment of which organism?","o":{"A":"Helicobacter pylori","B":"Epstein-Barr virus","C":"Helicobacter heilmannii","D":"Campylobacter jejuni"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"H. pylori infection drives chronic antigenic stimulation in gastric MALT lymphoma, and antibiotic eradication of H. pylori can cause tumor regression in early-stage disease.","incorrectas":{"B":"EBV is linked to other lymphomas (e.g., Burkitt, Hodgkin) but is not the organism whose eradication causes gastric MALToma regression.","C":"H. heilmannii is a rarer cause of gastritis and not the classic organism linked to MALToma regression with treatment.","D":"Campylobacter jejuni is associated with immunoproliferative small intestinal disease, not gastric MALToma."}}},{"id":"anking-cardiovascular-pathology-198","fuente":"anking","tema":15,"anki":"1462381664335","q":"Secondary hyperaldosteronism is caused by activation of which system?","o":{"A":"Renin-angiotensin system","B":"Sympathetic nervous system","C":"Natriuretic peptide system","D":"Hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Secondary hyperaldosteronism results from extra-adrenal activation of the renin-angiotensin system, as seen in renal artery stenosis, JG cell tumors, and edematous states like cirrhosis, heart failure, and nephrotic syndrome.","incorrectas":{"B":"Sympathetic activation raises renin secretion but is not itself the defining cause of secondary hyperaldosteronism.","C":"The natriuretic peptide system opposes aldosterone/sodium retention rather than driving it.","D":"The HPA axis governs cortisol, not aldosterone, secretion."}}},{"id":"anking-cardiovascular-pathology-199","fuente":"anking","tema":15,"anki":"1471797120815","q":"What term describes inflammation of the blood vessel wall?","o":{"A":"Vasculitis","B":"Atherosclerosis","C":"Thrombophlebitis","D":"Arteriosclerosis"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Vasculitis is defined as inflammation of the blood vessel wall.","incorrectas":{"B":"Atherosclerosis is lipid/plaque accumulation in vessel walls, not primarily an inflammatory vasculitic process.","C":"Thrombophlebitis is inflammation associated with a thrombus in a vein, not a general term for vessel wall inflammation.","D":"Arteriosclerosis refers to hardening/thickening of arterial walls, not inflammation."}}},{"id":"anking-cardiovascular-pathology-200","fuente":"anking","tema":15,"anki":"1471806043495","q":"Which layer forms the innermost part of the arterial wall, consisting of endothelial cells on a basement membrane?","o":{"A":"Intima","B":"Media","C":"Adventitia","D":"Serosa"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"The tunica intima is the innermost arterial layer, composed of endothelial cells resting on a basement membrane.","incorrectas":{"B":"The media is the middle, smooth muscle layer, not the innermost.","C":"The adventitia is the outermost connective tissue layer.","D":"The serosa is a serous membrane covering organs, not a layer of the arterial wall."}}},{"id":"anking-cardiovascular-pathology-201","fuente":"anking","tema":15,"anki":"1471806048024","q":"Which layer forms the middle part of the arterial wall and consists of smooth muscle?","o":{"A":"Media","B":"Intima","C":"Adventitia","D":"Endothelium"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"The tunica media is the middle arterial layer, composed of smooth muscle that regulates vessel tone.","incorrectas":{"B":"The intima is the innermost endothelial layer, not the muscular middle layer.","C":"The adventitia is the outer connective tissue layer.","D":"The endothelium is a single cell layer within the intima, not the muscular media."}}},{"id":"anking-cardiovascular-pathology-202","fuente":"anking","tema":15,"anki":"1471806052273","q":"Which layer forms the outer part of the arterial wall and consists of connective tissue surrounding the vessel?","o":{"A":"Adventitia","B":"Intima","C":"Media","D":"Basement membrane"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"The tunica adventitia is the outermost layer, composed of connective tissue that surrounds and supports the vessel.","incorrectas":{"B":"The intima is the innermost endothelial layer.","C":"The media is the middle smooth muscle layer.","D":"The basement membrane underlies the endothelium within the intima, not the outer layer."}}},{"id":"anking-cardiovascular-pathology-203","fuente":"anking","tema":15,"anki":"1471806065717","q":"Giant cell (temporal) arteritis is classified as which type of vasculitis based on vessel size?","o":{"A":"Large-vessel vasculitis","B":"Medium-vessel vasculitis","C":"Small-vessel vasculitis","D":"Variable-vessel vasculitis"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Giant cell (temporal) arteritis is a large-vessel vasculitis, typically affecting branches of the carotid artery including the temporal artery.","incorrectas":{"B":"Medium-vessel vasculitis includes conditions like polyarteritis nodosa and Kawasaki disease, not giant cell arteritis.","C":"Small-vessel vasculitis includes ANCA-associated and immune-complex vasculitides, not giant cell arteritis.","D":"Variable-vessel vasculitis (e.g., Behçet disease) affects vessels of any size, which is not the classic categorization for giant cell arteritis."}}},{"id":"anking-cardiovascular-pathology-204","fuente":"anking","tema":15,"anki":"1471806073709","q":"Which demographic is classically affected by giant cell (temporal) arteritis?","o":{"A":"Elderly females (>50 years)","B":"Young Asian females (<40 years)","C":"Middle-aged men","D":"Young children"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Giant cell (temporal) arteritis classically affects elderly females over age 50.","incorrectas":{"B":"Young Asian females under 40 are classically affected by Takayasu arteritis, not giant cell arteritis.","C":"Middle-aged men are classically affected by polyarteritis nodosa.","D":"Young children are affected by Kawasaki disease, not giant cell arteritis."}}},{"id":"anking-cardiovascular-pathology-205","fuente":"anking","tema":15,"anki":"1471806078219","q":"Involvement of the temporal artery in giant cell (temporal) arteritis classically causes what symptom?","o":{"A":"Unilateral headache","B":"Bilateral leg claudication","C":"Hematuria","D":"Abdominal pain"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Temporal artery involvement classically produces a unilateral headache due to inflammation of the vessel supplying the scalp and temporal region.","incorrectas":{"B":"Bilateral leg claudication suggests peripheral or aortic involvement, not the classic temporal artery presentation.","C":"Hematuria suggests renal or small-vessel vasculitis involvement, not giant cell arteritis.","D":"Abdominal pain is more typical of medium-vessel vasculitis such as polyarteritis nodosa."}}},{"id":"anking-cardiovascular-pathology-206","fuente":"anking","tema":15,"anki":"1471806086201","q":"Giant cell (temporal) arteritis may present with claudication of which structure?","o":{"A":"Jaw","B":"Calf","C":"Arm","D":"Tongue base"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Jaw claudication, pain with chewing due to ischemia of the muscles of mastication, is a classic finding in giant cell arteritis.","incorrectas":{"B":"Calf claudication is typical of peripheral arterial disease, not giant cell arteritis.","C":"Arm claudication suggests subclavian involvement as seen in Takayasu arteritis.","D":"Tongue claudication can occur but jaw claudication is the classically tested finding."}}},{"id":"anking-cardiovascular-pathology-207","fuente":"anking","tema":15,"anki":"1471806095511","q":"Involvement of the ophthalmic artery in giant cell (temporal) arteritis may cause what type of disturbance?","o":{"A":"Visual disturbances","B":"Auditory disturbances","C":"Olfactory disturbances","D":"Gustatory disturbances"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Ophthalmic artery involvement can cause visual disturbances, including sudden vision loss, making prompt treatment critical.","incorrectas":{"B":"Auditory disturbances are not a classic feature of ophthalmic artery involvement in giant cell arteritis.","C":"Olfactory disturbances are not associated with this vessel's involvement.","D":"Gustatory disturbances are not associated with ophthalmic artery ischemia."}}},{"id":"anking-cardiovascular-pathology-208","fuente":"anking","tema":15,"anki":"1471806114242","q":"Biopsy of a patient with giant cell (temporal) arteritis classically reveals fibrosis of which layer?","o":{"A":"Intimal fibrosis","B":"Adventitial fibrosis","C":"Medial fibrosis only","D":"Perivascular fat necrosis"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Biopsy in giant cell arteritis classically shows intimal fibrosis along with granulomatous inflammation and giant cells within the vessel wall.","incorrectas":{"B":"Adventitial fibrosis is not the classic biopsy finding described for this disease.","C":"Medial fibrosis alone does not capture the characteristic intimal involvement seen in giant cell arteritis.","D":"Perivascular fat necrosis is not a described feature of giant cell arteritis biopsies."}}},{"id":"anking-cardiovascular-pathology-209","fuente":"anking","tema":15,"anki":"1471806124315","q":"Why does diagnosis of giant cell (temporal) arteritis require biopsy of a long vessel segment?","o":{"A":"Because the lesions are segmental","B":"Because the lesions are diffuse and continuous","C":"Because the lesions only occur in the media","D":"Because the artery is normally very short"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"The lesions of giant cell arteritis are segmental (skip lesions), so a long segment must be biopsied to avoid a false-negative result; a negative biopsy does not exclude disease.","incorrectas":{"B":"If the lesions were diffuse and continuous, a short segment biopsy would suffice, contrary to actual practice.","C":"The lesions are not confined solely to the media; intimal involvement is characteristic.","D":"The requirement for a long segment relates to the patchy distribution of disease, not the artery's normal length."}}},{"id":"anking-cardiovascular-pathology-210","fuente":"anking","tema":15,"anki":"1471806144718","q":"Takayasu arteritis is classified as which type of vasculitis based on vessel size?","o":{"A":"Large-vessel vasculitis","B":"Medium-vessel vasculitis","C":"Small-vessel vasculitis","D":"Capillary-vessel vasculitis"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Takayasu arteritis is a large-vessel vasculitis typically involving the aorta and its major branches.","incorrectas":{"B":"Medium-vessel vasculitis includes polyarteritis nodosa and Kawasaki disease, not Takayasu arteritis.","C":"Small-vessel vasculitis affects arterioles, capillaries, and venules, not the large vessels typical of Takayasu.","D":"Capillary-vessel vasculitis is not a standard classification, and Takayasu spares capillaries."}}},{"id":"anking-cardiovascular-pathology-211","fuente":"anking","tema":15,"anki":"1471806157855","q":"Which demographic is classically affected by Takayasu arteritis?","o":{"A":"Young Asian females (<40 years)","B":"Elderly females (>50 years)","C":"Middle-aged men","D":"Young Caucasian males"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Takayasu arteritis classically affects young Asian females under age 40, earning it the nickname 'pulseless disease.'","incorrectas":{"B":"Elderly females over 50 are classically affected by giant cell arteritis, not Takayasu.","C":"Middle-aged men are classically affected by polyarteritis nodosa.","D":"Young Caucasian males are not the classic demographic; Takayasu is most associated with young Asian women."}}},{"id":"anking-cardiovascular-pathology-212","fuente":"anking","tema":15,"anki":"1471806162437","q":"Takayasu arteritis may present with which two types of symptoms?","o":{"A":"Visual and neurologic symptoms","B":"Cardiac and renal symptoms only","C":"Gastrointestinal and hepatic symptoms","D":"Dermatologic and articular symptoms only"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Takayasu arteritis, due to involvement of the aortic arch branches, can present with visual and neurologic symptoms, along with fever, night sweats, arthritis, myalgias, and skin nodules.","incorrectas":{"B":"Cardiac and renal symptoms can occur but are not the paired findings highlighted as classic for this presentation.","C":"Gastrointestinal and hepatic symptoms are not characteristic features of Takayasu arteritis.","D":"Dermatologic and articular symptoms may occur as additional features but are not the primary paired symptom set described."}}},{"id":"anking-cardiovascular-pathology-213","fuente":"anking","tema":15,"anki":"1471806184543","q":"What is the treatment of choice for a patient with Takayasu arteritis?","o":{"A":"Corticosteroids","B":"Methotrexate alone","C":"Aspirin alone","D":"Antibiotics"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Corticosteroids are the mainstay treatment for Takayasu arteritis to suppress vascular inflammation.","incorrectas":{"B":"Methotrexate may be used as a steroid-sparing agent in refractory cases but is not the primary first-line treatment.","C":"Aspirin alone does not address the underlying inflammatory process driving the disease.","D":"Antibiotics have no role since Takayasu arteritis is not an infectious process."}}},{"id":"anking-cardiovascular-pathology-214","fuente":"anking","tema":15,"anki":"1471806188325","q":"Polyarteritis nodosa is classified as which type of vasculitis based on vessel size?","o":{"A":"Medium-vessel vasculitis","B":"Large-vessel vasculitis","C":"Small-vessel vasculitis","D":"Venous-only vasculitis"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Polyarteritis nodosa is an immune-complex-mediated medium-vessel vasculitis.","incorrectas":{"B":"Large-vessel vasculitis includes giant cell and Takayasu arteritis, not polyarteritis nodosa.","C":"Small-vessel vasculitis includes ANCA-associated conditions, not polyarteritis nodosa.","D":"Polyarteritis nodosa affects arteries, not exclusively veins."}}},{"id":"anking-cardiovascular-pathology-215","fuente":"anking","tema":15,"anki":"1471806197883","q":"Which demographic is classically affected by polyarteritis nodosa?","o":{"A":"Middle-aged men","B":"Young Asian females (<40 years)","C":"Elderly females (>50 years)","D":"Infants and toddlers"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Polyarteritis nodosa classically affects middle-aged men and is associated with hepatitis B infection in some cases.","incorrectas":{"B":"Young Asian females are the classic demographic for Takayasu arteritis, not polyarteritis nodosa.","C":"Elderly females are the classic demographic for giant cell arteritis, not polyarteritis nodosa.","D":"Infants and toddlers are classically affected by Kawasaki disease, not polyarteritis nodosa."}}},{"id":"anking-musculoskeletal-pathology-216","fuente":"anking","tema":16,"anki":"1463279694569","q":"Spontaneous abortion is more common in patients with which type of hemostatic state, as seen in antiphospholipid syndrome?","o":{"A":"Hypercoagulable state","B":"Hypocoagulable state","C":"Hemodilutional state","D":"Hypofibrinogenemic state"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Antiphospholipid syndrome produces a hypercoagulable state due to antiphospholipid antibodies (e.g., lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin), which increases the risk of thrombosis and recurrent spontaneous abortion. Other risk factors for spontaneous abortion include congenital infection and teratogen exposure.","incorrectas":{"B":"A hypocoagulable state would predispose to bleeding, not the thrombotic complications and pregnancy loss seen in antiphospholipid syndrome.","C":"Hemodilution is not a recognized mechanism of pregnancy loss in antiphospholipid syndrome.","D":"Low fibrinogen would impair, not promote, clot formation, which is the opposite of the pathophysiology in antiphospholipid syndrome."}}},{"id":"anking-musculoskeletal-pathology-217","fuente":"anking","tema":16,"anki":"1475363908883","q":"Which type of nephrotic syndrome is a patient with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) most likely to develop?","o":{"A":"Membranous nephropathy","B":"Minimal change disease","C":"Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","D":"Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Membranous nephropathy is the classic nephrotic-range renal manifestation of SLE, characterized by subepithelial immune complex deposits causing a 'spike and dome' appearance on electron microscopy.","incorrectas":{"B":"Minimal change disease is typically idiopathic or associated with Hodgkin lymphoma, not a classic lupus nephropathy.","C":"Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis is more commonly associated with HIV, heroin use, or obesity rather than being the classic lupus nephrotic pattern.","D":"Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis can occur in lupus but is not the classic nephrotic syndrome pattern associated with SLE; it more often presents with a mixed nephrotic/nephritic picture."}}},{"id":"anking-musculoskeletal-pathology-218","fuente":"anking","tema":16,"anki":"1475376588139","q":"Which type of nephritic syndrome is a patient with SLE most likely to develop?","o":{"A":"Diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis","B":"IgA nephropathy","C":"Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis","D":"Goodpasture syndrome (anti-GBM disease)"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis is the most common and most severe renal manifestation of SLE, resulting from subendothelial immune complex deposition and causing nephritic-range disease.","incorrectas":{"B":"IgA nephropathy is a distinct primary glomerular disease, typically following mucosal infections, not the classic lupus nephritis pattern.","C":"Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis follows streptococcal infection and is unrelated to SLE.","D":"Goodpasture syndrome is caused by anti-glomerular basement membrane antibodies, a distinct mechanism from the immune complex disease seen in lupus nephritis."}}},{"id":"anking-musculoskeletal-pathology-219","fuente":"anking","tema":16,"anki":"1475376595559","q":"Diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis is a common cause of death in patients with which autoimmune disease?","o":{"A":"Systemic lupus erythematosus","B":"Rheumatoid arthritis","C":"Sjögren syndrome","D":"Systemic sclerosis (scleroderma)"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis is the most common and most severe form of lupus nephritis and is a leading cause of morbidity and mortality in SLE patients.","incorrectas":{"B":"Rheumatoid arthritis primarily causes joint destruction and is not classically associated with diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis as a major cause of death.","C":"Sjögren syndrome mainly affects exocrine glands and is not typically associated with this renal pathology as a leading cause of death.","D":"Systemic sclerosis causes renal crisis via vascular pathology (malignant hypertension), not classic immune complex diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis."}}},{"id":"anking-musculoskeletal-pathology-220","fuente":"anking","tema":16,"anki":"1475704608986","q":"A deficiency of which electrolyte causes bone loss, osteomalacia in adults, and rickets in children?","o":{"A":"Phosphate (PO4 3-)","B":"Calcium (Ca2+)","C":"Magnesium (Mg2+)","D":"Potassium (K+)"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Low phosphate impairs mineralization of osteoid, leading to defective bone mineralization that manifests as osteomalacia in adults and rickets in children.","incorrectas":{"B":"While calcium is also important for mineralization, this card specifically identifies phosphate as the electrolyte in question.","C":"Magnesium deficiency can cause secondary hypocalcemia but is not the classic cause of osteomalacia/rickets described here.","D":"Potassium disturbances affect cardiac and neuromuscular function, not bone mineralization."}}},{"id":"anking-musculoskeletal-pathology-221","fuente":"anking","tema":16,"anki":"1485127545269","q":"Which anti-epileptic drug may be used to treat peripheral neuropathy and fibromyalgia (chronic pain)?","o":{"A":"Gabapentin","B":"Phenytoin","C":"Valproic acid","D":"Lamotrigine"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Gabapentin, an anti-epileptic that modulates voltage-gated calcium channels, is commonly used off-label for neuropathic pain conditions such as peripheral neuropathy and fibromyalgia.","incorrectas":{"B":"Phenytoin is primarily used for seizure control and is not a first-line choice for fibromyalgia or peripheral neuropathy.","C":"Valproic acid is used for epilepsy, bipolar disorder, and migraine prophylaxis, not typically for fibromyalgia.","D":"Lamotrigine is used mainly for seizures and bipolar disorder, not as a standard treatment for peripheral neuropathy or fibromyalgia."}}},{"id":"anking-musculoskeletal-pathology-222","fuente":"anking","tema":16,"anki":"1487815304836","q":"Which demographic group is most commonly associated with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)?","o":{"A":"Middle-aged females","B":"Elderly males","C":"Young males","D":"Prepubescent children of either sex"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"SLE predominantly affects middle-aged females, reflecting a strong female predominance; the female bias is less pronounced in children and older adults.","incorrectas":{"B":"Elderly males are not the typical demographic; SLE shows a strong female predominance rather than affecting men preferentially.","C":"Young males are not the classic demographic for SLE; the disease favors females, particularly in the reproductive/middle-aged years.","D":"While SLE can occur in children, this is not the demographic with the strongest association, and the female bias is actually less dramatic in children."}}},{"id":"anking-musculoskeletal-pathology-223","fuente":"anking","tema":16,"anki":"1487815310898","q":"Which ethnicities are more commonly affected by SLE?","o":{"A":"African Americans and Hispanics","B":"Caucasians and Scandinavians","C":"East Asians and Ashkenazi Jews only","D":"Native Americans exclusively"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"SLE has a higher prevalence and often more severe disease course in African Americans and Hispanic populations compared to other ethnic groups.","incorrectas":{"B":"Caucasian populations, particularly of Northern European descent, have lower rates of SLE compared to African American and Hispanic populations.","C":"While SLE occurs in East Asian populations, this option incorrectly limits and mischaracterizes the ethnic groups with the highest recognized risk.","D":"SLE is not exclusive to Native Americans; African Americans and Hispanics are the ethnicities classically noted for higher risk."}}},{"id":"anking-musculoskeletal-pathology-224","fuente":"anking","tema":16,"anki":"1487815313912","q":"What type of hypersensitivity reaction is systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) primarily classified as?","o":{"A":"Type III hypersensitivity (immune complex deposition)","B":"Type I hypersensitivity (IgE-mediated)","C":"Type II hypersensitivity (antibody-mediated cytotoxicity)","D":"Type IV hypersensitivity (T cell-mediated)"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"SLE is primarily a type III hypersensitivity reaction due to immune complex deposition in tissues such as the kidneys and skin; an exception is the hematologic complications (anemia, thrombocytopenia, leukopenia), which are type II reactions.","incorrectas":{"B":"Type I hypersensitivity is IgE-mediated and involves mast cell degranulation, as seen in allergies and anaphylaxis, not SLE.","C":"Type II hypersensitivity is the mechanism behind SLE's hematologic complications specifically, not the primary overall classification of the disease.","D":"Type IV hypersensitivity is T cell-mediated (delayed-type), as seen in contact dermatitis or tuberculin reactions, not the primary mechanism in SLE."}}},{"id":"anking-musculoskeletal-pathology-225","fuente":"anking","tema":16,"anki":"1487815330412","q":"What is the most common and most severe form of renal disease in patients with SLE?","o":{"A":"Diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis (DPGN)","B":"Minimal change disease","C":"Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","D":"Amyloidosis"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis is the most common and most severe form of lupus nephritis; patients may also have coexisting membranous glomerulonephritis.","incorrectas":{"B":"Minimal change disease is not a typical or severe manifestation of lupus nephritis.","C":"Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis is not the classic or most common renal lesion in SLE.","D":"Renal amyloidosis is associated with chronic inflammatory conditions and plasma cell dyscrasias, not classically with SLE."}}},{"id":"anking-musculoskeletal-pathology-226","fuente":"anking","tema":16,"anki":"1487815337003","q":"What type of endocarditis is associated with SLE?","o":{"A":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis","B":"Infective (bacterial) endocarditis","C":"Marantic (nonbacterial thrombotic) endocarditis of malignancy","D":"Rheumatic endocarditis"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis is a nonbacterial, verrucous endocarditis characterized by sterile vegetations on the mitral and/or aortic valve, classically associated with SLE.","incorrectas":{"B":"Infective endocarditis is caused by bacterial infection of heart valves, not by lupus-associated immune mechanisms.","C":"Marantic endocarditis is associated with malignancy and hypercoagulable states from cancer, a distinct entity from the lupus-specific Libman-Sacks endocarditis.","D":"Rheumatic endocarditis follows rheumatic fever due to streptococcal infection, not SLE."}}},{"id":"anking-musculoskeletal-pathology-227","fuente":"anking","tema":16,"anki":"1487815353788","q":"Which SLE-associated antibody is a useful predictor of disease activity and renal involvement?","o":{"A":"Anti-dsDNA antibodies","B":"Anti-histone antibodies","C":"Anti-centromere antibodies","D":"Anti-Jo-1 antibodies"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Anti-dsDNA antibody titers correlate with disease activity and are particularly useful for monitoring renal involvement (lupus nephritis) in SLE.","incorrectas":{"B":"Anti-histone antibodies are characteristic of drug-induced lupus, not a marker of disease activity in SLE.","C":"Anti-centromere antibodies are associated with limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis (CREST syndrome), not SLE.","D":"Anti-Jo-1 antibodies are associated with polymyositis/dermatomyositis, not SLE."}}},{"id":"anking-musculoskeletal-pathology-228","fuente":"anking","tema":16,"anki":"1487815356381","q":"SLE is characterized by which change in complement levels of C3 and C4?","o":{"A":"Decreased levels","B":"Increased levels","C":"No change in levels","D":"Increased C3 with decreased C4 only"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Active SLE is characterized by decreased C3 and C4 levels due to consumption during immune complex formation.","incorrectas":{"B":"Increased complement levels would not reflect the consumptive process occurring with immune complex formation in active lupus.","C":"Complement levels are not unchanged in active SLE; they decrease due to consumption in immune complex reactions.","D":"Both C3 and C4 are decreased in SLE, not selectively one over the other."}}},{"id":"anking-musculoskeletal-pathology-229","fuente":"anking","tema":16,"anki":"1487815366607","q":"SLE is characterized by which change in CH50 (total hemolytic complement) levels?","o":{"A":"Decreased levels","B":"Increased levels","C":"Normal levels","D":"Levels that fluctuate unpredictably with no clinical correlation"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"CH50 is decreased in SLE due to consumption of complement components during immune complex formation, similar to the decreases seen in C3 and C4.","incorrectas":{"B":"Increased CH50 would not be consistent with a consumptive complement process as occurs in active lupus.","C":"CH50 levels are not normal in active SLE; they are decreased due to ongoing complement consumption.","D":"CH50 levels do correlate with disease activity in lupus, decreasing with active immune complex-mediated inflammation, rather than fluctuating without correlation."}}},{"id":"anking-musculoskeletal-pathology-230","fuente":"anking","tema":16,"anki":"1487815392099","q":"Is the elevated PTT caused by lupus anticoagulant correctable by addition of normal plasma (mixing study)?","o":{"A":"No, the PTT remains elevated","B":"Yes, the PTT fully corrects","C":"Yes, but only after a 2-hour incubation","D":"Partially, correcting to half the baseline value"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Lupus anticoagulant is an inhibitor, so the elevated PTT is not corrected by mixing with normal plasma; this is an important distinguishing feature from clotting factor deficiencies, which do correct with normal plasma.","incorrectas":{"B":"Full correction would indicate a factor deficiency rather than the presence of an inhibitor like lupus anticoagulant.","C":"Lupus anticoagulant does not correct even with incubation; this pattern would be more suggestive of a factor VIII inhibitor with time-dependent effect.","D":"There is no partial correction pattern classically described for lupus anticoagulant; the hallmark is non-correction on mixing studies."}}},{"id":"anking-musculoskeletal-pathology-231","fuente":"anking","tema":16,"anki":"1487815398186","q":"Antiphospholipid syndrome can occur as a primary disorder or as a secondary disorder associated with which condition?","o":{"A":"Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)","B":"Rheumatoid arthritis","C":"Type 1 diabetes mellitus","D":"Hashimoto thyroiditis"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Antiphospholipid syndrome is most commonly seen as a primary disorder but can also occur secondary to SLE.","incorrectas":{"B":"Rheumatoid arthritis is not the classic secondary association for antiphospholipid syndrome.","C":"Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune condition but is not classically linked to secondary antiphospholipid syndrome.","D":"Hashimoto thyroiditis is not the classic secondary association described for antiphospholipid syndrome."}}},{"id":"anking-musculoskeletal-pathology-232","fuente":"anking","tema":16,"anki":"1487815405384","q":"Antiphospholipid syndrome is characterized by what type of coagulable state, increasing the risk of thrombosis and recurrent pregnancy loss?","o":{"A":"Hypercoagulable state","B":"Hypocoagulable state","C":"Euvolemic coagulable state","D":"Dilutional coagulopathy state"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Antiphospholipid syndrome produces a hypercoagulable state, increasing the risk for thrombosis (arterial and venous) and recurrent pregnancy loss.","incorrectas":{"B":"A hypocoagulable state would cause bleeding tendencies, which is the opposite of the thrombotic risk seen in antiphospholipid syndrome.","C":"There is no such standard classification as 'euvolemic coagulable state' relevant to antiphospholipid syndrome.","D":"Dilutional coagulopathy occurs with massive transfusion or fluid resuscitation, not the pathophysiology of antiphospholipid syndrome."}}},{"id":"anking-musculoskeletal-pathology-233","fuente":"anking","tema":16,"anki":"1487815417093","q":"What is the treatment for antiphospholipid syndrome?","o":{"A":"Anticoagulants","B":"High-dose corticosteroids alone","C":"Antiplatelet agents alone (e.g., aspirin monotherapy in all cases)","D":"Intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) as first-line therapy"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Anticoagulation is the mainstay of treatment for antiphospholipid syndrome to prevent recurrent thrombotic events.","incorrectas":{"B":"Corticosteroids alone do not address the thrombotic risk and are not the standard treatment for antiphospholipid syndrome itself.","C":"While aspirin may be used in some contexts, anticoagulants (e.g., warfarin/heparin) are the primary treatment, not antiplatelet monotherapy alone.","D":"IVIG is not a first-line treatment for antiphospholipid syndrome; it is reserved for select refractory or pregnancy-related cases."}}},{"id":"anking-respiratory-pathology-234","fuente":"anking","tema":17,"anki":"1473535947702","q":"Which condition is defined as inflammation of the nasal mucosa?","o":{"A":"Rhinitis","B":"Sinusitis","C":"Pharyngitis","D":"Laryngitis"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Rhinitis is by definition inflammation of the nasal mucosa, commonly caused by viral infection or allergy.","incorrectas":{"B":"Sinusitis refers to inflammation of the paranasal sinuses, not the nasal mucosa itself.","C":"Pharyngitis is inflammation of the pharynx (throat), a distinct anatomic site.","D":"Laryngitis is inflammation of the larynx, not the nasal mucosa."}}},{"id":"anking-respiratory-pathology-235","fuente":"anking","tema":17,"anki":"1473536750240","q":"What is the most common cause of rhinitis (the common cold)?","o":{"A":"Rhinovirus","B":"Respiratory syncytial virus","C":"Adenovirus","D":"Influenza virus"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Rhinovirus is the most common cause of rhinitis/the common cold, accounting for the majority of cases.","incorrectas":{"B":"RSV more classically causes bronchiolitis in infants, not the typical common cold.","C":"Adenovirus can cause upper respiratory infections and conjunctivitis but is a less common cause of rhinitis than rhinovirus.","D":"Influenza virus causes a more systemic illness (fever, myalgias) rather than the typical common cold picture."}}},{"id":"anking-respiratory-pathology-236","fuente":"anking","tema":17,"anki":"1473536754044","q":"Which condition presents with sneezing, congestion, and runny nose, characteristic of the common cold?","o":{"A":"Rhinitis","B":"Epiglottitis","C":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","D":"Croup"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Rhinitis presents classically with sneezing, nasal congestion, and rhinorrhea, the hallmark of the common cold.","incorrectas":{"B":"Epiglottitis presents with drooling, muffled voice, and stridor, not sneezing/congestion.","C":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma presents with cervical lymphadenopathy and nasal obstruction, not an acute cold-like picture.","D":"Croup presents with a barking cough and inspiratory stridor, not typical cold symptoms."}}},{"id":"anking-respiratory-pathology-237","fuente":"anking","tema":17,"anki":"1473536759678","q":"Allergic rhinitis is characterized by an inflammatory infiltrate composed predominantly of which cell type?","o":{"A":"Eosinophils","B":"Neutrophils","C":"Plasma cells","D":"Mast cells alone"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Allergic rhinitis is a type I hypersensitivity reaction characterized by an eosinophil-rich inflammatory infiltrate in the nasal mucosa.","incorrectas":{"B":"Neutrophils are more typical of acute bacterial infections, not allergic rhinitis.","C":"Plasma cells are not the characteristic infiltrate of allergic rhinitis.","D":"While mast cells trigger the immediate reaction via IgE, the infiltrate classically described is eosinophilic, not isolated mast cells."}}},{"id":"anking-respiratory-pathology-238","fuente":"anking","tema":17,"anki":"1473536767081","q":"What are protrusions of edematous, inflamed nasal mucosa called?","o":{"A":"Nasal polyps","B":"Angiofibromas","C":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","D":"Adenoid hypertrophy"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Nasal polyps are protrusions of edematous, chronically inflamed nasal mucosa, often from recurrent rhinitis.","incorrectas":{"B":"Angiofibromas are benign vascular tumors, not simple mucosal protrusions from inflammation.","C":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is a malignant epithelial tumor, not a benign inflammatory mucosal protrusion.","D":"Adenoid hypertrophy refers to enlargement of pharyngeal tonsillar tissue, not nasal mucosal polyps."}}},{"id":"anking-respiratory-pathology-239","fuente":"anking","tema":17,"anki":"1473536769801","q":"Nasal polyps are usually secondary to repeated bouts of which condition?","o":{"A":"Rhinitis","B":"Sinusitis-only fungal infection","C":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","D":"Epiglottitis"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Nasal polyps typically arise from chronic or repeated bouts of rhinitis causing persistent mucosal edema and inflammation.","incorrectas":{"B":"While sinus disease can coexist, the classic association taught is with recurrent rhinitis, not isolated fungal sinusitis.","C":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is a separate malignant process, not a precursor causing polyps.","D":"Epiglottitis affects the epiglottis/larynx and is unrelated to nasal polyp formation."}}},{"id":"anking-respiratory-pathology-240","fuente":"anking","tema":17,"anki":"1473536772473","q":"In children, nasal polyps are classically associated with which underlying condition?","o":{"A":"Cystic fibrosis","B":"Aspirin-induced asthma","C":"Kartagener syndrome only","D":"Chronic granulomatous disease"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"In children, nasal polyps are classically associated with cystic fibrosis, and any child with nasal polyps should be evaluated for CF.","incorrectas":{"B":"Aspirin-induced asthma is the classic association in adults, not children.","C":"Kartagener syndrome (primary ciliary dyskinesia) can cause sinus disease but is not the classic pediatric association tested for nasal polyps.","D":"Chronic granulomatous disease predisposes to infections with catalase-positive organisms, not specifically nasal polyps."}}},{"id":"anking-respiratory-pathology-241","fuente":"anking","tema":17,"anki":"1473536775019","q":"In adults, nasal polyps are classically associated with which condition?","o":{"A":"Aspirin-induced asthma","B":"Cystic fibrosis","C":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","D":"Kartagener syndrome"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"In adults, nasal polyps are classically associated with aspirin-induced (aspirin-intolerant) asthma.","incorrectas":{"B":"Cystic fibrosis is the classic pediatric association, not the adult one.","C":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is a distinct malignant tumor, not linked to polyp formation via this mechanism.","D":"Kartagener syndrome is not the classic adult association taught for nasal polyps."}}},{"id":"anking-respiratory-pathology-242","fuente":"anking","tema":17,"anki":"1473536778562","q":"Which condition is characterized by the triad of asthma, aspirin-induced bronchospasm, and nasal polyps?","o":{"A":"Aspirin-induced asthma","B":"Churg-Strauss syndrome","C":"Samter's triad variant (allergic rhinitis)","D":"Loeffler syndrome"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Aspirin-induced (aspirin-intolerant) asthma presents as a triad of asthma, aspirin-induced bronchospasm, and nasal polyps, due to shunting of arachidonic acid toward the leukotriene pathway.","incorrectas":{"B":"Churg-Strauss syndrome (eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis) involves vasculitis and eosinophilia, a distinct systemic disease.","C":"Allergic rhinitis alone does not comprise this specific triad with aspirin sensitivity.","D":"Loeffler syndrome involves transient pulmonary infiltrates with eosinophilia, unrelated to this triad."}}},{"id":"anking-respiratory-pathology-243","fuente":"anking","tema":17,"anki":"1473536785588","q":"What are benign tumors of the nasal mucosa composed of large blood vessels and fibrous tissue called?","o":{"A":"Angiofibromas","B":"Nasal polyps","C":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","D":"Hemangiomas of the skin"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Angiofibromas are benign, highly vascular tumors of the nasal mucosa composed of large blood vessels and fibrous stroma.","incorrectas":{"B":"Nasal polyps are edematous inflammatory mucosal protrusions, not true vascular neoplasms.","C":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is a malignant epithelial tumor, not a benign vascular one.","D":"Cutaneous hemangiomas are a different entity located in the skin, not nasal mucosa."}}},{"id":"anking-respiratory-pathology-244","fuente":"anking","tema":17,"anki":"1473536788532","q":"Which demographic is classically associated with angiofibroma?","o":{"A":"Adolescent males","B":"Elderly females","C":"Young children of either sex","D":"Middle-aged smokers"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Angiofibromas classically occur in adolescent males, presenting with profuse epistaxis.","incorrectas":{"B":"Elderly females are not the classic demographic; angiofibroma is a disease of young males.","C":"Angiofibroma is not typically seen in young children of either sex; it is specific to adolescent males.","D":"Middle-aged smokers are more associated with malignancies like squamous cell carcinoma, not angiofibroma."}}},{"id":"anking-respiratory-pathology-245","fuente":"anking","tema":17,"anki":"1473536790852","q":"Nasopharyngeal angiofibromas often present with profuse occurrence of which symptom?","o":{"A":"Epistaxis","B":"Hemoptysis","C":"Otorrhea","D":"Dysphagia"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Nasopharyngeal angiofibromas are highly vascular tumors that classically present with profuse, recurrent epistaxis.","incorrectas":{"B":"Hemoptysis refers to coughing up blood from the lower respiratory tract, not a nasal tumor symptom.","C":"Otorrhea (ear discharge) is not a typical presentation of a nasal mass.","D":"Dysphagia (difficulty swallowing) is not the classic presenting symptom of angiofibroma."}}},{"id":"anking-respiratory-pathology-246","fuente":"anking","tema":17,"anki":"1473536793729","q":"What is the malignant tumor arising from nasopharyngeal epithelium called?","o":{"A":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","B":"Angiofibroma","C":"Nasal polyp","D":"Laryngeal papilloma"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is a malignant tumor of the nasopharyngeal epithelium, associated with EBV infection.","incorrectas":{"B":"Angiofibroma is a benign vascular tumor, not a malignant epithelial one.","C":"Nasal polyps are benign inflammatory lesions, not malignant tumors.","D":"Laryngeal papilloma is a benign lesion of the larynx associated with HPV, a different site and tumor type."}}},{"id":"anking-respiratory-pathology-247","fuente":"anking","tema":17,"anki":"1473536807246","q":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma often presents with involvement of which lymph nodes?","o":{"A":"Cervical","B":"Axillary","C":"Inguinal","D":"Mediastinal"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma commonly presents with painless cervical lymphadenopathy due to early lymphatic spread.","incorrectas":{"B":"Axillary nodes drain the upper extremity and breast, not the nasopharynx.","C":"Inguinal nodes drain the lower body, unrelated to nasopharyngeal drainage.","D":"Mediastinal nodes are an atypical and late finding, not the classic presenting site."}}},{"id":"anking-respiratory-pathology-248","fuente":"anking","tema":17,"anki":"1473536815967","q":"What is the most common cause of acute epiglottitis, especially in non-immunized children?","o":{"A":"Haemophilus influenzae type B","B":"Streptococcus pyogenes","C":"Parainfluenza virus","D":"Staphylococcus aureus"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Haemophilus influenzae type B is the classic cause of acute epiglottitis, particularly in unvaccinated children.","incorrectas":{"B":"Streptococcus pyogenes classically causes pharyngitis, not epiglottitis.","C":"Parainfluenza virus is the classic cause of croup (laryngotracheobronchitis), not epiglottitis.","D":"Staphylococcus aureus is not the classic pathogen associated with epiglottitis."}}},{"id":"anking-respiratory-pathology-249","fuente":"anking","tema":17,"anki":"1473536833722","q":"Acute epiglottitis may present with a muffled voice and what type of respiratory sound?","o":{"A":"Inspiratory stridor","B":"Expiratory wheeze","C":"Pleural friction rub","D":"Crackles"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Acute epiglottitis classically presents with muffled 'hot potato' voice, drooling, and inspiratory stridor due to upper airway obstruction.","incorrectas":{"B":"Expiratory wheeze is more typical of lower airway obstruction, such as asthma.","C":"Pleural friction rub suggests pleuritis, unrelated to epiglottitis.","D":"Crackles suggest alveolar/interstitial disease such as pneumonia, not upper airway obstruction."}}},{"id":"anking-respiratory-pathology-250","fuente":"anking","tema":17,"anki":"1473536836879","q":"Acute epiglottitis carries a risk of which life-threatening acute complication?","o":{"A":"Airway obstruction","B":"Pneumothorax","C":"Pulmonary embolism","D":"Cardiac tamponade"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Acute epiglottitis can cause rapid, severe swelling of the epiglottis leading to acute airway obstruction, a medical emergency.","incorrectas":{"B":"Pneumothorax is not a typical complication of epiglottitis.","C":"Pulmonary embolism is unrelated to upper airway inflammation.","D":"Cardiac tamponade is a pericardial process, not associated with epiglottitis."}}},{"id":"anking-respiratory-pathology-251","fuente":"anking","tema":17,"anki":"1473536839980","q":"What is the most common cause of laryngotracheobronchitis (croup)?","o":{"A":"Parainfluenza virus","B":"Haemophilus influenzae type B","C":"Respiratory syncytial virus","D":"Rhinovirus"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Parainfluenza virus is the most common cause of croup (laryngotracheobronchitis), typically presenting with a barking cough and stridor.","incorrectas":{"B":"Haemophilus influenzae type B is the classic cause of epiglottitis, not croup.","C":"RSV is more classically associated with bronchiolitis in infants than croup.","D":"Rhinovirus is the classic cause of the common cold, not croup."}}},{"id":"anking-psychiatry-pathology-252","fuente":"anking","tema":18,"anki":"1474666102186","q":"Combining dextromethorphan with other serotonergic agents (e.g., SSRIs, MAOIs) puts a patient at risk for which condition?","o":{"A":"Serotonin syndrome","B":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","C":"Malignant hyperthermia","D":"Anticholinergic toxicity"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Dextromethorphan has serotonergic activity and combining it with other serotonergic drugs (SSRIs, MAOIs) can precipitate serotonin syndrome, characterized by hyperthermia, clonus, hyperreflexia, and autonomic instability.","incorrectas":{"B":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is caused by dopamine receptor antagonists (antipsychotics), not serotonergic combinations.","C":"Malignant hyperthermia is triggered by inhaled anesthetics and succinylcholine in genetically susceptible individuals.","D":"Anticholinergic toxicity results from muscarinic receptor blockade, not serotonergic excess."}}},{"id":"anking-psychiatry-pathology-253","fuente":"anking","tema":18,"anki":"1484016780394","q":"What is the first-line treatment for nocturnal enuresis (bedwetting) in children?","o":{"A":"Motivational therapy (e.g., star charts)","B":"Desmopressin","C":"Imipramine","D":"Bladder augmentation surgery"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Motivational/behavioral therapy such as star charts and bedwetting alarms is the first-line, least invasive treatment for enuresis before pharmacologic options are considered.","incorrectas":{"B":"Desmopressin is a pharmacologic second-line option used if behavioral therapy fails.","C":"Imipramine is an older, less preferred pharmacologic agent reserved for refractory cases due to side effects.","D":"Surgery is not indicated for primary nocturnal enuresis and is reserved for rare anatomic causes."}}},{"id":"anking-psychiatry-pathology-254","fuente":"anking","tema":18,"anki":"1484618155233","q":"Long-term infant deprivation of affection classically results in failure to do what?","o":{"A":"Thrive","B":"Ambulate","C":"Wean","D":"Regulate temperature"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Chronic emotional deprivation in infancy is associated with failure to thrive, part of the classic \"weak, wordless, wary, wanting\" presentation.","incorrectas":{"B":"Motor milestones like ambulation are not the classic feature emphasized in deprivation syndromes.","C":"Weaning difficulty is not a described feature of infant deprivation.","D":"Temperature dysregulation is not part of the classic deprivation presentation."}}},{"id":"anking-psychiatry-pathology-255","fuente":"anking","tema":18,"anki":"1484623875483","q":"Long-term infant deprivation of affection may result in poor development of which skills?","o":{"A":"Language/socialization skills","B":"Fine motor skills","C":"Gross motor skills","D":"Visual-spatial skills"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Emotional deprivation impairs the infant's ability to develop normal language and social skills, consistent with the \"wordless\" component of the classic description.","incorrectas":{"B":"Fine motor skill delay is not the classic feature highlighted in deprivation syndromes.","C":"Gross motor delay is not specifically emphasized in this condition.","D":"Visual-spatial skill deficits are not part of the described syndrome."}}},{"id":"anking-psychiatry-pathology-256","fuente":"anking","tema":18,"anki":"1484623890671","q":"Long-term infant deprivation of affection may result in a lack of what fundamental psychological attribute?","o":{"A":"Basic trust","B":"Object permanence","C":"Self-esteem","D":"Autonomy"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Deprived infants fail to develop basic trust, an Eriksonian developmental milestone normally established through consistent caregiving in the first year of life.","incorrectas":{"B":"Object permanence is a cognitive (Piagetian) milestone, not the trust-related feature described here.","C":"Self-esteem issues develop later and are not the specific infancy-stage deficit described.","D":"Autonomy is the toddler-stage Eriksonian task, not the infancy-stage trust deficit."}}},{"id":"anking-psychiatry-pathology-257","fuente":"anking","tema":18,"anki":"1484623897163","q":"Severe, long-term infant deprivation of affection can lead to which named disorder, in which the infant is emotionally withdrawn and unresponsive to comfort?","o":{"A":"Reactive attachment disorder","B":"Disinhibited social engagement disorder","C":"Autism spectrum disorder","D":"Separation anxiety disorder"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Reactive attachment disorder occurs when a deprived infant becomes emotionally withdrawn and does not seek or respond to comfort, distinguishing it from disinhibited social engagement disorder.","incorrectas":{"B":"Disinhibited social engagement disorder features indiscriminate, overly familiar behavior toward strangers rather than withdrawal.","C":"Autism spectrum disorder is a neurodevelopmental condition not caused by deprivation and has different diagnostic criteria.","D":"Separation anxiety disorder involves excessive distress upon separation from an attachment figure, not global withdrawal."}}},{"id":"anking-psychiatry-pathology-258","fuente":"anking","tema":18,"anki":"1484623900452","q":"Infant deprivation lasting longer than approximately how long can lead to irreversible developmental changes?","o":{"A":"6 months","B":"2 weeks","C":"1 month","D":"2 years"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Deprivation persisting beyond about 6 months is associated with irreversible developmental and psychological changes in the infant.","incorrectas":{"B":"Two weeks is too short a duration to be the classically cited threshold for irreversibility.","C":"One month is shorter than the classically described threshold.","D":"Two years overstates the threshold; irreversible changes are described as occurring before this point."}}},{"id":"anking-psychiatry-pathology-259","fuente":"anking","tema":18,"anki":"1484623902681","q":"What is the most severe possible outcome of severe infant deprivation?","o":{"A":"Death","B":"Chronic constipation","C":"Precocious puberty","D":"Hyperactivity disorder"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"In its most severe form, infant deprivation (lack of nutrition, stimulation, and affection) can be fatal.","incorrectas":{"B":"Chronic constipation is not a recognized severe outcome of emotional/physical deprivation.","C":"Precocious puberty is unrelated to deprivation and has distinct endocrine causes.","D":"Hyperactivity disorder is not the described severe consequence of deprivation."}}},{"id":"anking-psychiatry-pathology-260","fuente":"anking","tema":18,"anki":"1484623905318","q":"Which finding on physical exam, especially rib fractures or spiral long-bone fractures in different stages of healing, is classic evidence of child physical abuse?","o":{"A":"Fractures","B":"Dislocations","C":"Osteoporosis","D":"Growth plate widening"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Fractures of the ribs or long bones (especially spiral fractures) in multiple stages of healing are a classic red flag for non-accidental trauma in children.","incorrectas":{"B":"Dislocations are not the classically cited abuse indicator; fractures in varying stages of healing are.","C":"Osteoporosis is a bone density disorder unrelated to abuse and rare in children.","D":"Growth plate widening is associated with conditions like rickets, not classically with abuse."}}},{"id":"anking-psychiatry-pathology-261","fuente":"anking","tema":18,"anki":"1484623907739","q":"Which skin finding, especially on the trunk, ears, or neck, or in a pattern resembling an implement, suggests child physical abuse?","o":{"A":"Bruises","B":"Petechiae","C":"Vitiligo lesions","D":"Café-au-lait spots"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Bruises in unusual locations (trunk, ears, neck) or in patterns matching an object (belt, cord) are highly suspicious for physical abuse in children.","incorrectas":{"B":"Petechiae typically suggest thrombocytopenia or increased venous pressure, not classically abuse patterning.","C":"Vitiligo is an autoimmune depigmentation disorder unrelated to trauma.","D":"Café-au-lait spots are associated with neurofibromatosis, not abuse."}}},{"id":"anking-psychiatry-pathology-262","fuente":"anking","tema":18,"anki":"1484623909853","q":"Which type of skin injury, such as from a cigarette or located on the buttocks/thighs, is classic evidence of child physical abuse?","o":{"A":"Burns","B":"Lacerations","C":"Abrasions","D":"Ulcers"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Burns, particularly small circular cigarette burns or burns localized to the buttocks/thighs (as from forced immersion), are classic signs of physical abuse.","incorrectas":{"B":"Lacerations are not the specifically cited classic abuse finding in this context.","C":"Abrasions are nonspecific and less classically diagnostic than patterned burns.","D":"Ulcers are typically related to vascular or pressure injury, not classic abuse presentations."}}},{"id":"anking-psychiatry-pathology-263","fuente":"anking","tema":18,"anki":"1484623913909","q":"Children who are victims of physical abuse often display what behavior on exam?","o":{"A":"Avoidance of eye contact","B":"Excessive eye contact","C":"Hyperverbal speech","D":"Increased physical affection toward the examiner"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Abused children often avoid eye contact during examination, reflecting fear, withdrawal, or learned hypervigilance around adults.","incorrectas":{"B":"Excessive eye contact is not the described behavior; avoidance is more typical.","C":"Hyperverbal speech is not a classic described feature of abused children.","D":"Increased affection toward examiners is atypical; withdrawal is more characteristic."}}},{"id":"anking-psychiatry-pathology-264","fuente":"anking","tema":18,"anki":"1484623916085","q":"In cases of child physical abuse, the abuser is usually which individual?","o":{"A":"The biological mother","B":"A stranger","C":"A teacher","D":"An older sibling"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Statistically, the biological mother is most often the perpetrator of physical abuse against children, frequently related to caregiver stress.","incorrectas":{"B":"Strangers are rarely the perpetrators; abuse is usually committed by caregivers or family members.","C":"Teachers are not the typically cited perpetrators of child physical abuse.","D":"Older siblings are not the most commonly cited abusers in this context."}}},{"id":"anking-psychiatry-pathology-265","fuente":"anking","tema":18,"anki":"1484623918068","q":"Approximately 40% of deaths related to child abuse or neglect occur in children younger than what age?","o":{"A":"1 year old","B":"3 years old","C":"5 years old","D":"10 years old"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Infants under 1 year old account for approximately 40% of fatalities from child abuse or neglect, reflecting their extreme vulnerability.","incorrectas":{"B":"Three years old is older than the age group cited for this statistic.","C":"Five years old overstates the age range associated with this mortality statistic.","D":"Ten years old is far older than the vulnerable infant age group described."}}},{"id":"anking-psychiatry-pathology-266","fuente":"anking","tema":18,"anki":"1484623919894","q":"Genital, anal, or oral trauma in a child is evidence of which type of abuse?","o":{"A":"Sexual abuse","B":"Physical abuse","C":"Emotional abuse","D":"Medical neglect"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Genital, anal, or oral trauma is characteristic physical evidence used to identify sexual abuse in children.","incorrectas":{"B":"Physical abuse is more classically evidenced by fractures, bruises, and burns rather than genital/anal trauma.","C":"Emotional abuse does not produce physical trauma findings.","D":"Medical neglect refers to withholding needed care, not causing traumatic injury."}}},{"id":"anking-psychiatry-pathology-267","fuente":"anking","tema":18,"anki":"1484623924102","q":"The perpetrator of child sexual abuse is usually which of the following?","o":{"A":"A male known to the victim","B":"A female stranger","C":"A male stranger","D":"A female known to the victim"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Child sexual abuse is most commonly perpetrated by a male who is known to the victim, such as a family member or acquaintance, rather than a stranger.","incorrectas":{"B":"Female strangers are not the typical perpetrator profile described.","C":"Strangers, whether male or female, are less commonly the perpetrators compared to known individuals.","D":"While known individuals are correct, the perpetrator is usually male, not female."}}},{"id":"anking-psychiatry-pathology-268","fuente":"anking","tema":18,"anki":"1484623925893","q":"What is the peak age incidence for child sexual abuse?","o":{"A":"9-12 years old","B":"0-2 years old","C":"3-5 years old","D":"15-17 years old"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"The peak incidence of child sexual abuse occurs between 9 and 12 years old.","incorrectas":{"B":"Ages 0-2 are not the peak incidence range cited for sexual abuse.","C":"Ages 3-5, while a vulnerable period, are not the cited peak incidence range.","D":"Ages 15-17 are older than the classically cited peak incidence range."}}},{"id":"anking-psychiatry-pathology-269","fuente":"anking","tema":18,"anki":"1484623929161","q":"Failure to provide a child with adequate food, shelter, supervision, education, and/or affection is defined as what?","o":{"A":"Child neglect","B":"Child physical abuse","C":"Child sexual abuse","D":"Munchausen syndrome by proxy"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Child neglect is defined as the failure to provide a child's basic needs, including food, shelter, supervision, education, and affection.","incorrectas":{"B":"Physical abuse involves active infliction of physical harm, not failure to provide basic needs.","C":"Sexual abuse involves sexual exploitation or contact, not failure to provide basic needs.","D":"Munchausen syndrome by proxy involves a caregiver fabricating or inducing illness in a child, not simple neglect of needs."}}},{"id":"anking-epidemiology-biostatistics-270","fuente":"anking","tema":19,"anki":"1485736698405","q":"What is the mathematical relationship expressed by prevalence / (1 - prevalence)?","o":{"A":"Incidence × average duration of disease","B":"Sensitivity × specificity","C":"Relative risk × attributable risk","D":"Positive predictive value / negative predictive value"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Prevalence odds equal incidence multiplied by the average duration of disease; if people with the disease live longer, prevalence rises even without a change in incidence.","incorrectas":{"B":"Sensitivity and specificity describe test performance, not the prevalence-incidence relationship.","C":"Relative risk and attributable risk are measures of association between exposure and outcome, unrelated to this formula.","D":"Predictive values depend on prevalence but their ratio is not equivalent to this formula."}}},{"id":"anking-epidemiology-biostatistics-271","fuente":"anking","tema":19,"anki":"1485736701018","q":"For a short-duration disease such as the common cold, how does prevalence compare to incidence?","o":{"A":"Prevalence is similar to incidence","B":"Prevalence is much greater than incidence","C":"Prevalence is always zero","D":"Prevalence cannot be measured for short-duration disease"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Because the disease resolves quickly, few cases accumulate at any one time, so prevalence approximates incidence.","incorrectas":{"B":"A prevalence much greater than incidence occurs with chronic, long-duration diseases, not short ones.","C":"Prevalence is not zero; cases do exist at any given time, just briefly.","D":"Prevalence can be measured for any disease duration, including short ones."}}},{"id":"anking-epidemiology-biostatistics-272","fuente":"anking","tema":19,"anki":"1485736703297","q":"For a chronic disease such as diabetes, how does prevalence compare to incidence?","o":{"A":"Prevalence is greater than incidence","B":"Prevalence is similar to incidence","C":"Prevalence is less than incidence","D":"Prevalence equals incidence divided by duration"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Chronic diseases accumulate a large pool of existing cases over time, so prevalence exceeds incidence.","incorrectas":{"B":"Similarity between prevalence and incidence occurs with short-duration diseases, not chronic ones.","C":"Prevalence is not less than incidence in chronic disease; the opposite is true.","D":"This is not the correct relationship; prevalence relates to incidence × duration, not incidence divided by duration."}}},{"id":"anking-epidemiology-biostatistics-273","fuente":"anking","tema":19,"anki":"1485736708068","q":"What happens to incidence and prevalence when an effective treatment for a disease is developed (without a cure)?","o":{"A":"No change in incidence; decreased prevalence","B":"Decreased incidence; decreased prevalence","C":"No change in incidence; increased prevalence","D":"Decreased incidence; no change in prevalence"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Treatment does not prevent new cases (no change in incidence) but shortens disease duration or leads to cure, thereby decreasing prevalence.","incorrectas":{"B":"Decreased incidence would require prevention of new cases, which treatment alone does not achieve.","C":"Prevalence decreases, not increases, when effective treatment shortens disease duration.","D":"Incidence is unaffected by treatment, and prevalence does change (decreases), not remains the same."}}},{"id":"anking-epidemiology-biostatistics-274","fuente":"anking","tema":19,"anki":"1485736711892","q":"What happens to incidence and prevalence when an effective vaccine for a disease is developed?","o":{"A":"Decreased incidence and decreased prevalence","B":"No change in incidence; decreased prevalence","C":"Decreased incidence; no change in prevalence","D":"Increased incidence; decreased prevalence"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"A vaccine prevents new infections, lowering incidence, which in turn lowers prevalence as well.","incorrectas":{"B":"Vaccines do reduce incidence by preventing new cases, unlike treatments which do not.","C":"Prevalence also decreases because fewer new cases accumulate over time.","D":"Vaccination decreases, not increases, incidence by preventing disease occurrence."}}},{"id":"anking-epidemiology-biostatistics-275","fuente":"anking","tema":19,"anki":"1485736715527","q":"If people with a chronic disease live longer (without a change in new case detection), what happens to incidence and prevalence?","o":{"A":"No change in incidence; increased prevalence","B":"Decreased incidence; increased prevalence","C":"No change in incidence; decreased prevalence","D":"Increased incidence; no change in prevalence"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Longer survival does not affect the rate of new cases (incidence) but increases the pool of existing cases, raising prevalence.","incorrectas":{"B":"Incidence remains unchanged since longer survival does not affect new case occurrence.","C":"Prevalence increases, not decreases, when patients live longer with disease.","D":"Incidence does not increase from longer survival; only prevalence rises."}}},{"id":"anking-epidemiology-biostatistics-276","fuente":"anking","tema":19,"anki":"1485905125428","q":"Which type of prevalence refers to the amount of an existing condition present in a population at a single point in time?","o":{"A":"Point prevalence","B":"Period prevalence","C":"Cumulative incidence","D":"Attack rate"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Point prevalence measures existing cases of disease at one specific moment in time.","incorrectas":{"B":"Period prevalence measures cases over a defined time interval, not a single point.","C":"Cumulative incidence measures new cases occurring over time, not existing cases at a point.","D":"Attack rate is a form of cumulative incidence used in outbreak settings, not a point-in-time prevalence measure."}}},{"id":"anking-epidemiology-biostatistics-277","fuente":"anking","tema":19,"anki":"1485905310769","q":"Which type of prevalence refers to the amount of an existing condition present in a population during a particular time period?","o":{"A":"Period prevalence","B":"Point prevalence","C":"Incidence rate","D":"Case fatality rate"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Period prevalence captures all existing cases (old and new) during a defined interval, such as a year.","incorrectas":{"B":"Point prevalence refers to a single moment in time, not a duration.","C":"Incidence rate measures new cases over time, not the total existing burden.","D":"Case fatality rate measures the proportion of deaths among diagnosed cases, unrelated to prevalence."}}},{"id":"anking-epidemiology-biostatistics-278","fuente":"anking","tema":19,"anki":"1485905312055","q":"Which study design is an observational study that compares a group of people with a disease to a group without the disease?","o":{"A":"Case-control study","B":"Cohort study","C":"Cross-sectional study","D":"Randomized controlled trial"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Case-control studies select participants based on disease status and look backward to compare prior exposure rates, asking 'what happened?'","incorrectas":{"B":"Cohort studies select participants based on exposure status, not disease status, and follow them forward.","C":"Cross-sectional studies assess exposure and disease simultaneously in one population sample, not by pre-selecting diseased vs non-diseased groups.","D":"A randomized controlled trial is an experimental study, not an observational one, and assigns exposure rather than comparing existing disease status."}}},{"id":"anking-epidemiology-biostatistics-279","fuente":"anking","tema":19,"anki":"1485913601502","q":"Case-control studies are almost always which type of design with respect to time?","o":{"A":"Retrospective","B":"Prospective","C":"Cross-sectional","D":"Ambispective"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Case-control studies look backward in time from disease status to prior exposures, making them retrospective.","incorrectas":{"B":"Prospective design (following subjects forward in time) is characteristic of cohort studies, not case-control studies.","C":"Cross-sectional describes studies assessing exposure and outcome at one time point, a distinct design from case-control.","D":"Ambispective (combining retrospective and prospective elements) is not the typical design for case-control studies."}}},{"id":"anking-epidemiology-biostatistics-280","fuente":"anking","tema":19,"anki":"1485913607195","q":"Are case-control studies useful for directly measuring incidence and/or prevalence?","o":{"A":"Neither incidence nor prevalence","B":"Only incidence","C":"Only prevalence","D":"Both incidence and prevalence"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Because subjects are selected based on disease status rather than sampled from the general population, case-control studies cannot calculate true incidence or prevalence; they primarily estimate odds ratios.","incorrectas":{"B":"Incidence requires following a population over time from exposure, which case-control studies do not do since cases are pre-selected.","C":"Prevalence requires sampling the general population at one time, which is not how case-control studies are structured.","D":"Case-control studies are not designed to measure either incidence or prevalence directly."}}},{"id":"anking-epidemiology-biostatistics-281","fuente":"anking","tema":19,"anki":"1485913618153","q":"Which type of study is an observational study that compares the frequency of disease and frequency of risk-related factors at one time point?","o":{"A":"Cross-sectional survey","B":"Case-control study","C":"Cohort study","D":"Twin concordance study"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Cross-sectional surveys measure exposure and outcome simultaneously in a population, asking 'what is happening?' but cannot establish temporal relationships.","incorrectas":{"B":"Case-control studies select groups based on disease status and look backward for exposure, unlike simultaneous assessment in cross-sectional studies.","C":"Cohort studies select groups based on exposure and follow them forward over time, not at a single time point.","D":"Twin concordance studies compare disease frequency between monozygotic and dizygotic twins, not general population risk factors at one time point."}}},{"id":"anking-epidemiology-biostatistics-282","fuente":"anking","tema":19,"anki":"1485913626221","q":"Are cross-sectional surveys useful for measuring incidence and/or prevalence?","o":{"A":"Prevalence","B":"Incidence","C":"Both incidence and prevalence","D":"Neither incidence nor prevalence"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Because cross-sectional surveys capture a snapshot of the population at one time, they are useful for measuring prevalence, not incidence.","incorrectas":{"B":"Incidence requires tracking new cases over time, which cross-sectional surveys cannot do since they capture only one time point.","C":"Cross-sectional surveys cannot measure incidence, so 'both' is incorrect.","D":"Cross-sectional surveys can measure prevalence, so 'neither' is incorrect."}}},{"id":"anking-epidemiology-biostatistics-283","fuente":"anking","tema":19,"anki":"1485913637098","q":"Which type of study is an observational study that compares groups with a given exposure or risk factor to a group without such exposure?","o":{"A":"Cohort study","B":"Case-control study","C":"Cross-sectional study","D":"Case series"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Cohort studies select participants based on exposure status and follow them to see if the exposure is associated with later development of disease.","incorrectas":{"B":"Case-control studies select groups based on disease status, not exposure status.","C":"Cross-sectional studies assess exposure and outcome simultaneously rather than following exposed vs unexposed groups over time.","D":"A case series simply describes a group of patients with a disease and includes no comparison group."}}},{"id":"anking-epidemiology-biostatistics-284","fuente":"anking","tema":19,"anki":"1485913642965","q":"Which type of observational study can be designed as either retrospective or prospective?","o":{"A":"Cohort study","B":"Case-control study","C":"Cross-sectional study","D":"Ecological study"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Cohort studies can follow subjects forward in time (prospective) or use existing exposure records to look backward (retrospective).","incorrectas":{"B":"Case-control studies are almost always retrospective by design.","C":"Cross-sectional studies assess exposure and outcome at a single time point, so the retro/prospective distinction does not apply in the same way.","D":"Ecological studies analyze population-level, not individual-level, data and are not classified this way."}}},{"id":"anking-epidemiology-biostatistics-285","fuente":"anking","tema":19,"anki":"1485913647193","q":"Which type of study design is useful for calculating relative risk (RR)?","o":{"A":"Cohort study","B":"Case-control study","C":"Cross-sectional study","D":"Case series"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Because cohort studies follow exposed and unexposed groups over time, they allow direct calculation of relative risk, e.g., comparing COPD risk in smokers versus nonsmokers.","incorrectas":{"B":"Case-control studies calculate odds ratios, not relative risk, since incidence cannot be directly determined.","C":"Cross-sectional studies typically report prevalence ratios or odds ratios, not relative risk based on incident cases.","D":"Case series lack a comparison group entirely, so no risk ratio can be calculated."}}},{"id":"anking-epidemiology-biostatistics-286","fuente":"anking","tema":19,"anki":"1485913653603","q":"Are cohort studies useful for measuring incidence and/or prevalence?","o":{"A":"Incidence","B":"Prevalence","C":"Neither incidence nor prevalence","D":"Both incidence and prevalence equally well"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Cohort studies follow exposed and unexposed groups forward over time, allowing calculation of the incidence of new disease cases.","incorrectas":{"B":"Prevalence measurement is better suited to cross-sectional studies, which capture existing cases at one time point.","C":"Cohort studies are useful for incidence, so 'neither' is incorrect.","D":"Cohort studies are primarily suited for incidence, not prevalence, so they are not equally useful for both."}}},{"id":"anking-epidemiology-biostatistics-287","fuente":"anking","tema":19,"anki":"1485913657551","q":"Which type of study is an observational study that compares the frequency with which monozygotic and dizygotic twins develop the same disease?","o":{"A":"Twin concordance study","B":"Cohort study","C":"Case-control study","D":"Adoption study"},"r":"A","e":{"correcta":"Twin concordance studies compare disease concordance rates between monozygotic and dizygotic twins to estimate the genetic contribution to a disease.","incorrectas":{"B":"Cohort studies compare exposed versus unexposed groups in a general population, not twin pairs specifically.","C":"Case-control studies compare diseased versus non-diseased individuals, not twin concordance rates.","D":"Adoption studies compare biological versus adoptive relatives to separate genetic and environmental influences, a different design from twin concordance studies."}}}]};
